export const B2 = [
  {"id":"b2-01","level":"B2","icon":"🗳️","title":"How the country is run","grammar":"The passive with modal verbs, and ways around the passive","summary":"Modal + passive: the modal is conjugated, participle + worden go to the end in either order (moet worden goedgekeurd / goedgekeurd worden). Ways around it: men, zijn te, vallen te, zich laten, krijgen + participle.","explain":"You know the passive from units B1 · 10 and B1 · 11: `Het plan wordt besproken`. In the news and in government letters it is often combined with a modal verb: something **must**, **can** or **should have been** done. Dutch also has several ways around the passive, and a good writer knows when to use which.\n\n### A modal verb with the passive\n\nThe modal verb is the conjugated verb and stands in second place. `worden` becomes an infinitive and goes to the end together with the participle. There is **no te** after a modal verb.\n\n| tense | example |\n| present | Het voorstel moet worden goedgekeurd. |\n| past | Het voorstel moest worden goedgekeurd. |\n| perfect | Het voorstel heeft moeten worden goedgekeurd. |\n| past perfect | Het voorstel had moeten worden goedgekeurd. |\n| zou | Het voorstel zou kunnen worden goedgekeurd. |\n\n> De wet had al lang moeten worden aangepast. — The law should have been changed long ago.\n> Dit probleem zou door de gemeente kunnen worden opgelost. — This problem could be solved by the municipality.\n\nIn the perfect the modal takes **hebben** and is an infinitive, not a participle (unit B1 · 26): `heeft moeten worden`, never *heeft gemoeten worden. `had moeten worden` + participle means **should have been**: it did not happen. `had kunnen worden` means **could have been**.\n\n### The order of the verbs at the end\n\nThe participle may stand **before or after** `worden`: `moet worden goedgekeurd` and `moet goedgekeurd worden` are both standard Dutch, in writing and in speech.\n\nWith three verbs the participle stands either **last** or **first** in the group: `had moeten worden aangepast` or `had aangepast moeten worden`. The middle (`had moeten aangepast worden`) is Belgian, not wrong. In a sub-clause the conjugated modal joins the group at the end, with the same two orders, and nothing else may come between the verbs:\n\n> Ik lees dat de wet moet worden aangepast. — I read that the law must be changed.\n> Ik lees dat de wet aangepast moet worden. — I read that the law must be changed.\n\n### The passive without a subject\n\nFormal texts use `er` + passive: `Er wordt verwacht dat …` (it is expected), `Er is besloten om …` (it has been decided to), `Er moet worden gestemd` (a vote must be held). In newspaper style the participle can even come first, without `er`: `Verwacht wordt dat …`, `Besloten is om …`.\n\n> Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag aftreedt. — It is expected that the minister will resign today.\n\n### Ways around the passive\n\n- **men** (formal): `Men verwacht dat …` = it is expected. In speech `je`, `ze` or `we`: `Ze gaan de belasting verhogen` (they, the authorities).\n- **zijn + te + infinitive** = can be done: `De uitslag is op de website te vinden`, `Dat is niet te doen`. In official letters it can mean *must be*: `Het bedrag is binnen dertig dagen te betalen`. A separable verb takes te in the middle: `in te zien`.\n- **vallen te**, mostly with niets, weinig, veel: `Er valt niets te zeggen`.\n- **zich laten** + infinitive (formal) = can be done: `Dat laat zich makkelijk verklaren`.\n- **krijgen + participle**: the person who receives something is the subject. `Hij kreeg een boete opgelegd`, `Ze kregen het formulier toegestuurd`.\n\n> Aan de uitslag valt niets meer te veranderen. — Nothing more can be changed about the result.\n> De kiezers krijgen hun stempas thuis toegestuurd. — The voters are sent their polling card at home.\n\n### Passive or active?\n\nThe passive suits formal writing and a doer who is unknown, unimportant or obvious. Use the active when the doer is the news (`De gemeenteraad heeft het plan afgewezen`) and in conversation.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Keep worden: `Het plan moet worden goedgekeurd`. Only short sentences can drop it: `Dat moet gezegd`.\n- hebben in the perfect: *De wet is moeten worden aangepast → `De wet heeft moeten worden aangepast`.\n- No te after a modal: *Het moet te worden betaald → `Het moet worden betaald`. Only `hoeven` takes te: `Het hoeft niet te worden betaald` (unit B2 · 14).\n- The receiver is not the subject of worden: *De kiezers worden een brief gestuurd → `De kiezers krijgen een brief gestuurd`, or formally `Aan de kiezers wordt een brief gestuurd`.\n- `moet worden goedgekeurd` is a process still to come; `moet zijn goedgekeurd` means it must be done by a certain moment.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian can say `вирішено` or `кажуть, що …` with no subject at all. Dutch needs something in that slot: `Er is besloten`, `Men zegt dat …`, `Ze zeggen dat …`.","words":[{"nl":"verkiezing","art":"de","pl":"verkiezingen","en":"election","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kabinet","art":"het","pl":"kabinetten","en":"cabinet (the government)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wetsvoorstel","art":"het","pl":"wetsvoorstellen","en":"bill (a proposed law)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"coalitie","art":"de","pl":"coalities","en":"coalition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"oppositie","art":"de","pl":"","en":"opposition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kiezer","art":"de","pl":"kiezers","en":"voter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stempas","art":"de","pl":"stempassen","en":"polling card","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitslag","art":"de","pl":"uitslagen","en":"result (of a vote or test)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"maatregel","art":"de","pl":"maatregelen","en":"measure (a step taken)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"begroting","art":"de","pl":"begrotingen","en":"budget","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gemeenteraad","art":"de","pl":"gemeenteraden","en":"municipal council","pos":"n"},{"nl":"goedkeuren","past":"keurde goed","perf":"heeft goedgekeurd","en":"to approve","pos":"v"},{"nl":"invoeren","past":"voerde in","perf":"heeft ingevoerd","en":"to introduce (a rule or system)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afschaffen","past":"schafte af","perf":"heeft afgeschaft","en":"to abolish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aftreden","past":"trad af","perf":"is afgetreden","en":"to resign (from office)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verwerpen","past":"verwierp","perf":"heeft verworpen","en":"to reject (a proposal or bill)","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Het wetsvoorstel moet door beide Kamers worden goedgekeurd.","en":"The bill has to be approved by both Houses (of parliament).","hint":["wetsvoorstel","moeten","beide","Kamer","worden","goedkeuren"],"note":"Moet is the conjugated verb in second place; worden and the participle goedgekeurd go to the end, in either order.","alt":["Het wetsvoorstel moet door beide Kamers goedgekeurd worden.","Het wetsvoorstel moet worden goedgekeurd door beide Kamers.","Het wetsvoorstel moet goedgekeurd worden door beide Kamers."]},{"nl":"Volgens de oppositie had de begroting veel eerder moeten worden aangepast.","en":"According to the opposition the budget should have been adjusted much earlier.","hint":["volgens","oppositie","begroting","eerder","moeten","aanpassen"],"note":"Should have been = had moeten worden + participle: it did not happen. Moeten is an infinitive here, not a participle, and the participle may also stand first: aangepast moeten worden.","alt":["Volgens de oppositie had de begroting veel eerder aangepast moeten worden.","De begroting had volgens de oppositie veel eerder moeten worden aangepast.","De begroting had volgens de oppositie veel eerder aangepast moeten worden.","Volgens de oppositie had de begroting veel eerder moeten aangepast worden.","De begroting had volgens de oppositie veel eerder moeten aangepast worden."]},{"nl":"De kiezers willen weten of de maatregel kan worden afgeschaft.","en":"The voters want to know whether the measure can be abolished.","hint":["kiezer","willen","weten","maatregel","kunnen","afschaffen"],"note":"In the of-clause the conjugated kan joins the verb group at the end. The participle stands last or first in the group: kan worden afgeschaft or afgeschaft kan worden.","alt":["De kiezers willen weten of de maatregel afgeschaft kan worden.","De kiezers willen weten of de maatregel kan afgeschaft worden."]},{"nl":"Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag aftreedt.","en":"It is expected that the minister will resign today.","hint":["verwachten","minister","vandaag","aftreden"],"note":"A passive without a subject: er fills the first place. Aftreden is not passive itself; in the dat-clause the separable verb stays in one piece at the end: aftreedt.","alt":["Verwacht wordt dat de minister vandaag aftreedt.","Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag zal aftreden.","Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag af zal treden.","Verwacht wordt dat de minister vandaag zal aftreden.","Verwacht wordt dat de minister vandaag af zal treden."]},{"nl":"Er is besloten om de verkiezingen een maand uit te stellen.","en":"It has been decided to postpone the elections by a month.","hint":["besluiten","verkiezing","maand","uitstellen"],"note":"Er is besloten: the impersonal passive in the perfect, typical of official texts. Om may be left out; uit te stellen has te between the prefix and the verb.","alt":["Er is besloten de verkiezingen een maand uit te stellen."]},{"nl":"Men zegt dat het kabinet binnenkort zal vallen.","en":"It is said that the cabinet will fall soon. (with men)","hint":["men","zeggen","kabinet","binnenkort","vallen"],"note":"Men replaces a passive (Er wordt gezegd dat …) and takes a singular verb: men zegt. It is formal; in speech you would say ze zeggen.","alt":["Men zegt dat het kabinet binnenkort valt."]},{"nl":"De uitslag van de verkiezingen is vanaf morgen op de website te vinden.","en":"The result of the elections can be found on the website from tomorrow. (with zijn te)","hint":["uitslag","verkiezing","vanaf","morgen","website","vinden"],"note":"zijn + te + infinitive means 'can be done': is te vinden = can be found. It replaces kan worden gevonden.","alt":["Vanaf morgen is de uitslag van de verkiezingen op de website te vinden."]},{"nl":"Aan de uitslag valt niets meer te veranderen.","en":"Nothing more can be changed about the result. (with vallen te)","hint":["uitslag","vallen","niets","meer","veranderen"],"note":"vallen te + infinitive means 'can be done', mostly with niets, weinig or veel. Valt stays singular, and the infinitive veranderen goes to the end with te.","alt":["Er valt aan de uitslag niets meer te veranderen.","Er valt niets meer aan de uitslag te veranderen."]},{"nl":"De winst van de oppositie laat zich makkelijk verklaren.","en":"The opposition's win can easily be explained. (with zich laten)","hint":["winst","oppositie","zich laten","makkelijk","verklaren"],"note":"zich laten + infinitive is a formal way of saying 'can be done': laat zich verklaren = kan worden verklaard. Zich refers back to the subject, de winst.","alt":["De winst van de oppositie laat zich gemakkelijk verklaren."]},{"nl":"Alle kiezers krijgen hun stempas thuis toegestuurd.","en":"All voters are sent their polling card at home. (with krijgen)","hint":["kiezer","krijgen","stempas","thuis","toesturen"],"note":"krijgen + participle makes the receiver the subject. With worden that is impossible: never *De kiezers worden hun stempas toegestuurd.","alt":["Alle kiezers krijgen thuis hun stempas toegestuurd."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het wetsvoorstel moet eerst door de Tweede Kamer worden ___.","options":["goedkeuren","goedgekeurd","goedgekeurt","te goedkeuren"],"correct":1,"en":"The bill must first be approved by the House of Representatives.","note":"After worden comes the participle: goedgekeurd. It ends in d, because keuren has the stem keur (not in 't kofschip)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Vanwege de storm ___ de verkiezingen moeten worden uitgesteld.","options":["zijn","hebben","werden"],"correct":1,"en":"Because of the storm the elections have had to be postponed.","note":"The perfect of a modal verb is always formed with hebben: hebben moeten. That stays so in the passive: *zijn moeten worden is wrong."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Alle leden van de gemeenteraad ___ het rapport vooraf toegestuurd.","options":["worden","zijn","krijgen"],"correct":2,"en":"All members of the municipal council are sent the report in advance.","note":"The members receive the report, so krijgen + participle. Dutch cannot make the receiver the subject of worden: *De leden worden het rapport toegestuurd."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het plan hoeft niet door de gemeenteraad ___ worden goedgekeurd.","options":["–","te","om te"],"correct":1,"en":"The plan does not need to be approved by the municipal council.","note":"Hoeven is the one modal-like verb that takes te: hoeft niet te worden. After moeten, kunnen, mogen and willen there is no te."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De maatregel zal waarschijnlijk worden ___ (afschaffen).","answers":["afgeschaft"],"en":"The measure will probably be abolished.","note":"zal … worden + participle. Afschaffen: ge comes after the prefix, and schaf ends in f, so the participle ends in t: afgeschaft."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Er ___ (worden) verwacht dat de coalitie zetels verliest.","answers":["wordt"],"en":"It is expected that the coalition will lose seats.","note":"The impersonal passive has er in first place and a singular verb: er wordt. What is expected follows in the dat-clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tijdens de crisis ___ (moeten) veel maatregelen snel worden ingevoerd.","answers":["moesten"],"en":"During the crisis many measures had to be introduced quickly.","note":"The subject is veel maatregelen, plural, and the story is in the past: moesten. Worden and ingevoerd stay at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De uitslag ___ (laten) zich moeilijk voorspellen.","answers":["laat"],"en":"The result is hard to predict.","note":"zich laten + infinitive: can be done. De uitslag is singular: laat, stem without t because the stem already ends in t."},{"type":"order","lead":"De oppositie vindt","words":["dat","de begroting","moet","worden","aangepast"],"answers":["De oppositie vindt dat de begroting moet worden aangepast.","De oppositie vindt dat de begroting aangepast moet worden.","De oppositie vindt dat de begroting moet aangepast worden."],"en":"The opposition thinks that the budget must be adjusted.","note":"In the sub-clause all three verbs stand together at the end. The participle stands last (moet worden aangepast) or first (aangepast moet worden); both are standard."},{"type":"order","lead":"Volgens de krant","words":["had","het wetsvoorstel","veel eerder","moeten","worden","verworpen"],"answers":["Volgens de krant had het wetsvoorstel veel eerder moeten worden verworpen.","Volgens de krant had het wetsvoorstel veel eerder verworpen moeten worden.","Volgens de krant had het wetsvoorstel veel eerder moeten verworpen worden."],"en":"According to the newspaper the bill should have been rejected much earlier.","note":"Volgens de krant is first, so had comes second and the subject follows. Should have been = had moeten worden + participle; the participle goes last or first in the group."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dit probleem","words":["zou","door de gemeenteraad","kunnen","worden","opgelost"],"answers":["Dit probleem zou door de gemeenteraad kunnen worden opgelost.","Dit probleem zou door de gemeenteraad opgelost kunnen worden.","Dit probleem zou kunnen worden opgelost door de gemeenteraad.","Dit probleem zou opgelost kunnen worden door de gemeenteraad.","Dit probleem zou door de gemeenteraad kunnen opgelost worden.","Dit probleem zou kunnen opgelost worden door de gemeenteraad."],"en":"This problem could be solved by the municipal council.","note":"Zou is conjugated and stands second; kunnen, worden and the participle form the group at the end. The door-phrase normally stands before the group, but may also follow it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it passive and leave out the doer. Keep the modal verb.","from":"De regering moet de wet aanpassen.","answers":["De wet moet worden aangepast.","De wet moet aangepast worden."],"en":"The law must be changed.","note":"The object de wet becomes the subject. Moet stays in second place; aanpassen becomes worden + aangepast at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with zijn + te + infinitive.","from":"Een nieuwe stempas kan op het stadhuis worden aangevraagd.","answers":["Een nieuwe stempas is op het stadhuis aan te vragen.","Een nieuwe stempas is aan te vragen op het stadhuis."],"en":"A new polling card can be applied for at the town hall.","note":"kan worden + participle becomes is te + infinitive. Aanvragen is separable, so te goes between the prefix and the verb: aan te vragen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say that it should have happened but did not: use had moeten.","from":"Het plan moet eerder worden besproken.","answers":["Het plan had eerder moeten worden besproken.","Het plan had eerder besproken moeten worden.","Het plan had eerder moeten besproken worden."],"en":"The plan should have been discussed earlier.","note":"Had takes the second place, moeten becomes an infinitive in the group at the end. Not *had gemoeten: with another verb the modal is an infinitive."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De wet is moeten worden aangepast.","answers":["De wet heeft moeten worden aangepast.","De wet heeft aangepast moeten worden.","De wet heeft moeten aangepast worden."],"en":"The law has had to be changed.","note":"The perfect of moeten takes hebben, also when worden follows. The is of an ordinary passive perfect (is aangepast) does not apply here."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De kiezers werden een brief toegestuurd.","answers":["De kiezers kregen een brief toegestuurd.","De kiezers werd een brief toegestuurd.","Aan de kiezers werd een brief toegestuurd.","Er werd de kiezers een brief toegestuurd.","Er werd aan de kiezers een brief toegestuurd."],"en":"The voters were sent a letter.","note":"The receiver cannot be the subject of worden. Make it the subject of krijgen (kregen … toegestuurd), or keep een brief as the subject: then the verb is singular, werd."}]},
  {"id":"b2-02","level":"B2","icon":"🔙","title":"Looking back","grammar":"The unreal past: als ik dat had geweten …","summary":"What did not happen: als + past perfect (Als ik het had geweten), then the verb first: past perfect or zou + participle + hebben/zijn (was ik gekomen / zou ik zijn gekomen). had moeten / kunnen + infinitive.","explain":"Dutch talks about things that did **not** happen, and what would have followed, with the past perfect (*de voltooid verleden tijd*): `Als ik dat had geweten, was ik gekomen`, if I had known that, I would have come. This is the grammar of regret, reproach and missed chances.\n\n### The als-clause: the past perfect\n\n`als` + subject + `had` or `was` + participle. The auxiliary is the same as in the perfect: `hebben` for most verbs, `zijn` for a change of place or state (`emigreren`, `vertrekken`, `beginnen`, `worden`). It is a sub-clause, so the verbs go to the end, in either order: `had geweten` or `geweten had`.\n\n> Als hij niet was geëmigreerd, had hij haar nooit ontmoet. — If he had not emigrated, he would never have met her.\n\n### The main clause: two forms\n\n| form | example |\n| past perfect | was ik gekomen |\n| zou + participle + hebben/zijn | zou ik zijn gekomen |\n\nBoth are standard. The past perfect is shorter and more common in speech; `zou` sounds a little more careful. With `zou` the participle and `hebben`/`zijn` may stand in either order: `zou ik zijn gekomen` or `zou ik gekomen zijn`.\n\nWhen the als-clause comes first, it takes the first place, so the main clause **starts with the verb**. You may put `dan` in between. The als-clause can also come second:\n\n> Als ik dat had geweten, zou ik eerder zijn vertrokken. — If I had known that, I would have left earlier.\n> Ik was eerder vertrokken als ik dat had geweten. — I would have left earlier if I had known that.\n\n### Should have, could have\n\n`had` + modal verb + infinitive is the everyday way to talk about what should or could have happened:\n\n- `had moeten`: should have (reproach, regret)\n- `had kunnen`: could have (a missed chance)\n- `had mogen`: would have been allowed to; also a mild reproach (`Dat had je wel even mogen zeggen`)\n- `had willen`: would have liked to\n\n> Je had moeten bellen. — You should have called.\n> Ik had kunnen winnen. — I could have won.\n> Dat had ik niet moeten doen. — I should not have done that.\n\nThe modal stays an **infinitive** when another verb follows (unit B1 · 26): never *had gemoeten bellen. Only on its own does it become a participle: `Dat had ik niet gewild`.\n\n### Without als: the verb first\n\nPut `had` or `was` at the start and add `maar`, and you have a wish about the past: *if only*. Without `maar`, the same order replaces an als-clause, usually followed by `dan`; this is a little more formal.\n\n> Had ik dat maar geweten! — If only I had known that!\n> Was hij maar eerder gekomen! — If only he had come earlier!\n> Had ik die kans gegrepen, dan was alles anders gelopen. — Had I taken that chance, everything would have turned out differently.\n\n### bijna and anders\n\n`bijna` with the past perfect: something almost happened, but did not: `Ik was bijna gevallen`. The perfect is used just as much, with the same meaning: `Ik ben bijna gevallen`. `anders` (otherwise) is followed by the verb: `Ik had het druk, anders was ik gekomen`.\n\n### A past condition with a result now\n\nWhen the result is about **now**, the main clause has the unreal present (unit B1 · 12): the simple past or `zou` + infinitive. Here `had` is simply the past of `hebben`:\n\n> Als ik toen had gespaard, had ik nu een huis. — If I had saved back then, I would have a house now.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Verb first after the als-clause: *Als ik dat had geweten, ik was gekomen → `…, was ik gekomen`.\n- `zijn` with verbs of change: *Als hij niet had geëmigreerd → `Als hij niet was geëmigreerd`.\n- Infinitive, not participle: *Ik had het gemoeten zeggen → `Ik had het moeten zeggen`.\n- `zou` needs its `hebben` or `zijn`: *zou ik gekomen → `zou ik gekomen zijn`.\n- `zou` in the als-clause as well (`Als ik het zou hebben geweten, zou ik …`) is not wrong and is common in speech, but in writing put the past perfect after als: `Als ik het had geweten, zou ik …`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian uses `б`/`би` + past tense for now and for then alike: `Якби я знав, я б прийшов`. Dutch keeps them apart: `Als ik het wist, zou ik komen` (now) against `Als ik het had geweten, was ik gekomen` (then).","words":[{"nl":"spijt","art":"de","pl":"","en":"regret","pos":"n","tag":"spijt hebben van"},{"nl":"keerpunt","art":"het","pl":"keerpunten","en":"turning point","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tweesprong","art":"de","pl":"tweesprongen","en":"crossroads (a choice between two ways)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afweging","art":"de","pl":"afwegingen","en":"weighing up / consideration","pos":"n"},{"nl":"omscholing","art":"de","pl":"omscholingen","en":"retraining (for a new profession)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"dilemma","art":"het","pl":"dilemma's","en":"dilemma","pos":"n"},{"nl":"loopbaan","art":"de","pl":"loopbanen","en":"career","pos":"n"},{"nl":"levenskeuze","art":"de","pl":"levenskeuzes","en":"life choice","pos":"n"},{"nl":"betreuren","past":"betreurde","perf":"heeft betreurd","en":"to regret (formal)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overwegen","past":"overwoog","perf":"heeft overwogen","en":"to consider","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aarzelen","past":"aarzelde","perf":"heeft geaarzeld","en":"to hesitate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afslaan","past":"sloeg af","perf":"heeft afgeslagen","en":"to turn down (an offer)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"stopzetten","past":"zette stop","perf":"heeft stopgezet","en":"to discontinue / to stop (for good)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"emigreren","past":"emigreerde","perf":"is geëmigreerd","en":"to emigrate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"destijds","en":"at the time (back then)","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"ingrijpend","en":"drastic / far-reaching","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Als ik dat destijds had geweten, was ik nooit geëmigreerd.","en":"If I had known that at the time, I would never have emigrated.","hint":["als","destijds","weten","nooit","emigreren"],"note":"Als-clause: past perfect, had geweten. Main clause: past perfect again, and with zijn, because emigreren is a change of place: was geëmigreerd. The main clause starts with the verb.","alt":["Als ik dat destijds had geweten, dan was ik nooit geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds had geweten, zou ik nooit zijn geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds had geweten, dan zou ik nooit zijn geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds had geweten, zou ik nooit geëmigreerd zijn.","Als ik dat destijds had geweten, dan zou ik nooit geëmigreerd zijn.","Als ik dat destijds geweten had, was ik nooit geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds geweten had, dan was ik nooit geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds geweten had, zou ik nooit zijn geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds geweten had, dan zou ik nooit zijn geëmigreerd.","Als ik dat destijds geweten had, zou ik nooit geëmigreerd zijn.","Als ik dat destijds geweten had, dan zou ik nooit geëmigreerd zijn.","Had ik dat destijds geweten, dan was ik nooit geëmigreerd.","Had ik dat destijds geweten, dan zou ik nooit zijn geëmigreerd.","Had ik dat destijds geweten, dan zou ik nooit geëmigreerd zijn."]},{"nl":"Als ze dat aanbod niet had afgeslagen, zou ze nu in het buitenland wonen.","en":"If she had not turned down that offer, she would be living abroad now.","hint":["als","aanbod","afslaan","nu","buitenland","wonen"],"note":"A past condition (had afgeslagen) with a result now: zou + infinitive, or the simple past woonde. Niet stands before the verbs at the end.","alt":["Als ze dat aanbod niet had afgeslagen, dan zou ze nu in het buitenland wonen.","Als ze dat aanbod niet had afgeslagen, woonde ze nu in het buitenland.","Als ze dat aanbod niet had afgeslagen, dan woonde ze nu in het buitenland.","Als ze dat aanbod niet afgeslagen had, zou ze nu in het buitenland wonen.","Als ze dat aanbod niet afgeslagen had, dan zou ze nu in het buitenland wonen.","Als ze dat aanbod niet afgeslagen had, woonde ze nu in het buitenland.","Als ze dat aanbod niet afgeslagen had, dan woonde ze nu in het buitenland."]},{"nl":"Je had me moeten vertellen dat je twijfelde.","en":"You should have told me that you were having doubts.","hint":["moeten","vertellen","twijfelen"],"note":"Should have = had moeten + infinitive. Moeten is an infinitive here, not *gemoeten, because vertellen follows it.","alt":["Je had mij moeten vertellen dat je twijfelde.","Jij had me moeten vertellen dat je twijfelde.","Jij had mij moeten vertellen dat je twijfelde.","Je had me moeten vertellen dat jij twijfelde."]},{"nl":"Achteraf had ik die baan nooit moeten opgeven.","en":"In hindsight I should never have given up that job.","hint":["achteraf","baan","nooit","moeten","opgeven"],"note":"had moeten + infinitive for regret. The separable verb may stay in one piece at the end (moeten opgeven) or its prefix may stand before the group (op moeten geven).","alt":["Achteraf had ik die baan nooit op moeten geven.","Achteraf gezien had ik die baan nooit moeten opgeven.","Achteraf gezien had ik die baan nooit op moeten geven.","Ik had die baan achteraf nooit moeten opgeven.","Ik had die baan achteraf nooit op moeten geven."]},{"nl":"Ik had die kans kunnen grijpen, maar ik aarzelde te lang.","en":"I could have seized that chance, but I hesitated too long.","hint":["kans","kunnen","grijpen","aarzelen","lang"],"note":"Could have = had kunnen + infinitive: a missed chance. The second half tells what really happened, so it is an ordinary past tense.","alt":["Ik had die kans kunnen grijpen, maar ik heb te lang geaarzeld.","Die kans had ik kunnen grijpen, maar ik aarzelde te lang.","Die kans had ik kunnen grijpen, maar ik heb te lang geaarzeld."]},{"nl":"Was ik maar nooit aan die opleiding begonnen!","en":"If only I had never started that course!","hint":["zijn","maar","nooit","opleiding","beginnen"],"note":"A wish about the past: the auxiliary first, then maar. Beginnen takes zijn, so it is Was ik, not *Had ik.","alt":["Als ik maar nooit aan die opleiding was begonnen!","Als ik maar nooit aan die opleiding begonnen was!","Was ik maar nooit met die opleiding begonnen!","Als ik maar nooit met die opleiding was begonnen!","Als ik maar nooit met die opleiding begonnen was!"]},{"nl":"Na twee jaar twijfel had ik mijn omscholing bijna stopgezet.","en":"After two years of doubt I had almost stopped my retraining.","hint":["twee","jaar","twijfel","omscholing","bijna","stopzetten"],"note":"bijna + past perfect: it almost happened, but it did not. The perfect (heb ik … bijna stopgezet) says the same and is just as common.","alt":["Na twee jaar twijfel heb ik mijn omscholing bijna stopgezet.","Ik had mijn omscholing na twee jaar twijfel bijna stopgezet.","Ik heb mijn omscholing na twee jaar twijfel bijna stopgezet."]},{"nl":"Gelukkig aarzelde ik destijds niet, anders had ik deze baan nooit gekregen.","en":"Luckily I did not hesitate at the time, otherwise I would never have got this job.","hint":["gelukkig","aarzelen","destijds","anders","baan","krijgen"],"note":"anders introduces what would have happened, and the verb follows it directly: anders had ik. The first half is what really happened.","alt":["Gelukkig aarzelde ik destijds niet, anders zou ik deze baan nooit hebben gekregen.","Gelukkig aarzelde ik destijds niet, anders zou ik deze baan nooit gekregen hebben.","Gelukkig heb ik destijds niet geaarzeld, anders had ik deze baan nooit gekregen.","Gelukkig heb ik destijds niet geaarzeld, anders zou ik deze baan nooit hebben gekregen.","Gelukkig heb ik destijds niet geaarzeld, anders zou ik deze baan nooit gekregen hebben."]},{"nl":"Als ik toen had gespaard, had ik nu een eigen huis.","en":"If I had saved back then, I would have my own house now.","hint":["als","toen","sparen","nu","eigen","huis"],"note":"The condition is in the past (had gespaard), the result is now. In the main clause had is the simple past of hebben: I would have, not I would have had.","alt":["Als ik toen had gespaard, dan had ik nu een eigen huis.","Als ik toen had gespaard, zou ik nu een eigen huis hebben.","Als ik toen had gespaard, dan zou ik nu een eigen huis hebben.","Als ik toen gespaard had, had ik nu een eigen huis.","Als ik toen gespaard had, dan had ik nu een eigen huis.","Als ik toen gespaard had, zou ik nu een eigen huis hebben.","Als ik toen gespaard had, dan zou ik nu een eigen huis hebben."]},{"nl":"Zonder die ingrijpende beslissing zou mijn leven er heel anders hebben uitgezien.","en":"Without that drastic decision my life would have looked very different.","hint":["zonder","ingrijpend","beslissing","leven","anders","uitzien"],"note":"The condition is in zonder …, so there is no als-clause. zou + hebben + participle; the past perfect is just as good: had mijn leven er heel anders uitgezien.","alt":["Zonder die ingrijpende beslissing zou mijn leven er heel anders uitgezien hebben.","Zonder die ingrijpende beslissing zou mijn leven er heel anders uit hebben gezien.","Zonder die ingrijpende beslissing had mijn leven er heel anders uitgezien.","Mijn leven zou er zonder die ingrijpende beslissing heel anders hebben uitgezien.","Mijn leven had er zonder die ingrijpende beslissing heel anders uitgezien.","Mijn leven zou er zonder die ingrijpende beslissing heel anders uitgezien hebben.","Mijn leven zou er zonder die ingrijpende beslissing heel anders uit hebben gezien."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Als ik van je dilemma had geweten, ___ ik je geholpen.","options":["heb","had","zou","was"],"correct":1,"en":"If I had known about your dilemma, I would have helped you.","note":"The main clause takes the past perfect: had geholpen (helpen takes hebben). Zou would need hebben at the end (zou ik je hebben geholpen), and heb is a plain perfect."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Als hij niet ___ geëmigreerd, had hij zijn vrouw nooit ontmoet.","options":["had","was","zou"],"correct":1,"en":"If he had not emigrated, he would never have met his wife.","note":"Emigreren is a change of place, so its perfect takes zijn: is geëmigreerd, and the past perfect was geëmigreerd. Not *had geëmigreerd."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Je had de gevolgen beter ___ overwegen.","options":["gemoeten","moeten","moest","te moeten"],"correct":1,"en":"You should have considered the consequences more carefully.","note":"had + modal + infinitive: had moeten overwegen. With another verb after it the modal is an infinitive, never *gemoeten."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik stond toen op een tweesprong. Als ik anders ___ gekozen, was ik nu arts.","options":["zou","was","heb","had"],"correct":3,"en":"I was at a crossroads then. If I had chosen differently, I would be a doctor now.","note":"The als-clause is about the past: had gekozen (kiezen takes hebben). The result is about now, so the main clause has the simple past was ik nu arts."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Als we nog langer ___ (aarzelen), was de kans voorbij geweest.","answers":["hadden geaarzeld","geaarzeld hadden"],"en":"If we had hesitated any longer, the chance would have been gone.","note":"Past perfect in the als-clause, with hebben: hadden geaarzeld. In a sub-clause the two verbs may stand in either order."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ach, ___ (zijn) we maar nooit naar die stad verhuisd!","answers":["waren"],"en":"Oh, if only we had never moved to that city!","note":"A wish about the past starts with the auxiliary, followed by maar. Verhuizen takes zijn (we zijn verhuisd), and we is plural: waren."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ___ (zijn) bijna te laat gekomen voor het gesprek dat een keerpunt in mijn loopbaan werd.","answers":["was","ben"],"en":"I almost arrived late for the interview that became a turning point in my career.","note":"bijna + past perfect (was … gekomen) says it almost happened but did not. The perfect, ben … gekomen, is used with the same meaning. Komen takes zijn."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Als je toen die levenskeuze had gemaakt, ___ (hebben) je er nu spijt van.","answers":["had"],"en":"If you had made that life choice back then, you would regret it now.","note":"The condition is past, the result is now: the main clause has the simple past of hebben, had je er nu spijt van. Not *had gehad, because the regret would be now."},{"type":"order","lead":"Als ik meer tijd had gehad,","words":["had","ik","die afweging","zorgvuldiger","gemaakt"],"answers":["Als ik meer tijd had gehad, had ik die afweging zorgvuldiger gemaakt."],"en":"If I had had more time, I would have weighed it up more carefully.","note":"The als-clause fills the first place, so the main clause starts with the verb had and then the subject. The participle gemaakt closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Je","words":["had","me","dat dilemma","moeten","voorleggen"],"answers":["Je had me dat dilemma moeten voorleggen."],"en":"You should have put that dilemma to me.","note":"had in second place, the two infinitives moeten voorleggen at the end. The short pronoun me comes before the object dat dilemma."},{"type":"order","lead":"Had ik die kans gegrepen,","words":["dan","was","mijn leven","heel anders","gelopen"],"answers":["Had ik die kans gegrepen, dan was mijn leven heel anders gelopen."],"en":"Had I taken that chance, my life would have turned out very differently.","note":"The inverted clause replaces an als-clause. Dan takes the first place of the main clause, so the verb was follows it directly. Lopen takes zijn here: is gelopen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join them into one sentence that starts with Als: what would have happened if I had known?","from":"Ik wist het niet, dus ik ben niet gekomen.","answers":["Als ik het had geweten, was ik gekomen.","Als ik het had geweten, dan was ik gekomen.","Als ik het had geweten, zou ik zijn gekomen.","Als ik het had geweten, dan zou ik zijn gekomen.","Als ik het had geweten, zou ik gekomen zijn.","Als ik het had geweten, dan zou ik gekomen zijn.","Als ik het geweten had, was ik gekomen.","Als ik het geweten had, dan was ik gekomen.","Als ik het geweten had, zou ik zijn gekomen.","Als ik het geweten had, dan zou ik zijn gekomen.","Als ik het geweten had, zou ik gekomen zijn.","Als ik het geweten had, dan zou ik gekomen zijn."],"en":"If I had known, I would have come.","note":"What did not happen goes into the past perfect: als ik het had geweten. The result is past perfect or zou + participle + zijn (komen takes zijn), with the verb first. Niet disappears: both halves are now the opposite of what happened."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it as a wish: start with the verb and use maar.","from":"Ik betreur het dat ik niet ben gegaan.","answers":["Was ik maar gegaan!"],"en":"If only I had gone!","note":"A wish about the past: the auxiliary of the perfect first (ben becomes was), then the subject, maar, and the participle at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Write it in good formal Dutch: no zou in the als-clause.","from":"Als mijn werkgever mij zou hebben gesteund, zou ik met de omscholing zijn begonnen.","answers":["Als mijn werkgever mij had gesteund, zou ik met de omscholing zijn begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever mij had gesteund, zou ik met de omscholing begonnen zijn.","Als mijn werkgever mij had gesteund, was ik met de omscholing begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever mij gesteund had, zou ik met de omscholing zijn begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever mij gesteund had, zou ik met de omscholing begonnen zijn.","Als mijn werkgever mij gesteund had, was ik met de omscholing begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever me had gesteund, zou ik met de omscholing zijn begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever me had gesteund, zou ik met de omscholing begonnen zijn.","Als mijn werkgever me had gesteund, was ik met de omscholing begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever me gesteund had, zou ik met de omscholing zijn begonnen.","Als mijn werkgever me gesteund had, zou ik met de omscholing begonnen zijn.","Als mijn werkgever me gesteund had, was ik met de omscholing begonnen."],"en":"If my employer had supported me, I would have started the retraining.","note":"zou twice is heard in speech, but in writing the als-clause takes the past perfect: had gesteund. The main clause may keep zou or use the past perfect, was begonnen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik had dat nooit gemoeten zeggen.","answers":["Ik had dat nooit moeten zeggen.","Dat had ik nooit moeten zeggen."],"en":"I should never have said that.","note":"When another infinitive follows, the modal does not become a participle: had moeten zeggen, two infinitives at the end."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Als hij het aanbod had aangenomen, had hij naar Canada geëmigreerd.","answers":["Als hij het aanbod had aangenomen, was hij naar Canada geëmigreerd.","Als hij het aanbod had aangenomen, zou hij naar Canada zijn geëmigreerd.","Als hij het aanbod had aangenomen, zou hij naar Canada geëmigreerd zijn."],"en":"If he had accepted the offer, he would have emigrated to Canada.","note":"Emigreren takes zijn, also in the unreal past: was geëmigreerd, or zou … zijn geëmigreerd. Aannemen takes hebben, so the als-clause is right."}]},
  {"id":"b2-03","level":"B2","icon":"📊","title":"At the meeting","grammar":"Three verbs at the end: order in the verb group","summary":"In the verb group the conjugated verb comes first and the main verb last (dat hij het zou kunnen doen). A participle may also go first (besproken zal worden); a prefix may stand before the group. Nothing goes inside.","explain":"In a Dutch sub-clause all the verbs gather at the end, and in a main clause everything except the conjugated verb does. With one or two verbs that is easy. At B2 you meet three or four together (`… dat het had moeten worden besproken`), and then the order inside that group matters. Dutch grammars call the two orders of a participle and its auxiliary *de rode en de groene volgorde*.\n\n### Two verbs\n\nA **participle** and its auxiliary may stand in either order. Both are equally correct and you will hear and read both:\n\n> Ik weet dat hij het verslag heeft geschreven. — I know that he has written the report.\n> Ik weet dat hij het verslag geschreven heeft. — I know that he has written the report.\n\nAn **infinitive** usually comes after the conjugated verb: `dat hij moet werken`. The other order, `dat hij werken moet`, is also correct, but less common and rather formal; you will meet it in writing.\n\n### Three verbs or more\n\nThe conjugated verb comes **first**. The others follow in the order in which they depend on each other: each verb governs the next one, and the main verb comes **last**.\n\n| sub-clause | the group |\n| dat hij het zou kunnen doen | zou + kunnen + doen |\n| dat ze had moeten komen | had + moeten + komen |\n| dat het zal worden besproken | zal + worden + besproken |\n| dat het had moeten worden besproken | had + moeten + worden + besproken |\n\n> Ik denk dat we de fusie zouden kunnen afronden. — I think that we could complete the merger.\n\nA **participle** has one extra possibility: it may also stand **first** in the group, before the conjugated verb: `dat het besproken zal worden`, `dat hij het gedaan zou hebben`, `dat het besproken had moeten worden`. The middle (`dat het zal besproken worden`) is mainly Belgian: not wrong, but write first or last. Infinitives never move to the front: *dat ze komen had moeten.\n\n> De voorzitter zegt dat het voorstel besproken zal worden. — The chair says that the proposal will be discussed.\n\n### Two infinitives in the perfect\n\nYou know this from unit B1 · 26: a modal verb, or `gaan`, `blijven`, `laten`, in the perfect is an infinitive, not a participle. The auxiliary comes first and the order is fixed. The auxiliary is the one of the first infinitive: `hebben` with the modals and `laten`, `zijn` with `gaan` and `blijven`. You will also hear `ik ben niet kunnen komen`, mainly in Belgium; `heb` is the usual form.\n\n> Het spijt me dat u zo lang heeft moeten wachten. — I am sorry that you have had to wait so long.\n> Ik begrijp niet waarom ze is blijven zitten. — I do not understand why she stayed seated.\n\n### Separable verbs\n\nThe prefix stays with its verb, or it stands **before the whole group**: `dat hij me zou opbellen` or `dat hij me op zou bellen`; `dat ze het had moeten toelichten` or `dat ze het toe had moeten lichten`. Inside the group (`dat ze het had toe moeten lichten`) the prefix is rare and of doubtful standing; avoid it.\n\n### te in the group\n\nWith `schijnen`, `lijken` and `blijken` the group contains `te`: `dat hij schijnt te hebben gewonnen` or `gewonnen te hebben`. More about these verbs in unit B2 · 14.\n\n### Nothing goes inside the group\n\nObjects, `niet` and adverbs stand **before** the group: `dat we het plan niet hadden kunnen uitvoeren`. A phrase with a preposition may also follow the group (`dat ze hebben ingestemd met het plan`), but nothing stands between the verbs.\n\n### The main clause\n\nThe conjugated verb takes the second place; the rest of the group goes to the end in the same order, with the same freedom for a participle and a prefix:\n\n> De directeur had de cijfers beter moeten toelichten. — The director should have explained the figures better.\n\nSo: `Het zal worden besproken` or `Het zal besproken worden`; `Ik heb moeten wachten`; `Hij had me moeten opbellen` or `Hij had me op moeten bellen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Nothing between the verbs: *dat we zouden het moeten doen → `dat we het zouden moeten doen`.\n- No participle of the modal: *dat ze heeft gemoeten wachten → `dat ze heeft moeten wachten`.\n- With two infinitives the auxiliary comes first: *dat ik moeten wachten heb → `dat ik heb moeten wachten`.\n- Best not in the middle (mainly Belgian): write `dat het zal worden besproken` or `dat het besproken zal worden`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian keeps the verbs close to the subject: `що ми могли б це зробити`. Dutch saves them all for the end of the clause: `dat we het zouden kunnen doen`.","words":[{"nl":"agendapunt","art":"het","pl":"agendapunten","en":"item on the agenda","pos":"n"},{"nl":"notulen","art":"de","pl":"","en":"minutes (of a meeting)","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"voorzitter","art":"de","pl":"voorzitters","en":"chair (of a meeting) / chairperson","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aandeelhouder","art":"de","pl":"aandeelhouders","en":"shareholder","pos":"n"},{"nl":"omzet","art":"de","pl":"","en":"turnover (sales)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"investering","art":"de","pl":"investeringen","en":"investment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"fusie","art":"de","pl":"fusies","en":"merger","pos":"n"},{"nl":"overname","art":"de","pl":"overnames","en":"takeover","pos":"n"},{"nl":"draagvlak","art":"het","pl":"","en":"(broad) support / backing","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toelichten","past":"lichtte toe","perf":"heeft toegelicht","en":"to explain / to clarify","pos":"v"},{"nl":"beklinken","past":"beklonk","perf":"heeft beklonken","en":"to seal (a deal)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"instemmen met","past":"stemde in","perf":"heeft ingestemd","en":"to agree to / to consent to","pos":"v"},{"nl":"bijsturen","past":"stuurde bij","perf":"heeft bijgestuurd","en":"to adjust (the course of something)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"doorvoeren","past":"voerde door","perf":"heeft doorgevoerd","en":"to implement (a change)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"haalbaar","en":"feasible","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"unaniem","en":"unanimous / unanimously","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De voorzitter zei dat het agendapunt volgende week zal worden besproken.","en":"The chair said that the item on the agenda will be discussed next week.","hint":["voorzitter","zeggen","agendapunt","volgende week","bespreken"],"note":"Three verbs: zal first, then worden, then the participle. The participle may also stand first in the group: besproken zal worden.","alt":["De voorzitter zei dat het agendapunt volgende week besproken zal worden.","De voorzitter zei dat het agendapunt volgende week zal besproken worden."]},{"nl":"Ik denk dat we de fusie voor de zomer zouden kunnen beklinken.","en":"I think that we could seal the merger before the summer.","hint":["denken","fusie","zomer","kunnen","beklinken"],"note":"zouden + kunnen + beklinken: the conjugated verb first, the main verb last. Two infinitives, so there is no other order."},{"nl":"Het spijt me dat u zo lang heeft moeten wachten.","en":"I am sorry that you (formal) have had to wait so long.","hint":["spijten","lang","moeten","wachten"],"note":"The perfect of moeten with another verb: heeft moeten wachten, two infinitives. Here the order is fixed: the auxiliary first.","alt":["Het spijt me dat u zo lang hebt moeten wachten."]},{"nl":"Het is jammer dat de aandeelhouders niet met de overname hebben ingestemd.","en":"It is a pity that the shareholders have not agreed to the takeover.","hint":["jammer","aandeelhouder","niet","overname","instemmen met"],"note":"Two verbs with a participle: hebben ingestemd or ingestemd hebben. The phrase met de overname stands before the group, or after it, never inside.","alt":["Het is jammer dat de aandeelhouders niet met de overname ingestemd hebben.","Het is jammer dat de aandeelhouders niet hebben ingestemd met de overname.","Het is jammer dat de aandeelhouders niet ingestemd hebben met de overname."]},{"nl":"Volgens de notulen had het voorstel al in maart moeten worden goedgekeurd.","en":"According to the minutes the proposal should already have been approved in March.","hint":["volgens","notulen","voorstel","maart","moeten","goedkeuren"],"note":"Main clause: had takes the second place, the rest of the group goes to the end: moeten worden goedgekeurd, or with the participle first, goedgekeurd moeten worden.","alt":["Volgens de notulen had het voorstel al in maart goedgekeurd moeten worden.","Het voorstel had volgens de notulen al in maart moeten worden goedgekeurd.","Het voorstel had volgens de notulen al in maart goedgekeurd moeten worden.","Volgens de notulen had het voorstel al in maart moeten goedgekeurd worden.","Het voorstel had volgens de notulen al in maart moeten goedgekeurd worden."]},{"nl":"De directeur had de cijfers beter moeten toelichten.","en":"The director should have explained the figures better.","hint":["directeur","cijfer","beter","moeten","toelichten"],"note":"In a main clause the group at the end is moeten toelichten. The prefix may stay with its verb or stand before the group: toe moeten lichten.","alt":["De directeur had de cijfers beter toe moeten lichten."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf schijnt vorig jaar minder omzet te hebben gemaakt.","en":"The company seems to have made less turnover last year.","hint":["bedrijf","schijnen","vorig jaar","omzet","maken"],"note":"schijnen + te: te hebben gemaakt at the end. With a participle both orders are possible: gemaakt te hebben.","alt":["Het bedrijf schijnt vorig jaar minder omzet gemaakt te hebben."]},{"nl":"Ik begrijp niet waarom de directie de fusie heeft laten doorgaan.","en":"I do not understand why the management has let the merger go ahead.","hint":["begrijpen","directie","fusie","laten","doorgaan"],"note":"The perfect of laten with another verb: heeft laten doorgaan, two infinitives after the auxiliary. The prefix may also stand before the group: door heeft laten gaan.","alt":["Ik begrijp niet waarom de directie de fusie door heeft laten gaan.","Ik begrijp niet waarom de directie de fusie heeft door laten gaan."]},{"nl":"De voorzitter vreest dat de reorganisatie zonder draagvlak niet zal kunnen worden doorgevoerd.","en":"The chair fears that without support the reorganisation will not be able to be carried out.","hint":["voorzitter","vrezen","reorganisatie","draagvlak","kunnen","doorvoeren"],"note":"Four verbs: zal + kunnen + worden + doorgevoerd, each governing the next. Only the participle may move; in careful Netherlands Dutch it goes to the front of the group: doorgevoerd zal kunnen worden.","alt":["De voorzitter vreest dat de reorganisatie zonder draagvlak niet doorgevoerd zal kunnen worden.","De voorzitter vreest dat zonder draagvlak de reorganisatie niet zal kunnen worden doorgevoerd.","De voorzitter vreest dat zonder draagvlak de reorganisatie niet doorgevoerd zal kunnen worden.","De voorzitter vreest dat de reorganisatie zonder draagvlak niet zal kunnen doorgevoerd worden.","De voorzitter vreest dat de reorganisatie zonder draagvlak niet zal doorgevoerd kunnen worden.","De voorzitter vreest dat zonder draagvlak de reorganisatie niet zal kunnen doorgevoerd worden.","De voorzitter vreest dat zonder draagvlak de reorganisatie niet zal doorgevoerd kunnen worden."]},{"nl":"Het bestuur heeft unaniem besloten dat het project moet worden bijgestuurd.","en":"The board has decided unanimously that the project must be adjusted.","hint":["bestuur","unaniem","besluiten","project","moeten","bijsturen"],"note":"In the dat-clause: moet worden bijgestuurd or bijgestuurd moet worden. The main clause has heeft second and besloten at the end.","alt":["Het bestuur heeft unaniem besloten dat het project bijgestuurd moet worden.","Het bestuur heeft unaniem besloten dat het project moet bijgestuurd worden."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik hoop dat de voorzitter het agendapunt ___.","options":["kan toelichten","kan toe te lichten","kan lichten toe"],"correct":0,"en":"I hope that the chair can explain the item on the agenda.","note":"The usual order is the conjugated verb first: kan toelichten (toe kan lichten and the formal toelichten kan are also correct). A modal takes no te, and the prefix never comes after its verb."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik vind dat dit agendapunt eerder ___.","options":["had gemoeten worden besproken","had worden moeten besproken","had moeten worden besproken"],"correct":2,"en":"I think that this item on the agenda should have been discussed earlier.","note":"had + moeten + worden + besproken, in the order of dependence: moeten governs worden, so it comes before it. The participle may also stand first (besproken had moeten worden). Never *gemoeten before another verb."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het spijt me dat ik je gisteren niet met de notulen ___ kunnen helpen.","options":["ben","heb","kon"],"correct":1,"en":"I am sorry that I was not able to help you with the minutes yesterday.","note":"With two infinitives the auxiliary belongs to the first one, kunnen, and kunnen takes hebben: heb kunnen helpen. Kon cannot go with the infinitive kunnen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij beweert dat hij de fusie al lang had ___ (willen) tegenhouden.","answers":["willen"],"en":"He claims that he had wanted to stop the merger for a long time.","note":"A modal in the perfect with another verb stays an infinitive: had willen tegenhouden, not *had gewild tegenhouden."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik snap niet waarom de aandeelhouders zo lang zijn ___ (blijven) twijfelen.","answers":["blijven"],"en":"I do not see why the shareholders have kept on hesitating for so long.","note":"blijven + infinitive in the perfect: zijn blijven twijfelen. Blijven takes zijn, and it is an infinitive, not *gebleven."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het bedrijf lijkt de deadline niet ___ (halen) te hebben.","answers":["gehaald"],"en":"The company seems not to have met the deadline.","note":"lijken + te + hebben + participle. The participle stands first in the group here: gehaald te hebben; te hebben gehaald is just as good."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik denk dat","words":["de investering","haalbaar","zou","kunnen","zijn"],"answers":["Ik denk dat de investering haalbaar zou kunnen zijn."],"en":"I think that the investment could be feasible.","note":"zou + kunnen + zijn form the group at the end. Haalbaar belongs before the group; it cannot stand between the verbs."},{"type":"order","lead":"De voorzitter zegt dat het agendapunt","words":["morgen","zal","worden","besproken"],"answers":["De voorzitter zegt dat het agendapunt morgen zal worden besproken.","De voorzitter zegt dat het agendapunt morgen besproken zal worden.","De voorzitter zegt dat het agendapunt morgen zal besproken worden."],"en":"The chair says that the item on the agenda will be discussed tomorrow.","note":"Morgen stands before the group. Inside it, zal comes before worden; the participle stands last or first."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het spijt me dat","words":["ik","de notulen","niet","heb","kunnen","lezen"],"answers":["Het spijt me dat ik de notulen niet heb kunnen lezen."],"en":"I am sorry that I have not been able to read the minutes.","note":"With two infinitives the order is fixed: heb kunnen lezen. The object and niet stand before the group."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hij had beloofd dat hij","words":["me","na de vergadering","op","zou","bellen"],"answers":["Hij had beloofd dat hij me na de vergadering op zou bellen."],"en":"He had promised that he would call me after the meeting.","note":"Here the prefix op stands before the whole group: op zou bellen. (Zou opbellen, in one word, is equally right.) The short pronoun me comes right after the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a sub-clause after Ik denk dat …","from":"De vergadering zal langer moeten duren.","answers":["Ik denk dat de vergadering langer zal moeten duren."],"en":"I think that the meeting will have to last longer.","note":"In the sub-clause zal joins the group at the end, in first place: zal moeten duren. Both are infinitives, so there is no other order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"We moesten de deadline verschuiven.","answers":["We hebben de deadline moeten verschuiven."],"en":"We have had to move the deadline.","note":"Moeten takes hebben, and with another verb it stays an infinitive: hebben … moeten verschuiven. Not *hebben gemoeten."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Ze zegt dat …","from":"De aandeelhouders zullen met de fusie instemmen.","answers":["Ze zegt dat de aandeelhouders met de fusie zullen instemmen.","Ze zegt dat de aandeelhouders met de fusie in zullen stemmen.","Ze zegt dat de aandeelhouders zullen instemmen met de fusie.","Ze zegt dat de aandeelhouders in zullen stemmen met de fusie.","Zij zegt dat de aandeelhouders met de fusie zullen instemmen.","Zij zegt dat de aandeelhouders met de fusie in zullen stemmen.","Zij zegt dat de aandeelhouders zullen instemmen met de fusie.","Zij zegt dat de aandeelhouders in zullen stemmen met de fusie."],"en":"She says that the shareholders will agree to the merger.","note":"zullen joins the group at the end: zullen instemmen. The prefix in may also stand before the group: in zullen stemmen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik denk dat we zouden het project moeten bijsturen.","answers":["Ik denk dat we het project zouden moeten bijsturen.","Ik denk dat we het project bij zouden moeten sturen.","Ik denk dat we het project zouden bij moeten sturen."],"en":"I think that we should adjust the project.","note":"Nothing can stand inside the verb group. The object het project goes before it: … het project zouden moeten bijsturen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik hoor dat de voorzitter de vergadering heeft gemoeten afzeggen.","answers":["Ik hoor dat de voorzitter de vergadering heeft moeten afzeggen.","Ik hoor dat de voorzitter de vergadering af heeft moeten zeggen.","Ik hoor dat de voorzitter de vergadering heeft af moeten zeggen."],"en":"I hear that the chair has had to cancel the meeting.","note":"A modal in the perfect followed by another verb is an infinitive: heeft moeten afzeggen. Gemoeten only stands on its own."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hij zegt dat het plan wordt morgen besproken.","answers":["Hij zegt dat het plan morgen wordt besproken.","Hij zegt dat het plan morgen besproken wordt."],"en":"He says that the plan will be discussed tomorrow.","note":"In a sub-clause all verbs stand together at the end; morgen goes before the group, not between wordt and besproken."}]},
  {"id":"b2-04","level":"B2","icon":"🏗️","title":"The city of tomorrow","grammar":"Participles as adjectives: de lachende man, het gebouwde huis","summary":"Present participle = infinitive + d (groeiend): active, going on. Past participle (gebouwd): passive or done. Both take the adjective -e. Written Dutch puts what belongs to them in front: het door de gemeente gebouwde huis.","explain":"A participle can stand in front of a noun, just like an adjective: `de groeiende stad`, `het gebouwde huis`. Written Dutch, in newspapers and reports, goes further: it packs a whole relative clause into the space between the article and the noun (`het door de gemeente gebouwde huis`).\n\n### The present participle: infinitive + d\n\n`groeien` → `groeiend`, `toenemen` → `toenemend`, `leegstaan` → `leegstaand`, `gaan` → `gaand`. It is **active** and **going on**: the city is growing, the building is standing empty. In front of a noun it follows the ordinary adjective rule (unit A2 · 10): an -e, except with an indefinite het-word.\n\n| | de-word | het-word |\n| with de / het | de groeiende stad | het groeiende aantal |\n| with een | een groeiende stad | een groeiend aantal |\n\n> Een toenemend aantal jongeren vindt geen betaalbare woning. — An increasing number of young people cannot find an affordable home.\n\nWithout a noun it describes **how** someone does something, and has no ending: `Lachend liep ze weg` (she walked away laughing). `Al doende leer je` (you learn by doing) is a fixed phrase.\n\nDutch does **not** use it for a continuous tense. \"I am reading\" is `Ik lees` or `Ik ben aan het lezen` (unit B1 · 8), never *Ik ben lezend.\n\n### The past participle as an adjective\n\nThe past participle is **passive** or **finished**: `het gebouwde huis` is the house that has been built. Compare `de stijgende prijzen` (still rising) and `de gestegen prijzen` (they have risen). The -e rule is the same, with two points of spelling:\n\n- A participle ending in **-en** gets no extra -e: `de gesloten brug`, `het geschreven rapport`.\n- The ending changes the spelling as with any adjective: `verbreed` → `de verbrede weg`, `gesloopt` → `het gesloopte gebouw`.\n\n> Door de gesloten brug moeten fietsers omrijden. — Because of the closed bridge cyclists have to take a detour.\n\n### The extended attribute\n\nEverything that belongs to the participle, such as the doer with `door`, a time, a place or an object, stands **between the article and the participle**. The noun comes last. Grammars call this *de uitgebreide bijvoeglijke bepaling*.\n\n| relative clause | extended attribute |\n| de brug die vorig jaar is geopend | de vorig jaar geopende brug |\n| het huis dat door de gemeente is gebouwd | het door de gemeente gebouwde huis |\n| de mensen die in het centrum wonen | de in het centrum wonende mensen |\n\n> Het door de gemeente gebouwde parkeergebouw staat al jaren leeg. — The car park built by the municipality has been empty for years.\n> De in het centrum wonende mensen klagen over het lawaai. — The people living in the centre complain about the noise.\n\n**Reading one**: when an article is followed by a preposition or an adverb, read on to the participle and the noun: `het | door de gemeente | gebouwde | huis`.\n\n**Making one**: drop `die`/`dat` and the auxiliary (`is`, `wordt`, `werd`), keep the rest in order, and put the participle with its ending right before the noun. With a present participle the verb itself becomes the participle: `die … wonen` → `wonende`.\n\n### te + infinitive as an adjective\n\n`te` + infinitive in front of a noun means **that must be done** or **that can be done**, like `zijn te` (unit B2 · 1). It never gets an ending.\n\n> De te verwachten kosten zijn hoger dan gepland. — The costs that can be expected are higher than planned.\n> De nog te bouwen wijk krijgt een eigen station. — The neighbourhood that is still to be built will get its own station.\n\nAlso: `een niet te missen kans` (a chance not to be missed).\n\n### Attribute or relative clause?\n\nThe extended attribute is written and formal. In speech, and whenever the attribute gets long, a relative clause is clearer: `het complex dat de gemeente in 2019 met twee corporaties heeft gebouwd` reads better than *het door de gemeente in 2019 met twee corporaties gebouwde complex*.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Indefinite het-word: *een groeiende aantal → `een groeiend aantal`.\n- No extra -e after -en: *de geslotene brug → `de gesloten brug`.\n- No -end for a continuous tense: *Ik ben de plannen lezend → `Ik ben de plannen aan het lezen`.\n- No ending on te + infinitive: *de te bouwende woningen → `de te bouwen woningen`.\n- Everything before the participle: *de gebouwde door de gemeente woningen → `de door de gemeente gebouwde woningen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian passive participles work much the same: `збудований торік міст` is `de vorig jaar gebouwde brug`, with the article in front.","words":[{"nl":"bestemmingsplan","art":"het","pl":"bestemmingsplannen","en":"zoning plan","pos":"n"},{"nl":"woningbouw","art":"de","pl":"","en":"house building / housing construction","pos":"n"},{"nl":"nieuwbouwwijk","art":"de","pl":"nieuwbouwwijken","en":"new housing estate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"infrastructuur","art":"de","pl":"","en":"infrastructure","pos":"n"},{"nl":"renovatie","art":"de","pl":"renovaties","en":"renovation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bouwvergunning","art":"de","pl":"bouwvergunningen","en":"building permit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aannemer","art":"de","pl":"aannemers","en":"(building) contractor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"woningnood","art":"de","pl":"","en":"housing shortage","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stedenbouwkundige","art":"de","pl":"stedenbouwkundigen","en":"urban planner","pos":"n"},{"nl":"slopen","past":"sloopte","perf":"heeft gesloopt","en":"to demolish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"renoveren","past":"renoveerde","perf":"heeft gerenoveerd","en":"to renovate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verbreden","past":"verbreedde","perf":"heeft verbreed","en":"to widen","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aanleggen","past":"legde aan","perf":"heeft aangelegd","en":"to construct (a road, park or line)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"betaalbaar","en":"affordable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"leegstaand","en":"empty / vacant (of a building)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"toenemend","en":"increasing","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De groeiende stad heeft dringend meer betaalbare woningen nodig.","en":"The growing city urgently needs more affordable homes.","hint":["groeien","stad","dringend","betaalbaar","woning","nodig"],"note":"groeiend is the present participle: groeien + d. Before a de-word with de it gets -e: de groeiende stad.","alt":["De groeiende stad heeft meer betaalbare woningen dringend nodig."]},{"nl":"Door de woningnood vindt een toenemend aantal jongeren geen huis.","en":"Because of the housing shortage an increasing number of young people find no house.","hint":["woningnood","vinden","toenemend","aantal","jongere","huis"],"note":"Het aantal is a het-word, and with een it gets no -e: een toenemend aantal. The verb may be singular (aantal) or plural (jongeren).","alt":["Door de woningnood vinden een toenemend aantal jongeren geen huis.","Een toenemend aantal jongeren vindt door de woningnood geen huis.","Een toenemend aantal jongeren vinden door de woningnood geen huis."]},{"nl":"Het leegstaande kantoorgebouw wordt omgebouwd tot appartementen.","en":"The empty office building is being converted into flats.","hint":["leegstaand","kantoorgebouw","ombouwen","appartement"],"note":"leegstaand is the present participle of leegstaan: the building is standing empty. With het it gets -e: het leegstaande kantoorgebouw.","alt":["Het leegstaande kantoorgebouw wordt tot appartementen omgebouwd."]},{"nl":"Zuchtend bekeek de stedenbouwkundige de nieuwe tekeningen.","en":"Sighing, the urban planner looked at the new drawings.","hint":["zuchten","bekijken","stedenbouwkundige","nieuw","tekening"],"note":"Without a noun the present participle tells how someone does something, and has no ending: zuchtend. It takes the first place, so the verb follows.","alt":["De stedenbouwkundige bekeek zuchtend de nieuwe tekeningen."]},{"nl":"De verouderde infrastructuur van de stad moet worden vernieuwd.","en":"The outdated infrastructure of the city must be renewed.","hint":["verouderen","infrastructuur","stad","moeten","vernieuwen"],"note":"verouderd is the past participle of verouderen, used as an adjective: de verouderde infrastructuur. The -e follows the normal rule.","alt":["De verouderde infrastructuur van de stad moet vernieuwd worden."]},{"nl":"Het door de gemeente gebouwde parkeergebouw staat al jaren leeg.","en":"The car park built by the municipality has been empty for years. (with an extended attribute)","hint":["gemeente","bouwen","parkeergebouw","jaren","leegstaan"],"note":"Extended attribute: door de gemeente stands between het and the participle gebouwde, and the noun comes last. Al jaren with the present tense: has been … for years."},{"nl":"De vorig jaar verbrede weg is nu alweer te smal.","en":"The road that was widened last year is already too narrow again now. (with an extended attribute)","hint":["vorig jaar","verbreden","weg","alweer","smal"],"note":"verbreed + e → verbrede: the open syllable needs only one e. The time vorig jaar stands between de and the participle.","alt":["De vorig jaar verbrede weg is alweer te smal."]},{"nl":"De in het centrum wonende mensen klagen over het lawaai van de woningbouw.","en":"The people living in the centre complain about the noise of the house building. (with an extended attribute)","hint":["centrum","wonen","mens","klagen","lawaai","woningbouw"],"note":"A present participle with its place in front of it: de in het centrum wonende mensen = de mensen die in het centrum wonen."},{"nl":"De te verwachten kosten van de renovatie zijn veel hoger dan gepland.","en":"The costs to be expected for the renovation are much higher than planned.","hint":["verwachten","kosten","renovatie","hoog","plannen"],"note":"te + infinitive before a noun: de te verwachten kosten = the costs that can be expected. Te verwachten has no ending."},{"nl":"De nog te bouwen nieuwbouwwijk krijgt een eigen station.","en":"The new housing estate that is still to be built will get its own station. (with te + infinitive)","hint":["nog","bouwen","nieuwbouwwijk","krijgen","eigen","station"],"note":"de nog te bouwen nieuwbouwwijk: te + infinitive means it still has to be built. The present tense krijgt is enough for the future.","alt":["De nog te bouwen nieuwbouwwijk zal een eigen station krijgen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Een ___ aantal bewoners klaagt over de bouw.","options":["groeiende","groeiend","gegroeide"],"correct":1,"en":"A growing number of residents complain about the building work.","note":"Het aantal is a het-word, and after een a het-word gets no -e: een groeiend aantal. Gegroeide would mean 'that has grown' and needs a different ending too."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De ___ brug is weer open voor het verkeer.","options":["verbreedde","verbreed","verbrede","verbreede"],"correct":2,"en":"The widened bridge is open to traffic again.","note":"The participle verbreed gets -e before de brug, and in an open syllable one e is enough: verbrede. Verbreedde is the simple past, not an adjective."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Op de plek van het ___ gebouw komen nieuwe woningen.","options":["gesloopt","gesloopde","gesloopte","slopende"],"correct":2,"en":"New homes are coming on the site of the demolished building.","note":"Slopen has the stem sloop; p is in 't kofschip, so the participle is gesloopt and with -e gesloopte. Slopende would mean the building is doing the demolishing."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De ___ maatregelen worden morgen bekendgemaakt.","options":["te nemende","nemen","te nemen"],"correct":2,"en":"The measures to be taken will be announced tomorrow.","note":"te + infinitive before a noun means 'that must be done' and never gets an ending: de te nemen maatregelen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De wethouder opende ___ (lachen) het nieuwe fietspad.","answers":["lachend"],"en":"Laughing, the alderman opened the new cycle path.","note":"The present participle as an adverb of manner: lachen + d, without an ending, because there is no noun after it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De ___ (renoveren) flats zijn alweer verhuurd.","answers":["gerenoveerde"],"en":"The renovated flats have already been let again.","note":"Renoveren: ge- + the stem renoveer + d (r is not in 't kofschip). Before de flats it gets -e: gerenoveerde."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit is het in 2020 ___ (aanleggen) park.","answers":["aangelegde"],"en":"This is the park that was laid out in 2020.","note":"Extended attribute: in 2020 stands between het and the participle. Aanleggen → aangelegd, and with het + noun it gets -e: aangelegde."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Door de ___ (sluiten) brug moeten we omrijden.","answers":["gesloten"],"en":"Because of the closed bridge we have to take a detour.","note":"Gesloten ends in -en, and such participles get no extra -e: de gesloten brug, never *geslotene."},{"type":"order","lead":"De","words":["door de aannemer","gebouwde","woningen","zijn","erg duur"],"answers":["De door de aannemer gebouwde woningen zijn erg duur."],"en":"The homes built by the contractor are very expensive.","note":"Everything that belongs to the participle stands between the article and the participle: de | door de aannemer | gebouwde | woningen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het","words":["vorig jaar","door de gemeenteraad","goedgekeurde","bestemmingsplan","wordt","aangepast"],"answers":["Het vorig jaar door de gemeenteraad goedgekeurde bestemmingsplan wordt aangepast.","Het door de gemeenteraad vorig jaar goedgekeurde bestemmingsplan wordt aangepast."],"en":"The zoning plan approved by the municipal council last year is being changed.","note":"Time and doer both go between het and goedgekeurde, usually the time first. The whole group het … bestemmingsplan is the subject, so wordt follows it."},{"type":"order","lead":"De","words":["op de bus","wachtende","reizigers","stonden","in de regen"],"answers":["De op de bus wachtende reizigers stonden in de regen."],"en":"The travellers waiting for the bus stood in the rain.","note":"A present participle with what belongs to it: wachten op de bus → de op de bus wachtende reizigers. The preposition phrase comes first, the participle right before the noun."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn the relative clause into an extended attribute.","from":"Het park dat vorig jaar is aangelegd, is erg populair.","answers":["Het vorig jaar aangelegde park is erg populair."],"en":"The park that was laid out last year is very popular.","note":"Drop dat and the auxiliary is, keep vorig jaar, and put the participle with -e before the noun: het vorig jaar aangelegde park."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn the extended attribute into a relative clause with die.","from":"De in de nieuwbouwwijk wonende gezinnen willen een school.","answers":["De gezinnen die in de nieuwbouwwijk wonen, willen een school.","De gezinnen die wonen in de nieuwbouwwijk, willen een school."],"en":"The families who live in the new housing estate want a school.","note":"The present participle becomes a conjugated verb at the end of the relative clause: die … wonen. Gezinnen is plural, so wonen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn the extended attribute into a relative clause in the passive, with die … is.","from":"De door de aannemer aangevraagde bouwvergunning is geweigerd.","answers":["De bouwvergunning die door de aannemer is aangevraagd, is geweigerd.","De bouwvergunning die door de aannemer aangevraagd is, is geweigerd.","De bouwvergunning die is aangevraagd door de aannemer, is geweigerd."],"en":"The building permit that was applied for by the contractor has been refused.","note":"The participle goes back into a passive relative clause: die door de aannemer is aangevraagd. Is and the participle may stand in either order."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik ben de plannen voor de nieuwe wijk lezend.","answers":["Ik ben de plannen voor de nieuwe wijk aan het lezen.","Ik lees de plannen voor de nieuwe wijk."],"en":"I am reading the plans for the new neighbourhood.","note":"Dutch does not make a continuous tense with the present participle. Use aan het + infinitive, or simply the present tense."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De te bouwende woningen moeten betaalbaar zijn.","answers":["De te bouwen woningen moeten betaalbaar zijn."],"en":"The homes that are to be built must be affordable.","note":"te + infinitive before a noun gets no ending: de te bouwen woningen. Te bouwende mixes it up with the present participle bouwende."}]},
  {"id":"b2-05","level":"B2","icon":"📄","title":"Official letters","grammar":"Noun style: het + infinitive, -ing, and turning it back into verbs","summary":"Formal Dutch turns actions into nouns: het + infinitive (het indienen van een aanvraag), -ing nouns, always de (de betaling), and phrases like na ontvangst van. The object follows van.","explain":"Official letters are full of nouns where everyday Dutch would use a verb: `Na ontvangst van uw aanvraag …` instead of `Nadat wij uw aanvraag hebben ontvangen …`. This is called noun style (*de naamwoordstijl*). You need to read it, write it where a formal text asks for it, and turn it back into verbs.\n\n### 1. het + infinitive\n\nAlmost any verb becomes a noun when you put `het` in front of the infinitive. It is **always a het-word** and has no plural. The object of the verb follows with `van`; the doer, if it is mentioned at all, follows with `door`.\n\n> Het invullen van het formulier duurt ongeveer tien minuten. — Filling in the form takes about ten minutes.\n> Bij het indienen van een bezwaar moet u een kopie van het besluit meesturen. — When submitting an objection you must enclose a copy of the decision.\n\n`bij het …` means \"when …, while …\", `na het …` \"after …\", `voor het …` \"before …\". A separable verb stays in one word: `het indienen`, `het intrekken`.\n\nAn adverb that goes with the verb may stay an adverb or become an adjective with -e: `het snel groeien` and `het snelle groeien` van de stad are both standard Dutch. Official letters usually keep the form without -e, especially with a phrase like `te laat`:\n\n> Het tijdig indienen van de aanvraag is uw eigen verantwoordelijkheid. — Submitting the application on time is your own responsibility.\n\n### 2. Nouns in -ing and other action nouns\n\nMany verbs make a noun with stem + `-ing`:\n\n| verb | noun |\n| betalen | de betaling |\n| behandelen | de behandeling |\n| verlengen | de verlenging |\n| afwijzen | de afwijzing |\n| indienen | de indiening |\n\n**Every -ing noun is a de-word**, with the plural in -en: `de betalingen`. Not every verb has an -ing noun. Many have a noun of their own, and then that is the one to use; its article has to be learned: `ontvangen → de ontvangst`, `aanvragen → de aanvraag`, `besluiten → het besluit`, `vertrekken → het vertrek`, `uitstellen → het uitstel`.\n\n> De behandeling van uw aanvraag duurt ongeveer acht weken. — The processing of your application takes about eight weeks.\n\n### 3. A preposition and a noun instead of a sub-clause\n\n| noun style | with a verb |\n| na ontvangst van uw brief | nadat wij uw brief hebben ontvangen |\n| bij vertrek | als u vertrekt |\n| wegens ziekte | omdat iemand ziek is |\n| ter voorkoming van fraude | om fraude te voorkomen |\n| in afwachting van het besluit | terwijl u op het besluit wacht |\n| op verzoek van uw werkgever | omdat uw werkgever erom heeft gevraagd |\n\nSeveral have no article: `na ontvangst`, `bij vertrek`. `ter` is an old form that only survives in fixed phrases (more in B2 · 21). `wegens` is a preposition: it takes a noun, never a sub-clause.\n\n> Wegens ziekte gaat het spreekuur vandaag niet door. — Because of illness the surgery hours are cancelled today.\n\n### Word order\n\nThe whole phrase counts as **one element**. When it stands first, the conjugated verb follows it directly, and then the subject.\n\n> Na ontvangst van uw brief nemen wij contact met u op. — After receipt of your letter we will contact you.\n\n### Reading it: find the action and the doer\n\nNoun style hides who does what, and when. Find the verb inside the noun (`behandeling → behandelen`) and the doer (often the authority), then say it with a verb:\n\n- `de behandeling van uw aanvraag` → wij behandelen uw aanvraag\n- `wegens te late indiening` → omdat u de aanvraag te laat hebt ingediend\n\n`te laat` becomes `te late` because it now stands before a de-noun.\n\n### Writing it yourself\n\nVerbs are clearer, so use them where you can; noun style fits forms and formal letters. Avoid chains of `van`: `de datum van de behandeling van de aanvraag van uw werkgever` is correct but hard to read; `de datum waarop wij de aanvraag van uw werkgever behandelen` is better.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **het** with the infinitive, **de** with -ing: `het verlengen`, `de verlenging`; never *het verlenging or *de verlengen.\n- The object needs `van`: `het intrekken van uw aanvraag`, not *het intrekken uw aanvraag.\n- After the phrase at the start comes the verb: `Na ontvangst van uw brief hebben wij …`, not *Na ontvangst van uw brief wij hebben …\n- `wegens` + noun, `omdat` + sub-clause: `Omdat ik ziek was, …`, never *Wegens ik ziek was, …\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian official style does the same: `подання заяви`, `розгляд заяви`. Where Ukrainian puts the object in the genitive, Dutch uses `van`: `het indienen van een aanvraag`, `de behandeling van de aanvraag`.","words":[{"nl":"aanvrager","art":"de","pl":"aanvragers","en":"applicant","pos":"n"},{"nl":"indienen","past":"diende in","perf":"heeft ingediend","en":"to submit / to file","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"ontvangst","art":"de","pl":"","en":"receipt (of a letter, payment)","pos":"n","tag":"no plural in this sense"},{"nl":"behandeling","art":"de","pl":"behandelingen","en":"processing (of a case) / treatment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verlenging","art":"de","pl":"verlengingen","en":"extension / renewal","pos":"n"},{"nl":"termijn","art":"de","pl":"termijnen","en":"period allowed / time limit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"besluit","art":"het","pl":"besluiten","en":"decision","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezwaar","art":"het","pl":"bezwaren","en":"objection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afwijzing","art":"de","pl":"afwijzingen","en":"rejection / refusal","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitstel","art":"het","pl":"","en":"postponement / extra time","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"vaststellen","past":"stelde vast","perf":"heeft vastgesteld","en":"to fix / to determine","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verstrekken","past":"verstrekte","perf":"heeft verstrekt","en":"to provide (information)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"intrekken","past":"trok in","perf":"heeft ingetrokken","en":"to withdraw (an application)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"tijdig","en":"timely / in time","pos":"adj","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"wegens","en":"because of / owing to","pos":"prep","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"in afwachting van","en":"pending / awaiting","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Na ontvangst van uw aanvraag sturen wij u binnen twee weken een bevestiging.","en":"After receipt of your application we will send you a confirmation within two weeks.","hint":["na","ontvangst","aanvraag","sturen","bevestiging"],"note":"Na ontvangst van stands for nadat wij … hebben ontvangen. The whole phrase is the first element, so the verb sturen follows directly and then wij.","alt":["Wij sturen u na ontvangst van uw aanvraag binnen twee weken een bevestiging."]},{"nl":"Bij het indienen van een bezwaar moet u een kopie van het besluit meesturen.","en":"When submitting an objection you must enclose a copy of the decision.","hint":["bij","indienen","bezwaar","moeten","kopie","meesturen"],"note":"het + infinitive: het indienen, always a het-word. Its object, een bezwaar, follows with van. Bij het … means \"when …\".","alt":["U moet bij het indienen van een bezwaar een kopie van het besluit meesturen."]},{"nl":"Wegens ziekte heb ik niet binnen de termijn kunnen reageren.","en":"Because of illness I was not able to respond within the time limit.","hint":["wegens","ziekte","termijn","kunnen","reageren"],"note":"Wegens is a preposition and takes a noun without an article: wegens ziekte, the noun-style version of omdat ik ziek was.","alt":["Wegens ziekte kon ik niet binnen de termijn reageren.","Ik heb wegens ziekte niet binnen de termijn kunnen reageren.","Ik kon wegens ziekte niet binnen de termijn reageren."]},{"nl":"De behandeling van uw aanvraag duurt langer als u niet alle gegevens verstrekt.","en":"The processing of your application takes longer if you do not provide all the details.","hint":["behandeling","aanvraag","duren","gegevens","verstrekken"],"note":"behandelen → de behandeling: an -ing noun is always de. The action is behandelen and the doer, the authority, is not mentioned.","alt":["De behandeling van uw aanvraag duurt langer wanneer u niet alle gegevens verstrekt."]},{"nl":"Het tijdig indienen van de aanvraag is uw eigen verantwoordelijkheid.","en":"Submitting the application on time is your own responsibility.","hint":["tijdig","indienen","aanvraag","eigen","verantwoordelijkheid"],"note":"tijdig goes with the verb inside het indienen. Official texts keep it without -e; het tijdige indienen is also correct.","alt":["Het tijdige indienen van de aanvraag is uw eigen verantwoordelijkheid."]},{"nl":"Op verzoek van uw werkgever hebben wij de termijn met een maand verlengd.","en":"At the request of your employer we have extended the time limit by one month.","hint":["op verzoek van","werkgever","termijn","maand","verlengen"],"note":"Op verzoek van uw werkgever replaces a clause like omdat uw werkgever erom heeft gevraagd. It is one element, so hebben comes straight after it.","alt":["Wij hebben de termijn op verzoek van uw werkgever met een maand verlengd."]},{"nl":"In afwachting van ons besluit mag u in Nederland blijven.","en":"Pending our decision you may stay in the Netherlands.","hint":["in afwachting van","besluit","mogen","Nederland","blijven"],"note":"In afwachting van packs \"while you wait for\" into a noun phrase. It stands first, so the verb mag follows and then u.","alt":["U mag in afwachting van ons besluit in Nederland blijven.","U mag in Nederland blijven in afwachting van ons besluit."]},{"nl":"Omdat u het formulier te laat hebt ingediend, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","en":"Because you submitted the form too late, your application is being rejected.","hint":["omdat","formulier","te laat","indienen","aanvraag","afwijzen"],"note":"This is the verb version of wegens te late indiening: the doer (u) and the tense are visible again. Hebt or heeft are both correct with u.","alt":["Omdat u het formulier te laat heeft ingediend, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Omdat u het formulier te laat ingediend hebt, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Omdat u het formulier te laat ingediend heeft, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Uw aanvraag wordt afgewezen omdat u het formulier te laat hebt ingediend.","Uw aanvraag wordt afgewezen omdat u het formulier te laat heeft ingediend."]},{"nl":"Wegens te late indiening is uw aanvraag afgewezen.","en":"Because of late submission your application has been rejected.","hint":["wegens","te laat","indiening","aanvraag","afwijzen"],"note":"indienen → de indiening. Te laat now stands before a de-noun, so it becomes an adjective with -e: te late indiening.","alt":["Uw aanvraag is wegens te late indiening afgewezen.","Wegens late indiening is uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Uw aanvraag is wegens late indiening afgewezen."]},{"nl":"Na het ontvangen van het besluit hebt u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","en":"After receiving the decision you have six weeks to lodge an objection.","hint":["na","ontvangen","besluit","tijd","bezwaar maken"],"note":"het ontvangen van het besluit: het + infinitive with the object after van. Na ontvangst van het besluit says the same with the noun of ontvangen.","alt":["Na het ontvangen van het besluit heeft u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","Na ontvangst van het besluit hebt u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","Na ontvangst van het besluit heeft u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","U hebt na het ontvangen van het besluit zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","U heeft na het ontvangen van het besluit zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ invullen van het formulier kost ongeveer tien minuten.","options":["De","Het","Een"],"correct":1,"en":"Filling in the form takes about ten minutes.","note":"An infinitive used as a noun is always a het-word: het invullen. De goes with -ing nouns, not with an infinitive."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De ___ van uw verblijfsvergunning is alleen mogelijk op afspraak.","options":["verlengen","verleng","verlenging","verlengd"],"correct":2,"en":"The renewal of your residence permit is only possible by appointment.","note":"With de in front only the -ing noun fits: de verlenging. The infinitive would need het: het verlengen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na ___ van uw brief nemen wij contact met u op.","options":["ontvangen","ontvangst","ontvanging","het ontvangst"],"correct":1,"en":"After receipt of your letter we will contact you.","note":"ontvangen has its own noun, de ontvangst; there is no ontvanging. Ontvangen without het cannot stand after na, and ontvangst is a de-word."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ ziekte gaat de cursus deze week niet door.","options":["Omdat","Wegens","Doordat"],"correct":1,"en":"Because of illness the course is not going ahead this week.","note":"Ziekte is a noun, so a preposition is needed: wegens. Omdat and doordat are conjunctions and need a full sub-clause with a verb."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tegen de ___ (afwijzen) van uw aanvraag kunt u bezwaar maken.","answers":["afwijzing"],"en":"You can lodge an objection against the rejection of your application.","note":"De in front asks for the -ing noun: afwijzen → de afwijzing. Het afwijzen would need het."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De ___ (wijzigen) van uw adres moet u binnen vijf dagen doorgeven.","answers":["wijziging"],"en":"You must report the change of your address within five days.","note":"wijzigen → de wijziging. Every -ing noun is a de-word, which matches the de in front."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na ___ (betalen) van het bedrag ontvangt u een bevestiging.","answers":["betaling","het betalen","de betaling"],"en":"After payment of the amount you will receive a confirmation.","note":"Both noun forms are correct: na betaling (the usual fixed phrase, no article), na de betaling, or na het betalen. Na betalen without het is not."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (snel) behandelen van aanvragen is ons belangrijkste doel.","answers":["snel","snelle"],"en":"Processing applications quickly is our most important aim.","note":"An adverb with het + infinitive may stay an adverb (het snel behandelen) or become an adjective with -e (het snelle behandelen). Both are standard."},{"type":"order","lead":"Na ontvangst van uw brief","words":["nemen","wij","zo snel mogelijk","contact met u","op"],"answers":["Na ontvangst van uw brief nemen wij zo snel mogelijk contact met u op."],"en":"After receipt of your letter we will contact you as soon as possible.","note":"The noun phrase at the start is one element, so the verb nemen comes second and wij third. The particle op closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Wegens financiële problemen","words":["heeft","zij","uitstel van betaling","aangevraagd"],"answers":["Wegens financiële problemen heeft zij uitstel van betaling aangevraagd."],"en":"Because of financial problems she has applied for a postponement of payment.","note":"Wegens + noun phrase is the first element; heeft follows directly, then zij. Uitstel van betaling is itself noun style: she wants to pay later."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace the sub-clause with na + the noun of ontvangen.","from":"Nadat wij uw aanvraag hebben ontvangen, sturen wij u een bevestiging.","answers":["Na ontvangst van uw aanvraag sturen wij u een bevestiging.","Wij sturen u na ontvangst van uw aanvraag een bevestiging."],"en":"After receipt of your application we will send you a confirmation.","note":"ontvangen → de ontvangst, with no article in this fixed phrase. The doer wij disappears from the phrase, and the verb sturen comes straight after it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace the omdat-clause with wegens + an -ing noun.","from":"Omdat de aanvraag te laat is ingediend, wordt deze niet behandeld.","answers":["Wegens te late indiening wordt de aanvraag niet behandeld.","De aanvraag wordt wegens te late indiening niet behandeld.","Wegens de te late indiening wordt de aanvraag niet behandeld."],"en":"Because of late submission the application is not being processed.","note":"indienen → de indiening, and te laat becomes te late before the noun. The noun aanvraag now has to be named in the main clause."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn the noun phrase into a sub-clause with omdat.","from":"Wegens het ontbreken van de handtekening van de aanvrager kunnen wij de aanvraag niet behandelen.","answers":["Omdat de handtekening van de aanvrager ontbreekt, kunnen wij de aanvraag niet behandelen.","Wij kunnen de aanvraag niet behandelen, omdat de handtekening van de aanvrager ontbreekt."],"en":"Because the applicant's signature is missing, we cannot process the application.","note":"The action is ontbreken and its subject is de handtekening van de aanvrager, which was hidden behind the first van. In the sub-clause the verb goes to the end: ontbreekt."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Write it with verbs: use wij and nadat.","from":"De vaststelling van het bedrag vindt plaats na ontvangst van alle gegevens.","answers":["Wij stellen het bedrag vast nadat wij alle gegevens hebben ontvangen.","Wij stellen het bedrag vast nadat wij alle gegevens ontvangen hebben.","Nadat wij alle gegevens hebben ontvangen, stellen wij het bedrag vast.","Nadat wij alle gegevens ontvangen hebben, stellen wij het bedrag vast."],"en":"We determine the amount after we have received all the details.","note":"vaststelling → vaststellen, ontvangst → ontvangen. With verbs the doer (wij) appears. At the end of the sub-clause both hebben ontvangen and ontvangen hebben are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Na ontvangst van uw brief wij hebben uw gegevens gecontroleerd.","answers":["Na ontvangst van uw brief hebben wij uw gegevens gecontroleerd."],"en":"After receipt of your letter we checked your details.","note":"Na ontvangst van uw brief is the first element, so the conjugated verb hebben must come second, before the subject wij."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het intrekken uw aanvraag is kosteloos.","answers":["Het intrekken van uw aanvraag is kosteloos."],"en":"Withdrawing your application is free of charge.","note":"With het + infinitive the object cannot follow directly; it needs van: het intrekken van uw aanvraag."}]},
  {"id":"b2-06","level":"B2","icon":"📈","title":"The world of work","grammar":"Formal conjunctions: aangezien, mits, tenzij, zodra, naarmate","summary":"Formal conjunctions start a sub-clause, verb at the end: aangezien (since), doordat (cause), mits (provided), tenzij (unless, no niet), zodra, naarmate. After al the verb comes first: Al is het zwaar, …","explain":"Contracts, vacancy texts and news about the labour market use conjunctions you rarely hear at home. They all start a **sub-clause**, so the conjugated verb goes to the end, as after `omdat` and `dat` (unit A2 · 23). The one exception is `al`, below.\n\n### Word order\n\n> Aangezien de vraag groot is, stijgen de lonen. — Since demand is high, wages are rising.\n\nA sub-clause at the start fills the first place, so the main clause starts with its verb: `stijgen de lonen`. When two verbs end the sub-clause, both orders are standard: `zodra het contract is getekend` and `zodra het contract getekend is`.\n\n### Reason and cause\n\n| formal | meaning | everyday |\n| aangezien | since, as | omdat |\n| daar | since, as (very formal) | omdat |\n| nu | now that | nu |\n| doordat | because (a cause) | omdat |\n\n`aangezien` and `daar` give a reason the reader knows or accepts, and usually start the sentence. `doordat` gives a **cause**: something that happened without anyone's intention. For a motive or a decision, use `omdat`.\n\n> Doordat de economie krimpt, neemt de werkloosheid toe. — Because the economy is shrinking, unemployment is rising.\n> Omdat hij meer wilde verdienen, zocht hij een andere baan. — Because he wanted to earn more, he looked for another job.\n\n### Condition\n\n| formal | meaning | everyday |\n| indien | if | als |\n| mits | provided that | als … maar wel |\n| tenzij | unless | behalve als |\n| zolang | as long as | zolang |\n\nAlso `in het geval dat` (in case) and `voor zover` (as far as: `voor zover ik weet`). `mits` is a strict condition and normally follows the main clause. `tenzij` already contains the negation: \"unless you resign\" = \"if you do not resign\".\n\n> U hebt recht op een uitkering, tenzij u zelf ontslag neemt. — You are entitled to a benefit unless you resign yourself.\n\n### Purpose and result\n\n`zodat` gives a result: \"so that, with the result that\". `opdat` gives a purpose but is very formal; everyday Dutch uses `om … te` or `zodat`.\n\n### Concession\n\n`hoewel` and the very formal `ofschoon` mean \"although\". `al` (even though) is different: **the verb comes directly after al**, and the main clause keeps its normal order. `ook al` works the same way; `zelfs als` (even if) has normal sub-clause order.\n\n> Al is het contract tijdelijk, het is een goede stap in mijn loopbaan. — Even though the contract is temporary, it is a good step in my career.\n\n### Time\n\n`zodra` as soon as, `zolang` as long as, `sinds` since (time only), `terwijl` while, and also \"whereas\" for a contrast. `naarmate` means \"as, the more …\", usually with a comparison in both halves. `alvorens` (before) is formal and normally takes `te` + infinitive: `alvorens te tekenen` = `voordat u tekent`.\n\n> Naarmate werknemers ouder worden, wordt omscholing moeilijker. — As employees get older, retraining becomes harder.\n> Zij heeft een vast contract, terwijl haar collega's een tijdelijk contract hebben. — She has a permanent contract, whereas her colleagues have a temporary one.\n\nFor `zoals` and `alsof`, see unit B2 · 18.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **No niet after tenzij**: `tenzij u zelf ontslag neemt`. *Tenzij u niet zelf ontslag neemt means the opposite.\n- **mits is not plain als**: it is only for a condition that must be met. `Als u ziek bent, belt u uw leidinggevende`, not *Mits u ziek bent.\n- **doordat is not for motives**: `Omdat zij meer tijd wilde`, not *Doordat zij meer tijd wilde.\n- **sinds is time only**: English \"since\" can also be a reason, Dutch `sinds` cannot. For a reason use `aangezien`.\n- **al + verb**: `Al is het werk zwaar`, not *Al het werk zwaar is.\n- **alvorens + te**: `Alvorens te tekenen`; with a subject, use `voordat`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `оскільки` is `aangezien`, not `sinds`: `sinds` is only `відтоді як`. In Ukrainian a conjunction does not change the word order of its clause; in Dutch it sends the verb to the end.","words":[{"nl":"arbeidsmarkt","art":"de","pl":"arbeidsmarkten","en":"labour market","pos":"n"},{"nl":"arbeidsovereenkomst","art":"de","pl":"arbeidsovereenkomsten","en":"employment contract","pos":"n","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"arbeidsvoorwaarden","art":"de","pl":"","en":"terms of employment","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"werkloosheid","art":"de","pl":"","en":"unemployment","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"uitkering","art":"de","pl":"uitkeringen","en":"benefit (payment)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ontslag","art":"het","pl":"ontslagen","en":"dismissal / resignation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"werkzoekende","art":"de","pl":"werkzoekenden","en":"jobseeker","pos":"n"},{"nl":"promotie","art":"de","pl":"promoties","en":"promotion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vakbond","art":"de","pl":"vakbonden","en":"trade union","pos":"n"},{"nl":"werkgelegenheid","art":"de","pl":"","en":"employment (jobs available)","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"ontslagvergoeding","art":"de","pl":"ontslagvergoedingen","en":"severance pay","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ontslaan","past":"ontsloeg","perf":"heeft ontslagen","en":"to dismiss / to fire","pos":"v"},{"nl":"in dienst nemen","en":"to take on / to hire (staff)","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"zich omscholen","past":"schoolde me om","perf":"heeft zich omgeschoold","en":"to retrain","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"tijdelijk","en":"temporary","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"gekwalificeerd","en":"qualified","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Aangezien de vraag naar technici groot is, stijgen hun lonen snel.","en":"Since demand for technicians is high, their wages are rising quickly.","hint":["aangezien","vraag","technicus","groot","stijgen","loon"],"note":"aangezien gives a reason and makes a sub-clause: is goes to the end. The sub-clause stands first, so the main clause starts with stijgen.","alt":["Hun lonen stijgen snel, aangezien de vraag naar technici groot is."]},{"nl":"Doordat de economie krimpt, neemt de werkloosheid toe.","en":"Because the economy is shrinking, unemployment is rising.","hint":["doordat","economie","krimpen","werkloosheid","toenemen"],"note":"doordat: a cause, not anyone's decision. Omdat is also correct here; for a motive only omdat fits.","alt":["De werkloosheid neemt toe doordat de economie krimpt.","Omdat de economie krimpt, neemt de werkloosheid toe.","De werkloosheid neemt toe omdat de economie krimpt."]},{"nl":"U hebt recht op een uitkering, tenzij u zelf ontslag hebt genomen.","en":"You are entitled to a benefit, unless you have resigned yourself.","hint":["recht","uitkering","tenzij","zelf","ontslag nemen"],"note":"tenzij already means \"if … not\", so there is no niet in the clause. Ontslag nemen is to resign; ontslagen worden is to be dismissed.","alt":["U heeft recht op een uitkering, tenzij u zelf ontslag heeft genomen.","U hebt recht op een uitkering, tenzij u zelf ontslag genomen hebt.","U heeft recht op een uitkering, tenzij u zelf ontslag genomen heeft."]},{"nl":"Uw arbeidsovereenkomst wordt verlengd, mits de proeftijd goed verloopt.","en":"Your employment contract will be extended, provided the probation period goes well.","hint":["arbeidsovereenkomst","verlengen","mits","proeftijd","verlopen"],"note":"mits: only if this condition is met. It follows the main clause, and verloopt goes to the end of the sub-clause."},{"nl":"Zodra de vakbond akkoord gaat, wordt de nieuwe regeling ingevoerd.","en":"As soon as the trade union agrees, the new arrangement will be introduced.","hint":["zodra","vakbond","akkoord gaan","regeling","invoeren"],"note":"zodra makes a sub-clause: gaat at the end, after akkoord. The clause comes first, so wordt follows directly.","alt":["De nieuwe regeling wordt ingevoerd zodra de vakbond akkoord gaat."]},{"nl":"Naarmate werknemers ouder worden, wordt het moeilijker om zich om te scholen.","en":"As employees get older, it becomes harder to retrain.","hint":["naarmate","werknemer","oud","moeilijk","zich omscholen"],"note":"naarmate links two things that grow together; both halves contain a comparison: ouder, moeilijker. Worden goes to the end of the sub-clause.","alt":["Het wordt moeilijker om zich om te scholen naarmate werknemers ouder worden."]},{"nl":"Al is het contract tijdelijk, het is een goede stap in mijn loopbaan.","en":"Even though the contract is temporary, it is a good step in my career.","hint":["al","contract","tijdelijk","stap","loopbaan"],"note":"After al the verb comes first (al is het contract), and the main clause keeps its own order: het is. With hoewel both clauses change.","alt":["Ook al is het contract tijdelijk, het is een goede stap in mijn loopbaan.","Hoewel het contract tijdelijk is, is het een goede stap in mijn loopbaan."]},{"nl":"Nu de werkgelegenheid in de zorg groeit, scholen veel mensen zich om.","en":"Now that employment in health care is growing, many people are retraining.","hint":["nu","werkgelegenheid","zorg","groeien","zich omscholen"],"note":"nu as a conjunction means \"now that\" and sends groeit to the end. In the main clause the separable verb splits: scholen … zich om.","alt":["Veel mensen scholen zich om nu de werkgelegenheid in de zorg groeit."]},{"nl":"Zij heeft een vast contract, terwijl haar collega's een tijdelijk contract hebben.","en":"She has a permanent contract, whereas her colleagues have a temporary contract.","hint":["vast","contract","terwijl","collega","tijdelijk"],"note":"terwijl is used here for a contrast (whereas), not for time. It is still a sub-clause: hebben at the end."},{"nl":"Alvorens een arbeidsovereenkomst te tekenen, moet u de arbeidsvoorwaarden goed lezen.","en":"Before signing an employment contract, you should read the terms of employment carefully. (formal)","hint":["alvorens","arbeidsovereenkomst","tekenen","moeten","arbeidsvoorwaarden","lezen"],"note":"alvorens takes te + infinitive and no subject. The everyday version is a full sub-clause: voordat u een arbeidsovereenkomst tekent.","alt":["U moet de arbeidsvoorwaarden goed lezen alvorens een arbeidsovereenkomst te tekenen.","Voordat u een arbeidsovereenkomst tekent, moet u de arbeidsvoorwaarden goed lezen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het bedrijf neemt haar in dienst, ___ zij binnen een jaar haar diploma haalt.","options":["tenzij","mits","doordat","alvorens"],"correct":1,"en":"The company will take her on, provided that she obtains her diploma within a year.","note":"mits: a condition that must be met. Tenzij would mean the opposite, doordat gives a cause, and alvorens needs te + infinitive."},{"type":"choose","nl":"U hebt recht op een uitkering, ___ u zelf ontslag neemt.","options":["tenzij","mits","zodat","aangezien"],"correct":0,"en":"You are entitled to a benefit unless you resign yourself.","note":"tenzij = unless, \"if … not\". Mits would make resigning the condition for the benefit, the opposite of what is meant."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de arbeidsmarkt krapper wordt, krijgen werkzoekenden meer kansen.","options":["Tenzij","Alvorens","Naarmate","Opdat"],"correct":2,"en":"As the labour market gets tighter, jobseekers get more opportunities.","note":"naarmate links two developments that grow together: krapper … meer. Alvorens takes te + infinitive, opdat states a purpose."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Al ___, ik neem de baan toch.","options":["het salaris laag is","is het salaris laag","het salaris is laag"],"correct":1,"en":"Even though the salary is low, I am taking the job anyway.","note":"al is the exception among these conjunctions: the verb comes directly after it, as in a question. The main clause keeps its normal order: ik neem."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (as soon as) zij met pensioen gaat, wil zij een jaar gaan reizen.","answers":["Zodra"],"en":"As soon as she retires, she wants to travel for a year.","note":"zodra = as soon as. It starts a sub-clause, so gaat goes to the end and the main clause starts with wil."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik werk hier al tien jaar, ___ (although) ik nooit promotie heb gekregen.","answers":["hoewel","ofschoon","alhoewel"],"en":"I have worked here for ten years, although I have never been promoted.","note":"hoewel or the very formal ofschoon, both with sub-clause order. Al or ook al do not fit here: they need the verb directly after them (ook al heb ik …)."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Alvorens het contract ___ (tekenen), leest u de voorwaarden.","answers":["te tekenen"],"en":"Before signing the contract, read the conditions.","note":"alvorens takes te + infinitive, without a subject. With a subject you would say: Voordat u het contract tekent, …"},{"type":"order","lead":"Hij","words":["werd","ontslagen","doordat","het bedrijf","failliet ging"],"answers":["Hij werd ontslagen doordat het bedrijf failliet ging."],"en":"He was dismissed because the company went bankrupt.","note":"A bankruptcy is a cause, not anyone's motive, so doordat fits. In the sub-clause the verb ging stands at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Al","words":["verdient","hij","weinig","hij blijft","bij dit bedrijf"],"answers":["Al verdient hij weinig, hij blijft bij dit bedrijf."],"en":"Even though he earns little, he is staying with this company.","note":"After al: verb first, then the subject (al verdient hij). The main clause that follows keeps its normal order."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het contract","words":["wordt","verlengd","mits","u","de training","afrondt"],"answers":["Het contract wordt verlengd, mits u de training afrondt.","Het contract wordt, mits u de training afrondt, verlengd."],"en":"The contract will be extended provided that you complete the training.","note":"mits starts a sub-clause with afrondt at the end. It normally follows the main clause; formal texts sometimes put it between wordt and verlengd."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace omdat by aangezien and start the sentence with it.","from":"De lonen stijgen, omdat er weinig gekwalificeerd personeel is.","answers":["Aangezien er weinig gekwalificeerd personeel is, stijgen de lonen."],"en":"Since there are few qualified staff, wages are rising.","note":"The aangezien-clause takes the first place, so the main clause starts with the verb: stijgen de lonen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with tenzij.","from":"Als u niet binnen vier weken reageert, vervalt uw recht op een uitkering.","answers":["Tenzij u binnen vier weken reageert, vervalt uw recht op een uitkering.","Uw recht op een uitkering vervalt, tenzij u binnen vier weken reageert."],"en":"Unless you respond within four weeks, your right to a benefit lapses.","note":"als … niet becomes tenzij, and niet disappears: tenzij already contains the negation."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it everyday Dutch: replace alvorens by voordat.","from":"Alvorens een nieuwe medewerker in dienst te nemen, controleren wij de referenties.","answers":["Voordat wij een nieuwe medewerker in dienst nemen, controleren wij de referenties.","Wij controleren de referenties voordat wij een nieuwe medewerker in dienst nemen."],"en":"Before we take on a new employee, we check the references.","note":"voordat needs a full sub-clause with a subject and a conjugated verb. The verb nemen goes to the end of the clause, after in dienst."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, tenzij u niet zelf ontslag hebt genomen.","answers":["U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, tenzij u zelf ontslag hebt genomen.","U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, tenzij u zelf ontslag genomen hebt.","U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, als u niet zelf ontslag hebt genomen.","U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, als u niet zelf ontslag genomen hebt.","U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, indien u niet zelf ontslag hebt genomen.","U krijgt een ontslagvergoeding, indien u niet zelf ontslag genomen hebt."],"en":"You will receive severance pay unless you have resigned yourself.","note":"tenzij already means \"if … not\": drop niet. Keeping niet and using als or indien instead says the same."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Doordat ze meer tijd voor haar kinderen wilde, ging ze parttime werken.","answers":["Omdat ze meer tijd voor haar kinderen wilde, ging ze parttime werken.","Aangezien ze meer tijd voor haar kinderen wilde, ging ze parttime werken.","Daar ze meer tijd voor haar kinderen wilde, ging ze parttime werken."],"en":"Because she wanted more time for her children, she started working part-time.","note":"Wanting more time is a motive, a choice she made, so doordat does not fit. Use omdat (or the formal aangezien or daar)."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Al het werk zwaar is, het salaris is goed.","answers":["Al is het werk zwaar, het salaris is goed."],"en":"Even though the work is hard, the pay is good.","note":"al is the one conjunction here that is followed directly by the verb: al is het werk zwaar. The main clause keeps its normal order."}]},
  {"id":"b2-07","level":"B2","icon":"🗣️","title":"Making your case","grammar":"Signal words: echter, bovendien, daarentegen, kortom, namelijk","summary":"Signal words show the link between sentences. Most cause inversion when first (Bovendien is het duur). Echter, namelijk, immers, dan ook prefer the middle; namelijk is never first. Kortom takes a comma. Vandaar dat: verb last.","explain":"In an argued text, an essay or a debate, small words show how one sentence relates to the next: a new point, a contrast, a reason, a conclusion. Dutch calls them signal words (*signaalwoorden*). Choosing the right one is half the work; the other half is putting it in the right place, because most of them affect word order.\n\n### What they do\n\n- **listing**: `ten eerste`, `ten tweede`, `bovendien`, `daarnaast`, `verder`, `ten slotte`\n- **contrast**: `echter`, `daarentegen`, `toch`, `desondanks`, `niettemin`, `integendeel`, `enerzijds … anderzijds`\n- **reason**: `namelijk` (you see: new information), `immers` (after all: the reader knows it), `want`\n- **consequence**: `daarom`, `dan ook`, `vandaar dat`, `dus`, `daardoor`, `hierdoor`, `als gevolg daarvan`\n- **example**: `bijvoorbeeld`, `zo`, `zoals`\n- **conclusion**: `kortom`, `al met al`, `concluderend`, `samenvattend`\n- **concession**: `weliswaar … maar`, `toegegeven`\n\n`daardoor` and `hierdoor` give a cause (as a result of this); `daarom` gives a reason for a decision (that is why).\n\n### 1. First place: the verb follows directly\n\nMost signal words are adverbs. When one stands first, it fills the first place, so the conjugated verb comes next and then the subject.\n\n> Bovendien hebben de tegenstanders hun bewering nooit onderbouwd. — Moreover, the opponents have never substantiated their claim.\n> Enerzijds begrijp ik de voorstanders, anderzijds vind ik hun argumenten zwak. — On the one hand I understand the supporters, on the other I find their arguments weak.\n\nThis goes for the listing words, `toch`, `desondanks`, `niettemin`, `daarom`, `daardoor`, `hierdoor`, `al met al`, `concluderend`, `samenvattend` and `zo` (for example): `Zo is het openbaar vervoer veel duurder geworden.` After `dus` both orders are used: `Dus is het plan te duur` (written) and `Dus het plan is te duur` (spoken).\n\n### 2. Separate, with a comma: normal order\n\n`kortom`, `toegegeven` and `integendeel` stand apart, followed by a comma and a sentence with its own normal order.\n\n> Kortom, de tegenstanders hebben geen enkel overtuigend argument. — In short, the opponents do not have a single convincing argument.\n\n### 3. In the middle\n\n`echter`, `namelijk`, `immers` and `dan ook` usually stand **after the conjugated verb**, in the middle of the sentence. `namelijk` and `dan ook` never start a sentence. At the start `echter` is formal and stands apart with a comma: `Echter, het plan is …`; with inversion (`Echter is …`) it is not accepted by everyone. `echter` and `daarentegen` may also follow a subject in first place: `De tegenstanders daarentegen vinden …`.\n\n> Dat argument is echter niet overtuigend. — That argument, however, is not convincing.\n> Wij zijn tegen dit plan; het is namelijk niet eerlijk tegenover jongeren. — We are against this plan; you see, it is not fair to young people.\n\n`weliswaar` also stands in the middle, and `maar` follows: `Het plan is weliswaar duur, maar het levert veel op.`\n\n### 4. Words with their own clause\n\n`want` joins two main clauses: normal order after it. `vandaar dat` (that is why) is followed by a sub-clause, so the verb goes to the end. `zoals` is followed by a noun or a sub-clause.\n\n> Vandaar dat steeds meer politici dit standpunt steunen. — That is why more and more politicians support this position.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **namelijk is never first**: `Hij is namelijk ziek`, not *Namelijk is hij ziek. To join the reason to the sentence before, use `want`: `…, want hij is ziek`.\n- **Verb after the adverb**: `Bovendien is het duur`, not *Bovendien het is duur.\n- **vandaar dat + verb at the end**: `Vandaar dat het plan is afgewezen`, not *Vandaar dat is het plan afgewezen.\n- **want + normal order**: `want het is duur`, not *want is het duur.\n- **echter is not maar**: it does not join two clauses. `Het is goedkoop, maar het werkt niet`, or `… Het werkt echter niet.`\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `однак`, `крім того` or `тому` can stand first without changing anything in the sentence. In Dutch an adverb in first place always pulls the verb in front of the subject.","words":[{"nl":"standpunt","art":"het","pl":"standpunten","en":"position / point of view","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stelling","art":"de","pl":"stellingen","en":"proposition / statement (to debate)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"betoog","art":"het","pl":"betogen","en":"argument (a reasoned text or speech)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tegenstander","art":"de","pl":"tegenstanders","en":"opponent","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voorstander","art":"de","pl":"voorstanders","en":"supporter / advocate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bewering","art":"de","pl":"beweringen","en":"claim / assertion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tegenargument","art":"het","pl":"tegenargumenten","en":"counter-argument","pos":"n"},{"nl":"conclusie","art":"de","pl":"conclusies","en":"conclusion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"invalshoek","art":"de","pl":"invalshoeken","en":"angle / perspective","pos":"n"},{"nl":"overtuigen","past":"overtuigde","perf":"heeft overtuigd","en":"to convince / to persuade","pos":"v"},{"nl":"weerleggen","past":"weerlegde","perf":"heeft weerlegd","en":"to refute","pos":"v"},{"nl":"beweren","past":"beweerde","perf":"heeft beweerd","en":"to claim / to assert","pos":"v"},{"nl":"benadrukken","past":"benadrukte","perf":"heeft benadrukt","en":"to emphasise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"pleiten voor","past":"pleitte","perf":"heeft gepleit","en":"to argue for / to advocate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"onderbouwen","past":"onderbouwde","perf":"heeft onderbouwd","en":"to substantiate / to back up","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overtuigend","en":"convincing","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ten eerste is het plan te duur, en ten tweede is het niet eerlijk.","en":"Firstly, the plan is too expensive, and secondly, it is not fair.","hint":["ten eerste","plan","duur","ten tweede","eerlijk"],"note":"Ten eerste and ten tweede fill the first place of their clause, so the verb is comes straight after them, before the subject."},{"nl":"Bovendien hebben de tegenstanders hun bewering nooit onderbouwd.","en":"Moreover, the opponents have never substantiated their claim.","hint":["bovendien","tegenstander","bewering","nooit","onderbouwen"],"note":"Bovendien adds a point. In first place it causes inversion: hebben de tegenstanders. It may also stand in the middle.","alt":["De tegenstanders hebben bovendien hun bewering nooit onderbouwd.","De tegenstanders hebben hun bewering bovendien nooit onderbouwd."]},{"nl":"Dat argument is echter niet overtuigend.","en":"That argument, however, is not convincing.","hint":["argument","echter","overtuigend"],"note":"echter (however) prefers the place right after the conjugated verb. Unlike maar it does not join two clauses.","alt":["Echter, dat argument is niet overtuigend.","Echter is dat argument niet overtuigend.","Dat argument echter is niet overtuigend."]},{"nl":"Wij zijn tegen dit plan; het is namelijk niet eerlijk tegenover jongeren.","en":"We are against this plan; you see, it is not fair to young people.","hint":["tegen","plan","namelijk","eerlijk","jongere"],"note":"namelijk explains the sentence before it. It stands after the verb and never at the start: not Namelijk is het …","alt":["Wij zijn tegen dit plan; het is namelijk niet eerlijk voor jongeren.","Wij zijn tegen dit plan, want het is niet eerlijk tegenover jongeren."]},{"nl":"De cijfers kloppen niet; ik ben het dan ook niet eens met deze conclusie.","en":"The figures are not right; I therefore do not agree with this conclusion.","hint":["cijfer","kloppen","dan ook","het eens zijn","conclusie"],"note":"dan ook (therefore, so) draws a consequence and stands in the middle, after the verb; it never starts the sentence.","alt":["De cijfers kloppen niet; ik ben het dan ook niet met deze conclusie eens.","De cijfers kloppen niet; daarom ben ik het niet eens met deze conclusie.","De cijfers kloppen niet; daarom ben ik het niet met deze conclusie eens.","De cijfers kloppen niet; ik ben het daarom niet eens met deze conclusie.","De cijfers kloppen niet; ik ben het daarom niet met deze conclusie eens."]},{"nl":"Vandaar dat steeds meer politici dit standpunt steunen.","en":"That is why more and more politicians support this position.","hint":["vandaar dat","steeds meer","politicus","standpunt","steunen"],"note":"vandaar dat introduces a sub-clause: the verb steunen goes to the end. With daarom you would get inversion: Daarom steunen steeds meer politici …","alt":["Daarom steunen steeds meer politici dit standpunt."]},{"nl":"Het plan is weliswaar duur, maar het levert op lange termijn veel op.","en":"The plan is admittedly expensive, but in the long run it yields a lot.","hint":["plan","weliswaar","duur","maar","op lange termijn","opleveren"],"note":"weliswaar … maar: first you grant a point, then maar brings the real argument. Weliswaar stands in the middle, after is.","alt":["Weliswaar is het plan duur, maar het levert op lange termijn veel op.","Het plan is weliswaar duur, maar op lange termijn levert het veel op.","Weliswaar is het plan duur, maar op lange termijn levert het veel op."]},{"nl":"Enerzijds begrijp ik de voorstanders, anderzijds vind ik hun argumenten niet overtuigend.","en":"On the one hand I understand the supporters, on the other hand I do not find their arguments convincing.","hint":["enerzijds","begrijpen","voorstander","anderzijds","argument","overtuigend"],"note":"enerzijds and anderzijds each fill the first place of their clause, so both are followed by the verb: begrijp ik, vind ik."},{"nl":"Kortom, de tegenstanders hebben geen enkel overtuigend argument.","en":"In short, the opponents do not have a single convincing argument.","hint":["kortom","tegenstander","geen enkel","overtuigend","argument"],"note":"kortom stands apart with a comma, and the sentence after it keeps normal order: de tegenstanders hebben."},{"nl":"Desondanks bleef de spreker bij zijn standpunt.","en":"Despite this, the speaker stuck to his position.","hint":["desondanks","blijven bij","spreker","standpunt"],"note":"desondanks (despite this) is an adverb; in first place it is followed by the verb bleef and then the subject.","alt":["De spreker bleef desondanks bij zijn standpunt.","Ondanks dat bleef de spreker bij zijn standpunt.","Toch bleef de spreker bij zijn standpunt."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De minister beweert dat het plan goedkoop is. ___ zou het veel banen opleveren.","options":["Daarentegen","Bovendien","Kortom","Desondanks"],"correct":1,"en":"The minister claims that the plan is cheap. Moreover, it would create a lot of jobs.","note":"A second point in favour is added: bovendien. Daarentegen and desondanks would announce a contrast, kortom a conclusion."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De voorstanders pleiten voor het plan. De tegenstanders ___ vinden het onrechtvaardig.","options":["bovendien","namelijk","daarentegen","dus"],"correct":2,"en":"The supporters argue for the plan. The opponents, on the other hand, find it unfair.","note":"A contrast between two groups: daarentegen. It is one of the few signal words that may follow a subject in first place."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik stem tegen het voorstel. ___","options":["Namelijk ben ik niet overtuigd.","Ik namelijk ben niet overtuigd.","Ik ben namelijk niet overtuigd."],"correct":2,"en":"I am voting against the proposal. You see, I am not convinced.","note":"namelijk never starts the sentence and does not stand between subject and verb: it comes after the conjugated verb, ben."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het plan gaat niet door, ___ de regering heeft geen geld.","options":["want","daarom","vandaar dat"],"correct":0,"en":"The plan is not going ahead, because the government has no money.","note":"want gives a reason and is followed by normal order: de regering heeft. Daarom would need inversion (daarom heeft de regering), and vandaar dat a sub-clause; both would also turn the meaning round."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (in short), we moeten het probleem vanuit een andere invalshoek bekijken.","answers":["Kortom"],"en":"In short, we have to look at the problem from a different angle.","note":"kortom introduces a conclusion and takes a comma; the sentence after it keeps normal order: we moeten."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zijn betoog was goed opgebouwd. ___ (nevertheless) wist hij de zaal niet te overtuigen.","answers":["Desondanks","Niettemin","Toch"],"en":"His argument was well structured. Nevertheless he did not manage to convince the audience.","note":"A contrast with what came before: desondanks, niettemin or toch. All three are adverbs, so the verb wist follows directly."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij steunen deze stelling; de feiten spreken ___ (after all) voor zich.","answers":["immers"],"en":"We support this proposition; after all, the facts speak for themselves.","note":"immers gives a reason the reader already knows or accepts, and stands after the verb. Namelijk is for a reason that is new to the reader."},{"type":"order","lead":"Bovendien","words":["heeft","de minister","het tegenargument","nooit","weerlegd"],"answers":["Bovendien heeft de minister het tegenargument nooit weerlegd.","Bovendien heeft de minister nooit het tegenargument weerlegd."],"en":"Moreover, the minister has never refuted the counter-argument.","note":"Bovendien takes the first place, so heeft comes second, before the subject. The participle weerlegd closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vandaar dat","words":["de voorstanders","hun betoog","hebben","aangepast"],"answers":["Vandaar dat de voorstanders hun betoog hebben aangepast.","Vandaar dat de voorstanders hun betoog aangepast hebben."],"en":"That is why the supporters have adjusted their argument.","note":"vandaar dat is followed by a sub-clause, so the subject comes first and the verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het voorstel","words":["is","weliswaar","duur","maar","het","levert","veel","op"],"answers":["Het voorstel is weliswaar duur, maar het levert veel op."],"en":"The proposal is admittedly expensive, but it yields a lot.","note":"weliswaar stands after the verb is; maar then joins a main clause with normal order: het levert … op."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace want by namelijk, in a second sentence.","from":"Ik ben tegen het plan, want het is niet eerlijk.","answers":["Ik ben tegen het plan. Het is namelijk niet eerlijk."],"en":"I am against the plan. You see, it is not fair.","note":"namelijk cannot join two clauses as want does, and it cannot start a sentence. It goes after the verb: het is namelijk."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace daarom by vandaar dat.","from":"Daarom stemt de partij tegen het voorstel.","answers":["Vandaar dat de partij tegen het voorstel stemt."],"en":"That is why the party is voting against the proposal.","note":"After daarom the verb comes second; after vandaar dat comes a sub-clause, so stemt moves to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start the sentence with Ten slotte.","from":"Ik wil ten slotte benadrukken dat dit mijn persoonlijke mening is.","answers":["Ten slotte wil ik benadrukken dat dit mijn persoonlijke mening is."],"en":"Finally, I would like to emphasise that this is my personal opinion.","note":"Ten slotte in first place pulls the verb wil in front of the subject ik. The dat-clause keeps its own order."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hij stemt tegen. Namelijk is hij niet overtuigd.","answers":["Hij stemt tegen. Hij is namelijk niet overtuigd.","Hij stemt tegen, want hij is niet overtuigd."],"en":"He is voting against. You see, he is not convinced.","note":"namelijk never starts a sentence. Put it after the verb (hij is namelijk), or use want."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Bovendien de voorstanders hebben geen tegenargument.","answers":["Bovendien hebben de voorstanders geen tegenargument.","De voorstanders hebben bovendien geen tegenargument."],"en":"Moreover, the supporters have no counter-argument.","note":"Bovendien in first place is followed by the verb, then the subject: bovendien hebben de voorstanders."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Vandaar dat is het plan afgewezen.","answers":["Vandaar dat het plan is afgewezen.","Vandaar dat het plan afgewezen is."],"en":"That is why the plan has been rejected.","note":"vandaar dat starts a sub-clause, not a main clause: subject first, verb at the end."}]},
  {"id":"b2-08","level":"B2","icon":"🔬","title":"Science and research","grammar":"Relative clauses: wat, wie, wiens, waarvan, hetgeen","summary":"wat after alles, iets, het enige (dat is possible too) and for a whole clause (formal: hetgeen). Wie … = whoever. Whose: van wie (formal wiens, wier) for people, waarvan for things. Preposition + wie for people. The verb goes last.","explain":"You know `die` and `dat` (unit B1 · 03) and `waarmee`, `waarover` (B1 · 04). Academic and formal texts need more: `wat` after words like `alles`, `wat` for a whole clause, `wie` without a noun, and the forms for \"whose\". In every relative clause the conjugated verb goes to the end.\n\n### 1. wat after alles, niets, iets, het enige …\n\nAfter `alles` and `veel` the usual relative pronoun is `wat`; `dat` is also correct but much less common (`alles dat`). After `iets`, `niets`, `het enige`, `het eerste`, `het laatste` and a superlative used as a noun (`het beste`, `het mooiste`), `wat` is the usual form and `dat` is just as correct.\n\n> Alles wat de proefpersonen zeiden, werd opgenomen. — Everything the test subjects said was recorded.\n> Het enige wat nog ontbreekt, is een betrouwbare steekproef. — The only thing still missing is a reliable sample.\n\nWhen a noun follows the superlative, the noun decides: `de beste publicatie die ik ken`, `het beste artikel dat ik ken`.\n\n### 2. wat for a whole clause\n\nWhen the relative clause comments on the **whole** previous clause, not on one noun, use `wat`; `dat` or `die` is impossible. `hetgeen` is the formal variant.\n\n> De uitkomst was negatief, wat de onderzoekers verraste. — The outcome was negative, which surprised the researchers.\n\n### 3. wie and wat without a noun\n\n`Wie …` means \"whoever, anyone who\"; `Wat …` means \"what, whatever\". The clause is the first element of the sentence, so the main verb follows it directly.\n\n> Wie aan het onderzoek meedoet, krijgt een kleine vergoeding. — Whoever takes part in the study receives a small fee.\n> Wat de hoogleraar beweert, is nooit aangetoond. — What the professor claims has never been demonstrated.\n\n### 4. whose\n\n| owner | normal | formal |\n| a man, a boy | van wie het artikel | wiens artikel |\n| a woman, or plural | van wie het artikel | wier artikel |\n| a thing | waarvan het artikel | — |\n\n`wiens` and `wier` are chosen by the **owner**, not by the thing owned, and take no article. `van wie` and `waarvan` are followed by the article: `het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten …`.\n\n> Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten zijn gepubliceerd, krijgt veel kritiek. — The study whose results have been published is getting a lot of criticism.\n\n### 5. With a preposition\n\nFor things: `waar` + preposition (`de methode waarmee`, `het onderwerp waarover`). For people: preposition + `wie`: `de hoogleraar met wie ik samenwerk`. `waarmee` for a person is common in speech but avoided in writing. Some prepositions cannot join `waar`, such as `gedurende` and `tijdens`; very formal texts then use `welke`: `de periode gedurende welke …`.\n\n### 6. The comma changes the meaning\n\n> De proefpersonen die vrijwilligers waren, kregen een vergoeding. — The test subjects who were volunteers received a fee (only those).\n> De proefpersonen, die allemaal vrijwilligers waren, kregen een vergoeding. — The test subjects, who were all volunteers, received a fee (all of them).\n\nWithout a comma before `die`, the clause selects; with commas round it, it only adds information. A comma after the clause, between two verbs, is usual in both.\n\n### 7. Long sentences\n\nThe relative clause usually follows its noun directly. When the main clause ends in a participle or infinitive, the relative clause may also come after it; both are correct, and with a long clause the second is easier to read: `Ik heb het artikel dat zij schreef gelezen` / `Ik heb het artikel gelezen dat zij schreef`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **Whole clause = wat**: `…, wat niemand verbaasde`, not *…, dat niemand verbaasde.\n- **Noun after the superlative**: `de beste publicatie die …`, not *de beste publicatie wat.\n- **No article after wiens or wier**: `wiens proefschrift`, not *wiens het proefschrift.\n- **Verb at the end**, also after `waarvan`: `waarvan de resultaten betrouwbaar zijn`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `чий`, `чия`, `чиє` agree with the thing owned (`чия книга`). Dutch `wiens` and `wier` depend on the owner: `de onderzoeker wiens studie`, `de onderzoekster wier studie`.","words":[{"nl":"onderzoeker","art":"de","pl":"onderzoekers","en":"researcher","pos":"n"},{"nl":"proefpersoon","art":"de","pl":"proefpersonen","en":"test subject / participant","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bevinding","art":"de","pl":"bevindingen","en":"finding","pos":"n"},{"nl":"steekproef","art":"de","pl":"steekproeven","en":"sample (in research)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitkomst","art":"de","pl":"uitkomsten","en":"outcome / result","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vakgebied","art":"het","pl":"vakgebieden","en":"field (of study)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"promovendus","art":"de","pl":"promovendi","en":"PhD candidate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"proefschrift","art":"het","pl":"proefschriften","en":"doctoral thesis","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoogleraar","art":"de","pl":"hoogleraren","en":"professor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"publicatie","art":"de","pl":"publicaties","en":"publication","pos":"n"},{"nl":"subsidie","art":"de","pl":"subsidies","en":"grant / subsidy","pos":"n"},{"nl":"promoveren","past":"promoveerde","perf":"is gepromoveerd","en":"to obtain a doctorate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aantonen","past":"toonde aan","perf":"heeft aangetoond","en":"to demonstrate / to prove","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verrichten","past":"verrichtte","perf":"heeft verricht","en":"to carry out (research, work)","pos":"v","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"betrouwbaar","en":"reliable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"wetenschappelijk","en":"scientific / academic","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Alles wat de proefpersonen zeiden, werd opgenomen.","en":"Everything the test subjects said was recorded.","hint":["alles","wat","proefpersoon","zeggen","opnemen"],"note":"After alles the usual relative pronoun is wat (alles dat is also correct, but less common). English can leave it out (\"everything they said\"); Dutch cannot.","alt":["Alles wat de proefpersonen zeiden, is opgenomen.","Alles dat de proefpersonen zeiden, werd opgenomen.","Alles dat de proefpersonen zeiden, is opgenomen."]},{"nl":"Het enige wat nog ontbreekt, is een betrouwbare steekproef.","en":"The only thing still missing is a reliable sample.","hint":["het enige","ontbreken","betrouwbaar","steekproef"],"note":"After het enige, wat is the usual form; het enige dat is also correct. Ontbreekt stands at the end of the relative clause.","alt":["Het enige dat nog ontbreekt, is een betrouwbare steekproef."]},{"nl":"De uitkomst was negatief, wat de onderzoekers verraste.","en":"The outcome was negative, which surprised the researchers.","hint":["uitkomst","negatief","wat","onderzoeker","verrassen"],"note":"wat refers to the whole clause (that the outcome was negative), not to de uitkomst, so die is impossible. Formal: hetgeen.","alt":["De uitkomst was negatief, hetgeen de onderzoekers verraste."]},{"nl":"Wie aan het onderzoek meedoet, krijgt een kleine vergoeding.","en":"Whoever takes part in the study receives a small fee.","hint":["wie","onderzoek","meedoen","vergoeding"],"note":"wie without a noun before it means \"whoever\". The whole wie-clause is the first element, so krijgt follows directly.","alt":["Wie meedoet aan het onderzoek, krijgt een kleine vergoeding."]},{"nl":"Wat de hoogleraar beweert, is nooit aangetoond.","en":"What the professor claims has never been demonstrated.","hint":["wat","hoogleraar","beweren","nooit","aantonen"],"note":"wat without a noun: \"what, the thing that\". It makes a sub-clause (beweert at the end) that is the subject of is aangetoond."},{"nl":"De onderzoeker van wie het artikel gisteren verscheen, geeft morgen een lezing.","en":"The researcher whose article appeared yesterday is giving a lecture tomorrow.","hint":["onderzoeker","van wie","artikel","verschijnen","lezing"],"note":"van wie is the normal \"whose\" for a person, followed by the article: van wie het artikel. Formal: wiens artikel, without het.","alt":["De onderzoeker wiens artikel gisteren verscheen, geeft morgen een lezing.","De onderzoeker van wie het artikel gisteren is verschenen, geeft morgen een lezing.","De onderzoeker van wie het artikel gisteren verschenen is, geeft morgen een lezing.","De onderzoeker wiens artikel gisteren is verschenen, geeft morgen een lezing.","De onderzoeker wiens artikel gisteren verschenen is, geeft morgen een lezing."]},{"nl":"Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten vorige week in een wetenschappelijk tijdschrift zijn gepubliceerd, krijgt veel kritiek.","en":"The study whose results were published in a scientific journal last week is getting a lot of criticism.","hint":["onderzoek","waarvan","resultaat","wetenschappelijk","publiceren","kritiek"],"note":"For a thing, \"whose\" is waarvan, followed by the article: waarvan de resultaten. The verbs go to the end of the relative clause.","alt":["Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten vorige week in een wetenschappelijk tijdschrift gepubliceerd zijn, krijgt veel kritiek.","Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten vorige week in een wetenschappelijk tijdschrift werden gepubliceerd, krijgt veel kritiek.","Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten vorige week in een wetenschappelijk tijdschrift gepubliceerd werden, krijgt veel kritiek."]},{"nl":"De hoogleraar met wie ik samenwerk, is een expert in haar vakgebied.","en":"The professor I work with is an expert in her field.","hint":["hoogleraar","met wie","samenwerken","expert","vakgebied"],"note":"For a person: preposition + wie (met wie), not waarmee in careful Dutch. The preposition stands in front, never at the end as in English."},{"nl":"De proefpersonen, die allemaal vrijwilligers waren, kregen een vergoeding.","en":"The test subjects, who were all volunteers, received a fee.","hint":["proefpersoon","allemaal","vrijwilliger","krijgen","vergoeding"],"note":"The commas round the clause mean it only adds information about all the test subjects; without them it would pick out some of them."},{"nl":"Ik heb het artikel gelezen dat zij over dit experiment heeft geschreven.","en":"I have read the article that she has written about this experiment.","hint":["lezen","artikel","experiment","schrijven"],"note":"The relative clause follows the participle gelezen here, which keeps the main clause short. Directly after het artikel is also correct.","alt":["Ik heb het artikel gelezen dat zij over dit experiment geschreven heeft.","Ik heb het artikel dat zij over dit experiment heeft geschreven gelezen.","Ik heb het artikel dat zij over dit experiment geschreven heeft gelezen.","Ik heb het artikel gelezen dat ze over dit experiment heeft geschreven.","Ik heb het artikel gelezen dat ze over dit experiment geschreven heeft."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De steekproef was te klein, ___ de uitkomst onbetrouwbaar maakt.","options":["dat","die","wat","waarvan"],"correct":2,"en":"The sample was too small, which makes the outcome unreliable.","note":"The clause comments on the whole fact that the sample was too small, so only wat (or formal hetgeen) fits. Die would refer to de steekproef alone."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De onderzoekster ___ artikel veel aandacht kreeg, is pas dertig.","options":["wiens","wier","wie","die"],"correct":1,"en":"The (female) researcher whose article got a lot of attention is only thirty.","note":"The owner is a woman, so the formal \"whose\" is wier. Wiens is for a male owner; the gender of artikel plays no part."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het experiment ___ de uitkomsten ons verbaasden, wordt herhaald.","options":["van wie","wiens","waarvan","dat"],"correct":2,"en":"The experiment whose outcomes surprised us is being repeated.","note":"An experiment is a thing, so \"whose\" is waarvan. Van wie and wiens are for people."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___","options":["De studenten, die waren geslaagd, kregen een certificaat.","De studenten die waren geslaagd, kregen een certificaat.","De studenten wat waren geslaagd, kregen een certificaat."],"correct":1,"en":"Only those students who had passed received a certificate.","note":"The clause selects some students, so there is no comma before die. With commas it would mean that all the students had passed. Wat cannot refer to a noun like studenten."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De collega ___ ik het experiment heb verricht, is vandaag ziek.","options":["met wie","met die","met wat","waar"],"correct":0,"en":"The colleague with whom I carried out the experiment is ill today. (written Dutch)","note":"For a person, written Dutch uses preposition + wie: met wie. Die and wat cannot follow a preposition. Waarmee is common in speech but avoided in writing."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het eerste ___ opvalt, is de kleine steekproef.","answers":["wat","dat"],"en":"The first thing that stands out is the small sample.","note":"After het eerste, wat is the usual form and dat is also correct. There is no noun after het eerste, so it does not choose die or dat by itself."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (whoever) subsidie aanvraagt, moet een duidelijk onderzoeksplan indienen.","answers":["Wie"],"en":"Whoever applies for a grant must submit a clear research plan.","note":"wie without a noun = whoever. The wie-clause is the first element, so moet follows it directly."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De periode ___ (during which) het experiment werd verricht, duurde drie maanden.","answers":["gedurende welke","tijdens welke","waarin"],"en":"The period during which the experiment was carried out lasted three months.","note":"gedurende and tijdens cannot join waar-, so formal Dutch uses welke: gedurende welke, tijdens welke. Waarin (in which) says the same in an everyday way."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het was de beste publicatie ___ ik dit jaar heb gelezen.","answers":["die"],"en":"It was the best publication I have read this year.","note":"Here a noun follows the superlative: de publicatie, a de-word, so die. Wat is only for a superlative without a noun: het beste wat ik heb gelezen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","het artikel","gelezen","dat","zij","heeft geschreven"],"answers":["Ik heb het artikel gelezen dat zij heeft geschreven.","Ik heb het artikel dat zij heeft geschreven gelezen."],"en":"I have read the article that she has written.","note":"The relative clause may follow het artikel directly or come after the participle gelezen. Both are correct; the second keeps the main clause together."},{"type":"order","lead":"Wie","words":["het proefschrift","leest","begrijpt","de bevindingen","beter"],"answers":["Wie het proefschrift leest, begrijpt de bevindingen beter."],"en":"Whoever reads the thesis understands the findings better.","note":"In the wie-clause the verb leest goes to the end. The whole clause is the first element, so begrijpt comes right after it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with waarvan.","from":"Wij herhalen het onderzoek. De uitkomsten ervan waren onbetrouwbaar.","answers":["Wij herhalen het onderzoek waarvan de uitkomsten onbetrouwbaar waren."],"en":"We are repeating the study whose outcomes were unreliable.","note":"de uitkomsten ervan (its outcomes) becomes waarvan de uitkomsten. Waren moves to the end of the relative clause."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal: replace van wie by wier.","from":"De studenten van wie de scripties te laat waren, kregen een lager cijfer.","answers":["De studenten wier scripties te laat waren, kregen een lager cijfer."],"en":"The students whose theses were late got a lower mark.","note":"The owners are plural, so wier. After wier the article de disappears."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De meting werd twee keer herhaald, dat veel geld kostte.","answers":["De meting werd twee keer herhaald, wat veel geld kostte.","De meting werd twee keer herhaald, hetgeen veel geld kostte."],"en":"The measurement was repeated twice, which cost a lot of money.","note":"What cost money is the repeating, the whole clause, not de meting (and dat could not refer to a de-word anyway). A clause is referred to with wat (formal: hetgeen)."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten zijn betrouwbaar, wordt gepubliceerd.","answers":["Het onderzoek waarvan de resultaten betrouwbaar zijn, wordt gepubliceerd."],"en":"The study whose results are reliable is being published.","note":"A relative clause is a sub-clause: the conjugated verb zijn goes to the end, also after waarvan."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De promovendus wiens het proefschrift werd goedgekeurd, promoveert volgende maand.","answers":["De promovendus wiens proefschrift werd goedgekeurd, promoveert volgende maand.","De promovendus van wie het proefschrift werd goedgekeurd, promoveert volgende maand."],"en":"The PhD candidate whose thesis was approved will obtain his doctorate next month.","note":"wiens already means \"whose\" and takes no article. Keep het only with van wie: van wie het proefschrift."}]},
  {"id":"b2-09","level":"B2","icon":"🎙️","title":"Media and journalism","grammar":"Reported speech: tenses, zou, and what is only claimed","summary":"After a past verb of saying the tense moves back (ik ben → hij was; ik heb → hij had) and the future becomes zou. zou also marks a claim that is not confirmed; volgens is followed by the verb.","explain":"You can quote someone's words (direct speech, *de directe rede*) or build them into a sub-clause with `dat`, `of` or a question word (indirect speech, *de indirecte rede*). In that sub-clause the verbs go to the end (unit B1 · 02). Journalists also have their own tools to show that something is only a claim.\n\n### Tenses after a past verb of saying\n\nWhen the verb of saying is in the present (`Hij zegt dat …`), the tense of the words stays as it was. When it is in the past (`zei`, `vertelde`, `meldde`), the tense usually moves one step back:\n\n| said | reported after zei dat … |\n| ik ben ziek | hij ziek **was** |\n| ik heb gebeld | hij **had** gebeld |\n| ik belde | hij **had** gebeld |\n| ik zal komen / ik kom morgen | hij **zou** komen |\n\n> De woordvoerder zei dat de minister ziek was. — The spokesperson said that the minister was ill.\n> De verslaggever vertelde dat hij drie keer had gebeld. — The reporter said that he had called three times.\n> Hij zei dat hij naar de persconferentie zou komen. — He said that he would come to the press conference.\n\nAt the end of the clause both orders of the two verbs are standard Dutch: `dat hij had gebeld` and `dat hij gebeld had`. With `zou` + infinitive, `zou komen` is the usual order; `komen zou` also occurs.\n\nThe present stays when the statement is still true or is a general fact. Dutch is less strict than English here, and in speech the present is heard more widely; in careful writing, move the tense back.\n\n> Hij zei dat Amsterdam de hoofdstad is. — He said that Amsterdam is the capital.\n\n### Person, time and place\n\nPersons move to the reporter's point of view (`ik` → `hij`/`zij`, `jij` → `ik`), and time and place words change when that moment or place lies behind you:\n\n- `vandaag` → `die dag`; `nu` → `toen`, `op dat moment`\n- `morgen` → `de volgende dag`, `de dag erna`; `gisteren` → `de dag ervoor`\n- `volgende week` → `de week erna`; `hier` → `daar`\n\n> Ze zei dat ze me de volgende dag zou bellen. — She said that she would call me the next day.\n\n### Questions and orders\n\nA yes/no question is reported with `of`; a question word stays. An order becomes `dat … moest`, or `vragen` + `(om) te` + infinitive.\n\n> De verslaggever vroeg of de minister zou aftreden. — The reporter asked whether the minister would resign.\n> De redactie zei dat ik moest wachten. — The editors said that I had to wait.\n> De redactie vroeg me te wachten. — The editors asked me to wait.\n\n### zou: only claimed\n\nIn the news, `zou` / `zouden` + infinitive also marks something as **unconfirmed**: the journalist passes it on without vouching for it (English *is said to*, *allegedly*). For the past: `zou hebben` / `zou zijn` + participle. So `De minister zou aftreden` is either \"was going to resign\" or \"is said to be resigning\": context decides.\n\n> Er zouden tien gewonden zijn. — There are said to be ten injured.\n> De burgemeester zou van de fraude hebben geweten. — The mayor allegedly knew about the fraud.\n\nOther ways to mark a claim:\n\n- `volgens` + source, often first, with the verb straight after it.\n- `naar verluidt` (reportedly), `naar eigen zeggen` (by his or her own account).\n- `beweren dat`, `stellen dat`.\n- `aldus` + source after a quotation or a summary of one, with no verb.\n- `schijnen te` (is said to): `Hij schijnt ziek te zijn` (more in B2 · 14).\n\n> Volgens de politie is de dader gevlucht. — According to the police the offender has fled.\n> De brand is onder controle, aldus de brandweer. — The fire is under control, according to the fire brigade.\n\n### Verbs of saying\n\n- `zeggen`, `vertellen`: neutral. `melden`: report news. `stellen`: state firmly.\n- `beweren`: claim; it hints that the claim may be untrue.\n- `benadrukken`: stress. `toegeven`: admit. `ontkennen`: deny.\n- `suggereren`: imply without saying it outright. To propose a plan is `voorstellen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **Inversion after volgens**: `Volgens de politie is de man gevlucht`, not *Volgens de politie de man is gevlucht.\n- **of** for a yes/no question: `Hij vroeg of ik kwam`, not *Hij vroeg dat ik kwam or *als ik kwam.\n- **ontkennen already says no**: `Hij ontkende dat hij had gelogen` means he said he had not lied. A `niet` in the clause turns the meaning around.\n- **aldus + source, no clause**: `…, aldus de woordvoerder`, not *…, aldus de woordvoerder zei.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian keeps the tense of the original words: `Він сказав, що він хворий`. Dutch moves it back: `Hij zei dat hij ziek was`. The Ukrainian `чи` in a reported question is Dutch `of`.","words":[{"nl":"verslaggever","art":"de","pl":"verslaggevers","en":"reporter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"woordvoerder","art":"de","pl":"woordvoerders","en":"spokesperson","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bron","art":"de","pl":"bronnen","en":"source (of information)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"persbericht","art":"het","pl":"persberichten","en":"press release","pos":"n"},{"nl":"persconferentie","art":"de","pl":"persconferenties","en":"press conference","pos":"n"},{"nl":"redactie","art":"de","pl":"redacties","en":"editorial staff / newsroom","pos":"n"},{"nl":"persvoorlichter","art":"de","pl":"persvoorlichters","en":"press officer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verklaring","art":"de","pl":"verklaringen","en":"statement (official)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoofdredacteur","art":"de","pl":"hoofdredacteuren","en":"editor-in-chief","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ontkennen","past":"ontkende","perf":"heeft ontkend","en":"to deny","pos":"v"},{"nl":"toegeven","past":"gaf toe","perf":"heeft toegegeven","en":"to admit","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"citaat","art":"het","pl":"citaten","en":"quotation / quote","pos":"n"},{"nl":"suggereren","past":"suggereerde","perf":"heeft gesuggereerd","en":"to imply / to hint","pos":"v"},{"nl":"anoniem","en":"anonymous","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"naar verluidt","en":"reportedly","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"aldus","en":"according to (after a quotation)","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Tijdens de persconferentie benadrukte de woordvoerder dat het onderzoek nog niet klaar was.","en":"During the press conference the spokesperson stressed that the investigation was not finished yet.","hint":["persconferentie","benadrukken","woordvoerder","onderzoek","klaar"],"note":"The words were 'Het onderzoek is nog niet klaar'; after the past verb benadrukte the present is moves back to was, at the end of the dat-clause.","alt":["De woordvoerder benadrukte tijdens de persconferentie dat het onderzoek nog niet klaar was."]},{"nl":"De verslaggever vertelde ons dat hij de minister al drie keer had gebeld.","en":"The reporter told us that he had already called the minister three times.","hint":["verslaggever","vertellen","minister","drie keer","bellen"],"note":"His words were 'Ik heb al drie keer gebeld': the perfect becomes the past perfect, had gebeld. Gebeld had is just as correct.","alt":["De verslaggever vertelde ons dat hij de minister al drie keer gebeld had."]},{"nl":"De hoofdredacteur zei dat een goede journalist altijd twee bronnen controleert.","en":"The editor-in-chief said that a good journalist always checks two sources.","hint":["hoofdredacteur","zeggen","journalist","bron","controleren"],"note":"A general rule that is still true may keep the present after zei: controleert, as in the English. Controleerde would also be correct, but it says checked.","alt":["De hoofdredacteur zei dat een goed journalist altijd twee bronnen controleert."]},{"nl":"De minister beloofde dat hij de volgende dag zou reageren.","en":"The minister promised that he would respond the next day.","hint":["minister","beloven","volgende","dag","reageren"],"note":"He said 'Ik reageer morgen'. A future becomes zou + infinitive, and morgen becomes de volgende dag because that day has passed.","alt":["De minister beloofde dat hij de volgende dag reageren zou.","De minister beloofde dat hij de dag erna zou reageren.","De minister beloofde dat hij de dag erna reageren zou.","De minister beloofde dat hij de dag daarna zou reageren.","De minister beloofde dat hij de dag daarna reageren zou."]},{"nl":"Bij de explosie zouden tien mensen gewond zijn geraakt.","en":"Ten people are said to have been injured in the explosion.","hint":["explosie","zullen","mens","gewond raken"],"note":"zouden marks a report that is not confirmed. For something in the past it is zou(den) + zijn/hebben + participle: zouden … gewond zijn geraakt.","alt":["Bij de explosie zouden tien mensen gewond geraakt zijn.","Tien mensen zouden bij de explosie gewond zijn geraakt.","Tien mensen zouden bij de explosie gewond geraakt zijn.","Bij de explosie zouden er tien mensen gewond zijn geraakt.","Bij de explosie zouden er tien mensen gewond geraakt zijn.","Er zouden bij de explosie tien mensen gewond zijn geraakt.","Er zouden bij de explosie tien mensen gewond geraakt zijn.","Tien mensen zouden gewond zijn geraakt bij de explosie.","Tien mensen zouden gewond geraakt zijn bij de explosie."]},{"nl":"Volgens een anonieme bron wist de redactie al maanden van de fraude.","en":"According to an anonymous source the editorial staff had known about the fraud for months.","hint":["volgens","anoniem","bron","redactie","weten","fraude"],"note":"Volgens een anonieme bron is the first element, so the verb wist comes straight after it, before the subject de redactie. Al maanden + past: had known for months.","alt":["De redactie wist volgens een anonieme bron al maanden van de fraude.","Volgens een anonieme bron had de redactie al maanden van de fraude geweten.","De redactie had volgens een anonieme bron al maanden van de fraude geweten."]},{"nl":"De verslaggever had de bron naar eigen zeggen nooit ontmoet.","en":"By his own account, the reporter had never met the source.","hint":["verslaggever","bron","naar eigen zeggen","nooit","ontmoeten"],"note":"naar eigen zeggen says that only he himself claims this. It stands in the middle like an adverb, or first: Naar eigen zeggen had de verslaggever …","alt":["Naar eigen zeggen had de verslaggever de bron nooit ontmoet.","De verslaggever had naar eigen zeggen de bron nooit ontmoet."]},{"nl":"Ze vroeg de woordvoerder of de minister zou aftreden.","en":"She asked the spokesperson whether the minister would resign.","hint":["vragen","woordvoerder","minister","aftreden"],"note":"A yes/no question is reported with of. The question was about the future, so it becomes zou + infinitive at the end of the clause.","alt":["Ze vroeg de woordvoerder of de minister af zou treden.","Ze vroeg de woordvoerder of de minister aftreden zou.","Ze vroeg aan de woordvoerder of de minister zou aftreden.","Ze vroeg aan de woordvoerder of de minister af zou treden.","Ze vroeg aan de woordvoerder of de minister aftreden zou."]},{"nl":"De redactie zei dat ik het artikel moest inkorten.","en":"The editors said that I had to shorten the article.","hint":["redactie","zeggen","artikel","moeten","inkorten"],"note":"The order 'Kort het artikel in!' is reported as dat … moest + infinitive. In the clause the separable verb may stay whole (moest inkorten) or split (in moest korten).","alt":["De redactie zei dat ik het artikel in moest korten.","De redactie zei dat ik het artikel inkorten moest."]},{"nl":"De minister gaf toe dat hij met de verslaggever had gesproken.","en":"The minister admitted that he had spoken to the reporter.","hint":["minister","toegeven","verslaggever","spreken"],"note":"toegeven: he admits something he would rather not say. In the main clause the separable verb splits: gaf … toe; the perfect in the clause becomes had gesproken.","alt":["De minister gaf toe dat hij met de verslaggever gesproken had.","De minister gaf toe dat hij de verslaggever had gesproken.","De minister gaf toe dat hij de verslaggever gesproken had."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Volgens de woordvoerder ___ geen sprake van fraude.","options":["er is","is er","er"],"correct":1,"en":"According to the spokesperson there is no question of fraud.","note":"Volgens de woordvoerder is the first element, so the verb comes next: is er. *Volgens de woordvoerder er is breaks the verb-second rule."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De verslaggever vroeg ___ de minister het persbericht had gelezen.","options":["dat","als","of","wat"],"correct":2,"en":"The reporter asked whether the minister had read the press release.","note":"A reported yes/no question starts with of. Dat would make it a statement, and als for 'whether' is not standard Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Bij de brand ___ drie mensen gewond zijn geraakt, maar de politie heeft dat nog niet bevestigd.","options":["zou","werden","zouden"],"correct":2,"en":"Three people are said to have been injured in the fire, but the police have not confirmed that yet.","note":"zou(den) marks an unconfirmed report. The subject drie mensen is plural, so zouden; werden does not go with zijn geraakt."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De krant ___ dat de minister iets te verbergen had, maar schreef het niet letterlijk.","options":["benadrukte","suggereerde","gaf toe"],"correct":1,"en":"The newspaper implied that the minister had something to hide, but did not write it in so many words.","note":"suggereren is saying something without saying it outright. Benadrukken (to stress) does not fit with 'niet letterlijk', and a newspaper cannot admit something on the minister's behalf."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vorige week beweerde de politicus dat hij ziek ___ (zijn), maar die avond stond hij gewoon op een feest.","answers":["was"],"en":"Last week the politician claimed that he was ill, but that evening he was simply at a party.","note":"After the past verb beweerde, 'ik ben ziek' becomes was. Beweren also shows that the claim was doubtful."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op maandag zei de woordvoerder dat de minister de volgende dag een verklaring ___ (zullen) afleggen, maar dat is nooit gebeurd.","answers":["zou"],"en":"On Monday the spokesperson said that the minister would make a statement the next day, but that never happened.","note":"A future reported after a past verb becomes zou + infinitive. Zal belongs to the moment of speaking, which lies in the past."},{"type":"gap","nl":"'Ik bel je morgen,' zei de bron vorige week. Hij zei dat hij me ___ zou bellen.","answers":["de volgende dag","de dag erna","de dag daarna","de dag erop"],"en":"He said that he would call me the next day.","note":"Morgen was seen from last week, so it becomes de volgende dag (or de dag erna). Morgen now would mean a different day."},{"type":"gap","nl":"'Ik heb de bron gisteren gesproken.' Ze vertelde dat ze de bron de dag ervoor ___ (hebben) gesproken.","answers":["had"],"en":"She said that she had spoken to the source the day before.","note":"The perfect heb gesproken becomes the past perfect had gesproken after vertelde, and gisteren becomes de dag ervoor."},{"type":"order","lead":"Volgens de politie","words":["zijn","er","bij de brand","twee mensen","gewond geraakt"],"answers":["Volgens de politie zijn er bij de brand twee mensen gewond geraakt.","Volgens de politie zijn er twee mensen bij de brand gewond geraakt.","Volgens de politie zijn er twee mensen gewond geraakt bij de brand."],"en":"According to the police two people were injured in the fire.","note":"After Volgens de politie comes the verb zijn, then er. Bij de brand may stand before or after twee mensen, or after the participle."},{"type":"order","lead":"De verslaggever vroeg","words":["of","de woordvoerder","het nieuws","kon","bevestigen"],"answers":["De verslaggever vroeg of de woordvoerder het nieuws kon bevestigen.","De verslaggever vroeg of de woordvoerder het nieuws bevestigen kon."],"en":"The reporter asked whether the spokesperson could confirm the news.","note":"of starts the reported question and the verbs go to the end. Kon bevestigen is the usual order; bevestigen kon is also correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"The spokesperson (he) said this. Report it with ontkennen: start with De woordvoerder ontkende dat.","from":"Ik heb het persbericht niet geschreven.","answers":["De woordvoerder ontkende dat hij het persbericht had geschreven.","De woordvoerder ontkende dat hij het persbericht geschreven had."],"en":"The spokesperson denied that he had written the press release.","note":"ontkennen already contains the 'not', so niet goes. With niet the sentence would mean that he claims he did write it. Heb geschreven becomes had geschreven."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"A journalist asked the spokesperson (he) this. Report it: start with De journalist vroeg de woordvoerder.","from":"Waarom heeft u de bron niet genoemd?","answers":["De journalist vroeg de woordvoerder waarom hij de bron niet had genoemd.","De journalist vroeg de woordvoerder waarom hij de bron niet genoemd had."],"en":"The journalist asked the spokesperson why he had not named the source.","note":"The question word waarom stays and starts the clause; u becomes hij, and heeft genoemd becomes had genoemd at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"The press officer said this to us. Report it later, somewhere else: start with De persvoorlichter vroeg ons, and use te.","from":"Wacht hier, alstublieft.","answers":["De persvoorlichter vroeg ons daar te wachten.","De persvoorlichter vroeg ons om daar te wachten."],"en":"The press officer asked us to wait there.","note":"A request becomes vragen + (om) te + infinitive. We are no longer in that place, so hier becomes daar."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with zou instead of naar verluidt. Start with De minister.","from":"Naar verluidt heeft de minister gelogen.","answers":["De minister zou hebben gelogen.","De minister zou gelogen hebben."],"en":"The minister allegedly lied.","note":"For an unconfirmed claim about the past use zou + hebben + participle. Both orders of hebben and gelogen are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Volgens de redactie het citaat klopt niet.","answers":["Volgens de redactie klopt het citaat niet."],"en":"According to the editors the quotation is not correct.","note":"Volgens de redactie fills the first place, so the verb klopt must come second, before the subject het citaat."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het onderzoek is afgerond, aldus de woordvoerder zei.","answers":["Het onderzoek is afgerond, aldus de woordvoerder.","Het onderzoek is afgerond, zei de woordvoerder.","Het onderzoek is afgerond, zoals de woordvoerder zei."],"en":"The investigation has been completed, according to the spokesperson.","note":"aldus is followed only by the source, with no verb and no sub-clause: aldus de woordvoerder. With a verb it is zei de woordvoerder, or a clause with zoals."}]},
  {"id":"b2-10","level":"B2","icon":"📉","title":"Figures and trends","grammar":"Er: the complete picture","summary":"er: a place (daar when stressed), an indefinite subject (Er is een daling), a number (Ik heb er twee), with a preposition (erop, daar … op), a passive (Er wordt gezegd). No er with a definite subject.","explain":"`er` does five different jobs in Dutch. You have met them one at a time (A2 · 26, B1 · 05, B1 · 06, B1 · 11); this unit puts them side by side and shows when `er` stays and when it goes. Reports on figures and trends need almost all of them.\n\n### The five jobs\n\n| job | example | stressed or first |\n| 1 place | Ik woon er al jaren. | daar |\n| 2 indefinite subject | Er zijn drie redenen. | er first |\n| 3 number or amount | Ik heb er twee. | never first |\n| 4 with a preposition | Ik reken erop. | daar … op |\n| 5 passive without subject | Er wordt gestaakt. | er first |\n\n- **Place**: unstressed 'there', for a place already mentioned. Stressed or first it becomes `daar`: `Daar woon ik al jaren`.\n- **Indefinite subject**: a subject with `een`, `geen`, a number, `veel`, `iemand`, `iets` usually moves behind the verb, and `er` holds the first place.\n- **Number or amount**: when a number, `veel`, `weinig`, `een paar` or `geen` stands without its noun, `er` stands in for the noun. It is never stressed and never first.\n- **Preposition**: `er` + preposition replaces a thing (not a person). `met` becomes `mee`, `tot` becomes `toe`. Stressed: `daar`; in a question: `waar`.\n- **Passive**: a passive without a subject (B1 · 11).\n\n> Er is een groot verschil tussen de twee groepen. — There is a big difference between the two groups.\n> De tabel noemt tien landen, maar ik ken er maar drie. — The table lists ten countries, but I only know three of them.\n> Daar ben ik het niet mee eens. — I do not agree with that.\n> Waar is dit percentage op gebaseerd? — What is this percentage based on?\n> Er wordt veel over de cijfers gediscussieerd. — There is a lot of discussion about the figures.\n\n### When er goes and when it stays\n\n- **Definite subject: no er.** `Het aantal is gestegen`, never *Er is het aantal gestegen.\n- **Indefinite subject: er stays**, also when something else stands first and in a sub-clause: `Vorig jaar was er een daling`; `dat er een probleem is`. Careful Dutch keeps it, with `zijn` and with other verbs (`gebeuren`, `komen`). Mainly after a phrase of place is it often left out: `In de tabel staat (er) een fout`, `In de grafiek is (er) een daling te zien`.\n- **Passive: er may go** when another element stands first. Both are correct:\n\n> In Nederland wordt veel gefietst. — People cycle a lot in the Netherlands.\n> In Nederland wordt er veel gefietst. — People cycle a lot in the Netherlands.\n\n### Two jobs, one er\n\nWhen two jobs meet in one clause, a single `er` does both: `Er wordt veel over gesproken` (passive and `erover`). Only the number-er keeps its own: `Er zijn er drie`.\n\n### Where er stands\n\nUnstressed `er` comes early in the middle field: straight after the verb and the subject (and after `het`). The preposition stays behind, just before the final verbs. Side by side they are one word: `erover`, `ermee`.\n\n> Ik heb er gisteren met mijn collega over gesproken. — I talked about it with my colleague yesterday.\n\n`er` + preposition can also point forward to a `dat`-clause or an `om te`-clause: `Ik reken erop dat …`, `We zorgen ervoor dat …`, `Het gaat erom de cijfers goed te lezen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **A missing er with an indefinite subject**: *Is een groot verschil → `Er is een groot verschil`.\n- **er and the noun together**: *Ik reken er op het rapport → `Ik reken op het rapport` or `Ik reken erop`. `er` replaces the noun; you need one or the other.\n- **er with a definite subject**: *Er staat de grafiek op pagina 3 → `De grafiek staat op pagina 3`.\n- **A number without er**: *Ik heb twee → `Ik heb er twee`, when the noun is left out.\n- **People**: `Ik denk aan haar`, not *Ik denk eraan, when you mean a person.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has nothing like `er`. `Є три причини` is `Er zijn drie redenen`, `Їх три` is `Er zijn er drie`, and `Я розраховую на це` is `Ik reken erop`. Where Ukrainian needs no word at all, Dutch often needs `er`.","words":[{"nl":"stijging","art":"de","pl":"stijgingen","en":"rise / increase","pos":"n"},{"nl":"daling","art":"de","pl":"dalingen","en":"fall / decline","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toename","art":"de","pl":"toenames","en":"increase / growth","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afname","art":"de","pl":"afnames","en":"decrease","pos":"n"},{"nl":"percentage","art":"het","pl":"percentages","en":"percentage","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gemiddelde","art":"het","pl":"gemiddelden","en":"average","pos":"n"},{"nl":"grafiek","art":"de","pl":"grafieken","en":"graph / chart","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verhouding","art":"de","pl":"verhoudingen","en":"ratio / proportion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"prognose","art":"de","pl":"prognoses","en":"forecast / projection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toenemen","past":"nam toe","perf":"is toegenomen","en":"to increase","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"afnemen","past":"nam af","perf":"is afgenomen","en":"to decrease","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"dalen","past":"daalde","perf":"is gedaald","en":"to fall / to go down","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verdubbelen","past":"verdubbelde","perf":"is/heeft verdubbeld","en":"to double","pos":"v"},{"nl":"schommelen","past":"schommelde","perf":"heeft geschommeld","en":"to fluctuate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aanzienlijk","en":"considerable / significant","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"geleidelijk","en":"gradual","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Er zijn drie redenen voor de daling van het aantal studenten.","en":"There are three reasons for the fall in the number of students.","hint":["reden","daling","aantal","student"],"note":"drie redenen is an indefinite subject, so er takes the first place and the subject follows the verb.","alt":["Voor de daling van het aantal studenten zijn er drie redenen."]},{"nl":"Volgens de grafiek was er in maart een sterke stijging.","en":"According to the graph there was a sharp rise in March.","hint":["volgens","grafiek","maart","sterk","stijging"],"note":"Volgens de grafiek stands first, but the subject een sterke stijging is indefinite, so er stays, right after the verb.","alt":["Volgens de grafiek was er een sterke stijging in maart.","In maart was er volgens de grafiek een sterke stijging."]},{"nl":"Het rapport bevat zes grafieken, maar ik begrijp er maar twee.","en":"The report contains six graphs, but I only understand two of them.","hint":["rapport","bevatten","grafiek","begrijpen","twee"],"note":"twee stands without its noun, so er stands in for grafieken. Without er, *ik begrijp maar twee is not correct Dutch.","alt":["Het rapport bevat zes grafieken, maar ik begrijp er slechts twee."]},{"nl":"De prijzen schommelen sterk, en daar maken veel bedrijven zich zorgen over.","en":"Prices are fluctuating strongly, and many companies are worried about that.","hint":["prijs","schommelen","sterk","bedrijf","zich zorgen maken"],"note":"daar … over is the stressed form of erover, so it may stand first; the preposition stays at the end of the clause.","alt":["De prijzen schommelen sterk, en veel bedrijven maken zich daar zorgen over.","De prijzen schommelen sterk, en veel bedrijven maken zich er zorgen over.","De prijzen schommelen sterk, en daarover maken veel bedrijven zich zorgen.","De prijzen schommelen sterk, en veel bedrijven maken zich daarover zorgen."]},{"nl":"We moeten ervoor zorgen dat de cijfers kloppen.","en":"We must make sure that the figures are correct.","hint":["moeten","zorgen voor","cijfer","kloppen"],"note":"zorgen voor + a clause: ervoor points forward to the dat-clause, which says what you take care of. Ervoor is one word; zorgen dat without ervoor is also correct.","alt":["We moeten zorgen dat de cijfers kloppen."]},{"nl":"Er wordt verwacht dat de werkloosheid geleidelijk zal afnemen.","en":"It is expected that unemployment will gradually decrease.","hint":["verwachten","werkloosheid","geleidelijk","afnemen"],"note":"A passive without a subject starts with er; English says 'it is expected', but Dutch normally uses er here, not het.","alt":["Er wordt verwacht dat de werkloosheid geleidelijk afneemt.","Er wordt verwacht dat de werkloosheid geleidelijk af zal nemen.","Er wordt verwacht dat de werkloosheid geleidelijk afnemen zal.","Verwacht wordt dat de werkloosheid geleidelijk zal afnemen."]},{"nl":"In Nederland wordt steeds minder gerookt.","en":"In the Netherlands there is less and less smoking. (passive)","hint":["Nederland","steeds","minder","roken"],"note":"An impersonal passive with another element first: er may be left out. In Nederland wordt er steeds minder gerookt is just as correct.","alt":["In Nederland wordt er steeds minder gerookt.","Er wordt in Nederland steeds minder gerookt."]},{"nl":"Sinds 2010 is het aantal elektrische auto's meer dan verdubbeld.","en":"Since 2010 the number of electric cars has more than doubled.","hint":["sinds","aantal","elektrisch","auto","verdubbelen"],"note":"het aantal is a definite subject, so there is no er. Verdubbelen without an object takes zijn: is verdubbeld.","alt":["Het aantal elektrische auto's is sinds 2010 meer dan verdubbeld."]},{"nl":"Van de honderd bedrijven in de steekproef zijn er tien failliet gegaan.","en":"Of the hundred companies in the sample, ten have gone bankrupt.","hint":["bedrijf","steekproef","tien","failliet gaan"],"note":"tien stands without its noun, so the number-er follows the verb. Failliet gaan takes zijn in the perfect.","alt":["Tien van de honderd bedrijven in de steekproef zijn failliet gegaan.","Van de honderd bedrijven in de steekproef zijn tien failliet gegaan."]},{"nl":"Waar hangt de verhouding tussen de twee groepen van af?","en":"What does the ratio between the two groups depend on?","hint":["verhouding","groep","afhangen van"],"note":"Asking about a thing after a preposition: waar … van, with van at the end, just before the particle af. Waarvan hangt … af? is also correct.","alt":["Waarvan hangt de verhouding tussen de twee groepen af?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Volgens de nieuwste prognose ___ volgend jaar een lichte daling.","options":["er is","is er","is"],"correct":1,"en":"According to the latest forecast there will be a slight fall next year.","note":"Volgens de nieuwste prognose is the first element, so the verb comes second: is er. The subject een lichte daling is indefinite, so er is needed."},{"type":"choose","nl":"In de tabel ___ het gemiddelde van de afgelopen tien jaar.","options":["staat er","staat","er staat"],"correct":1,"en":"The table shows the average of the last ten years.","note":"het gemiddelde is a definite subject, so there is no er. Er goes with an indefinite subject such as een fout."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het rapport noemt vijf oorzaken voor de afname, maar ik ken ___ maar twee.","options":["ze","er","het","–"],"correct":1,"en":"The report mentions five causes of the decrease, but I only know two of them.","note":"twee stands without its noun, so the number-er is needed: ik ken er maar twee. Ze or het cannot go with a number like that."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De nieuwe cijfers zijn verrassend: ___ wordt veel over gesproken.","options":["het","er er","er"],"correct":2,"en":"The new figures are surprising: they are being talked about a lot.","note":"One er does two jobs here: the passive er and the er of erover. Two er next to each other only happens with the number-er."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De werkloosheid neemt af, maar veel mensen merken ___ nog niets van.","answers":["er","daar"],"en":"Unemployment is decreasing, but many people do not notice anything of it yet.","note":"merken van: er … van refers to the decrease. Er stands early in the middle field and van stays at the end. Daar is the stressed form and also correct."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ben je al klaar met de grafiek? Ja, ik ben ___ (met) klaar.","answers":["ermee","daarmee"],"en":"Have you finished the graph yet? Yes, I have finished it.","note":"met becomes mee after er: ermee, written as one word. *Ik ben er met klaar is wrong, and so is *met het klaar."},{"type":"gap","nl":"In de steekproef zaten vijfhonderd mensen; er waren ___ maar twintig ouder dan tachtig.","answers":["er","daarvan"],"en":"There were five hundred people in the sample; only twenty of them were older than eighty.","note":"The first er is the subject-er; the second stands in for mensen after the number twintig. The number-er always keeps its own er (or daarvan: of those)."},{"type":"order","lead":"In de grafiek","words":["is","er","een duidelijke daling","te zien"],"answers":["In de grafiek is er een duidelijke daling te zien."],"en":"A clear fall can be seen in the graph.","note":"After the first element come the verb is and then er; the indefinite subject follows, and te zien closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik heb","words":["er","gisteren","met mijn collega","over","gesproken"],"answers":["Ik heb er gisteren met mijn collega over gesproken.","Ik heb er met mijn collega gisteren over gesproken.","Ik heb er gisteren over gesproken met mijn collega."],"en":"I talked about it with my colleague yesterday.","note":"Unstressed er comes straight after heb; over stays just before the participle. Met mijn collega may also follow gesproken."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het gaat","words":["erom","dat","de cijfers","betrouwbaar","zijn"],"answers":["Het gaat erom dat de cijfers betrouwbaar zijn."],"en":"What matters is that the figures are reliable.","note":"Het gaat om: erom points forward to the dat-clause. In the clause the verb zijn goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Vorig jaar and keep er.","from":"Er was vorig jaar een aanzienlijke toename van het aantal inwoners.","answers":["Vorig jaar was er een aanzienlijke toename van het aantal inwoners."],"en":"Last year there was a considerable increase in the number of inhabitants.","note":"The subject is indefinite, so er stays when Vorig jaar stands first; it moves to the place after the verb."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with In Nederland.","from":"Er wordt in Nederland veel gefietst.","answers":["In Nederland wordt veel gefietst.","In Nederland wordt er veel gefietst."],"en":"People cycle a lot in the Netherlands.","note":"In a passive without a subject, er may go when another element stands first. Both sentences are correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace op de nieuwe cijfers with er + op.","from":"De minister baseert haar beleid op de nieuwe cijfers.","answers":["De minister baseert haar beleid erop.","De minister baseert er haar beleid op."],"en":"The minister bases her policy on it.","note":"er + op replaces a thing. Er may stay next to op (erop) or move forward before the object, leaving op behind."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Ask about the thing after op, with waar.","from":"Het percentage is gebaseerd op een kleine steekproef.","answers":["Waar is het percentage op gebaseerd?","Waarop is het percentage gebaseerd?"],"en":"What is the percentage based on?","note":"For a thing after a preposition the question word is waar + preposition. Waar goes first; op may stay with it or wait before the participle."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Is een groot verschil tussen mannen en vrouwen.","answers":["Er is een groot verschil tussen mannen en vrouwen."],"en":"There is a big difference between men and women.","note":"The subject een groot verschil is indefinite, so the sentence starts with er. Without it the sentence reads as a broken question."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"We rekenen er op een snelle stijging.","answers":["We rekenen op een snelle stijging."],"en":"We are counting on a quick rise.","note":"er replaces the thing after the preposition. When the thing itself is named (een snelle stijging), er has to go."}]},
  {"id":"b2-11","level":"B2","icon":"🌍","title":"Climate and energy","grammar":"Word order for emphasis: what goes first, what goes last","summary":"First place: the link with what came before, or a contrast (Dat plan steun ik niet). After the final verbs: phrases with a preposition, dan-comparisons, sub-clauses; never an object, an adjective or niet.","explain":"A Dutch sentence has a fixed frame: the first place, the conjugated verb, the middle field (*het middenveld*), the final verbs, and a last field after them (*het achterveld*). The verbs stay where they are. What you choose is what goes first, what goes to the end of the middle field and what goes after the final verbs. Often several orders are correct; they differ only in emphasis.\n\n| first place | verb | middle field | final verbs, last field |\n| Ik | heb | dat plan nooit | gesteund. |\n| Dat plan | heb | ik nooit | gesteund. |\n| We | hebben | lang | gewacht op de monteur. |\n\n### The first place\n\nBesides the subject, almost any one element can stand first: a time or place, an object, a whole sub-clause, even an infinitive or a participle. The verb follows, then the subject. What stands first is usually **the link with what came before** (often `dat`, `die`, `daar`) or **a contrast**.\n\n> Dat plan steun ik niet. — That plan I do not support.\n> Als de gasprijs stijgt, isoleren meer mensen hun huis. — If the gas price rises, more people insulate their homes.\n> Betalen wil niemand. — Nobody wants to pay.\n> Gelezen heb ik het rapport wel, maar ik ben het er niet mee eens. — I did read the report, but I do not agree with it.\n\nAn infinitive or participle in first place nearly always means contrast; it is stressed, and `wel` or `niet` often follows. And when an object stands first, the verb still agrees with the subject: `Windturbines wil niemand in zijn achtertuin`.\n\n### The last field: after the final verbs\n\nAfter the final verbs Dutch can put:\n\n- a phrase with a preposition: `We hebben lang gewacht op de monteur`, or `lang op de monteur gewacht`; both are correct.\n- a comparison with `dan` or `als`: `Het is duurder geworden dan vorig jaar`, or `duurder dan vorig jaar geworden`.\n- a `dat`-clause or `of`-clause: it **must** go there, unless it stands first. `Ik heb gezegd dat het te duur is`, never *Ik heb dat het te duur is gezegd.\n- a `te`-clause: `We hebben besloten (om) het huis te isoleren`.\n\nMoving a long part there keeps the conjugated verb and the final verbs close together, so the reader does not have to wait for the verb.\n\nWhat may **not** go there:\n\n- an object without a preposition: `Ik heb het rapport gelezen`, not *Ik heb gelezen het rapport.\n- a predicate adjective: `Het is duur geworden`, not *Het is geworden duur.\n- `niet`: `We kunnen het niet stoppen`, not *We kunnen het stoppen niet.\n\n### The pincer (de tang)\n\nArticle and noun, and the two verb positions, work like the two arms of a pincer (*de tang*). Put too much between them and the sentence becomes hard to follow. `de door de regering vorig jaar genomen maatregelen` is correct but heavy; a relative clause after the noun is easier. Between the verbs, move a phrase with a preposition to the last field.\n\n> De maatregelen die de regering vorig jaar heeft genomen, werken niet. — The measures that the government took last year do not work.\n> Het kabinet heeft tot diep in de nacht vergaderd over de klimaatwet. — The cabinet met until deep into the night about the climate law.\n\n### The end of the middle field\n\nThe last place before the final verbs is usually where **new and important** information goes; it carries the stress. Things that are already known (pronouns, a noun mentioned before) come earlier.\n\n> We hebben vorig jaar zonnepanelen gekocht. — We bought solar panels last year.\n> We hebben de zonnepanelen vorig jaar gekocht. — We bought the solar panels last year.\n\nIn the first sentence the news is the panels; in the second it is when.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **One element before the verb**: *Dat plan ik steun niet → `Dat plan steun ik niet`.\n- **The verb agrees with the subject**, not with the first element: `Fossiele brandstoffen wil de regering duurder maken`.\n- **No object, adjective or niet after the final verbs** (see above).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian can move almost any word for emphasis, because the case endings show who does what. Dutch can do this only in the places shown above; the verbs stay put. `Цей план я не підтримую` works just like `Dat plan steun ik niet`.","words":[{"nl":"klimaatverandering","art":"de","pl":"","en":"climate change","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"opwarming","art":"de","pl":"","en":"warming (of the earth)","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"broeikasgas","art":"het","pl":"broeikasgassen","en":"greenhouse gas","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zonnepaneel","art":"het","pl":"zonnepanelen","en":"solar panel","pos":"n"},{"nl":"windturbine","art":"de","pl":"windturbines","en":"wind turbine","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aardgas","art":"het","pl":"","en":"natural gas","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"brandstof","art":"de","pl":"brandstoffen","en":"fuel","pos":"n"},{"nl":"warmtepomp","art":"de","pl":"warmtepompen","en":"heat pump","pos":"n"},{"nl":"energieverbruik","art":"het","pl":"","en":"energy consumption","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"zeespiegel","art":"de","pl":"zeespiegels","en":"sea level","pos":"n"},{"nl":"isoleren","past":"isoleerde","perf":"heeft geïsoleerd","en":"to insulate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verminderen","past":"verminderde","perf":"heeft verminderd","en":"to reduce","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opwekken","past":"wekte op","perf":"heeft opgewekt","en":"to generate (energy)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"terugdringen","past":"drong terug","perf":"heeft teruggedrongen","en":"to cut back / to bring down","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"fossiel","en":"fossil","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"hernieuwbaar","en":"renewable","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Dat plan steun ik niet, want het is veel te duur.","en":"That plan I do not support, because it is far too expensive.","hint":["plan","steunen","niet","duur"],"note":"The object dat plan stands first as the link with what was just said; the verb steun stays second and the subject ik follows it.","alt":["Ik steun dat plan niet, want het is veel te duur."]},{"nl":"Zonnepanelen hebben we al, maar een warmtepomp nog niet.","en":"Solar panels we already have, but not a heat pump yet.","hint":["zonnepaneel","hebben","al","warmtepomp"],"note":"Zonnepanelen stands first for contrast with een warmtepomp. After it comes the verb, then we.","alt":["We hebben al zonnepanelen, maar een warmtepomp nog niet.","Zonnepanelen hebben we al, maar nog geen warmtepomp.","We hebben al zonnepanelen, maar nog geen warmtepomp."]},{"nl":"Betalen wil niemand, maar iedereen wil schone lucht.","en":"Nobody wants to pay, but everyone wants clean air.","hint":["betalen","willen","niemand","schoon","lucht"],"note":"An infinitive in first place gives strong contrast. The verb wil agrees with the subject niemand.","alt":["Niemand wil betalen, maar iedereen wil schone lucht."]},{"nl":"Als de gasprijs blijft stijgen, gaan veel mensen hun huis isoleren.","en":"If the gas price keeps rising, many people are going to insulate their homes.","hint":["gasprijs","blijven","stijgen","isoleren"],"note":"The whole als-clause fills the first place, so the verb gaan follows directly after the comma, before the subject veel mensen.","alt":["Veel mensen gaan hun huis isoleren als de gasprijs blijft stijgen.","Als de gasprijs blijft stijgen, gaan veel mensen hun huizen isoleren.","Veel mensen gaan hun huizen isoleren als de gasprijs blijft stijgen.","Als de gasprijs stijgen blijft, gaan veel mensen hun huis isoleren.","Veel mensen gaan hun huis isoleren als de gasprijs stijgen blijft."]},{"nl":"De regering heeft lang gewacht met maatregelen tegen klimaatverandering.","en":"The government waited a long time with measures against climate change.","hint":["regering","wachten","maatregel","klimaatverandering"],"note":"The long phrase with met stands after the participle gewacht, so heeft and gewacht stay close together. Inside the middle field it is also correct.","alt":["De regering heeft lang met maatregelen tegen klimaatverandering gewacht.","De regering heeft met maatregelen tegen klimaatverandering lang gewacht."]},{"nl":"Stroom is dit jaar duurder geworden dan vorig jaar.","en":"Electricity has become more expensive this year than last year.","hint":["stroom","dit jaar","duur","worden","vorig jaar"],"note":"The comparison dan vorig jaar may follow the final verb geworden; the adjective duurder itself must stay before it.","alt":["Stroom is dit jaar duurder dan vorig jaar geworden.","Dit jaar is stroom duurder geworden dan vorig jaar.","Dit jaar is stroom duurder dan vorig jaar geworden."]},{"nl":"We hebben besloten om ons oude huis beter te isoleren.","en":"We have decided to insulate our old house better.","hint":["besluiten","oud","huis","goed","isoleren"],"note":"A te-clause goes after the final verb besloten. Om is optional here.","alt":["We hebben besloten ons oude huis beter te isoleren."]},{"nl":"De maatregelen die de regering vorig jaar heeft genomen, werken nog niet.","en":"The measures that the government took last year are not working yet.","hint":["maatregel","regering","vorig jaar","nemen","werken"],"note":"A relative clause after the noun avoids a heavy pincer like de door de regering vorig jaar genomen maatregelen.","alt":["De maatregelen die de regering vorig jaar genomen heeft, werken nog niet."]},{"nl":"Hoeveel energie we kunnen opwekken, hangt af van het weer.","en":"How much energy we can generate depends on the weather.","hint":["energie","kunnen","opwekken","afhangen van","weer"],"note":"The whole clause is the subject and fills the first place; hangt follows it. Van het weer may also stand before af: hangt van het weer af.","alt":["Hoeveel energie we kunnen opwekken, hangt van het weer af.","Het hangt van het weer af hoeveel energie we kunnen opwekken.","Het hangt af van het weer hoeveel energie we kunnen opwekken.","Hoeveel energie we op kunnen wekken, hangt af van het weer.","Hoeveel energie we op kunnen wekken, hangt van het weer af.","Hoeveel energie we opwekken kunnen, hangt af van het weer.","Hoeveel energie we opwekken kunnen, hangt van het weer af."]},{"nl":"Gelezen heeft de minister het klimaatrapport wel, maar ze heeft er niets mee gedaan.","en":"The minister did read the climate report, but she did nothing with it.","hint":["lezen","minister","klimaatrapport","wel","doen"],"note":"The participle gelezen in first place contrasts reading with doing. Wel underlines the contrast; the subject follows the verb heeft.","alt":["De minister heeft het klimaatrapport wel gelezen, maar ze heeft er niets mee gedaan.","Het klimaatrapport heeft de minister wel gelezen, maar ze heeft er niets mee gedaan.","Gelezen heeft de minister het klimaatrapport wel, maar zij heeft er niets mee gedaan.","De minister heeft het klimaatrapport wel gelezen, maar zij heeft er niets mee gedaan."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Die ene windturbine ___ de bewoners van het dorp niet tegenhouden.","options":["kan","kunnen","kunt"],"correct":1,"en":"The residents of the village cannot stop that one wind turbine.","note":"The object stands first, but the subject is de bewoners, so the verb is plural: kunnen. With kan the turbine would be stopping the residents."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Fossiele brandstoffen ___ de regering de komende jaren duurder maken.","options":["willen","wil","wilt"],"correct":1,"en":"The government wants to make fossil fuels more expensive in the coming years.","note":"The verb agrees with the subject de regering, not with the first element fossiele brandstoffen: wil."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het kabinet heeft lang gediscussieerd ___.","options":["hernieuwbare energie","niet","over hernieuwbare energie"],"correct":2,"en":"The cabinet discussed renewable energy for a long time.","note":"After the final verb only a phrase with a preposition fits. An object without a preposition or niet cannot stand there."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dat het klimaat verandert, ___ (ontkennen) bijna niemand meer.","answers":["ontkent"],"en":"Hardly anyone denies any more that the climate is changing.","note":"The dat-clause is the object and fills the first place, so the verb follows straight after the comma. The subject is niemand, singular: ontkent."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vooral voor zonnepanelen ___ (geven) de overheid veel subsidie.","answers":["geeft"],"en":"The government gives a lot of subsidy above all for solar panels.","note":"Vooral voor zonnepanelen is one element in first place, so the verb comes next and the subject de overheid after it: geeft."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Terugdringen ___ (moeten) we de uitstoot hoe dan ook; de vraag is alleen hoe snel.","answers":["moeten"],"en":"We have to cut emissions back no matter what; the only question is how fast.","note":"The infinitive terugdringen stands first for contrast. The conjugated verb agrees with we: moeten."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","lang","gewacht","op de installateur"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik lang gewacht op de installateur.","Gisteren heb ik lang op de installateur gewacht.","Gisteren heb ik op de installateur lang gewacht."],"en":"Yesterday I waited a long time for the installer.","note":"A phrase with a preposition may stand after the participle or in the middle field. Putting it after gewacht keeps the verbs close together."},{"type":"order","lead":"Aardgas","words":["is","veel duurder","geworden","dan vroeger"],"answers":["Aardgas is veel duurder geworden dan vroeger.","Aardgas is veel duurder dan vroeger geworden."],"en":"Natural gas has become much more expensive than it used to be.","note":"The comparison with dan may go after the final verb or stay with the adjective. Veel duurder itself must stand before geworden."},{"type":"order","lead":"De gemeente","words":["heeft","beloofd","alle scholen","te isoleren","voor 2030"],"answers":["De gemeente heeft beloofd alle scholen voor 2030 te isoleren.","De gemeente heeft beloofd voor 2030 alle scholen te isoleren.","De gemeente heeft beloofd alle scholen te isoleren voor 2030."],"en":"The municipality has promised to insulate all schools before 2030.","note":"The te-clause comes after the participle beloofd. Inside it, voor 2030 may stand before or after alle scholen, or after te isoleren."},{"type":"order","lead":"Als we niets doen,","words":["stijgt","de zeespiegel","nog","verder"],"answers":["Als we niets doen, stijgt de zeespiegel nog verder."],"en":"If we do nothing, the sea level will rise even further.","note":"The als-clause counts as the first element, so the verb stijgt comes directly after the comma and then the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Zonnepanelen.","from":"Mijn buren hebben vorig jaar zonnepanelen op hun dak gelegd.","answers":["Zonnepanelen hebben mijn buren vorig jaar op hun dak gelegd."],"en":"My neighbours put solar panels on their roof last year.","note":"The object moves to the first place, the verb hebben stays second and the subject mijn buren follows it. The rest keeps its order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Move the phrase with over to the end of the sentence.","from":"De ministers hebben gisteren tot diep in de nacht over het verminderen van broeikasgassen vergaderd.","answers":["De ministers hebben gisteren tot diep in de nacht vergaderd over het verminderen van broeikasgassen."],"en":"Yesterday the ministers met until deep into the night about reducing greenhouse gases.","note":"A long phrase with a preposition may follow the participle. Then hebben and vergaderd are no longer so far apart."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Avoid the heavy pincer: put a relative clause after maatregel.","from":"De door het kabinet vorige maand aangekondigde maatregel is omstreden.","answers":["De maatregel die het kabinet vorige maand heeft aangekondigd, is omstreden.","De maatregel die het kabinet vorige maand aangekondigd heeft, is omstreden.","De maatregel die vorige maand door het kabinet is aangekondigd, is omstreden.","De maatregel die vorige maand door het kabinet aangekondigd is, is omstreden.","De maatregel die het kabinet vorige maand aankondigde, is omstreden.","De maatregel die vorige maand door het kabinet werd aangekondigd, is omstreden.","De maatregel die door het kabinet vorige maand is aangekondigd, is omstreden.","De maatregel die door het kabinet vorige maand aangekondigd is, is omstreden.","De maatregel die door het kabinet vorige maand werd aangekondigd, is omstreden."],"en":"The measure that the cabinet announced last month is controversial.","note":"Everything between de and maatregel moves into a relative clause after the noun. Die is used because maatregel is a de-word."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb gelezen het hele klimaatrapport.","answers":["Ik heb het hele klimaatrapport gelezen."],"en":"I have read the whole climate report.","note":"An object without a preposition cannot stand after the final verb; it belongs in the middle field."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het energieverbruik is geworden veel lager.","answers":["Het energieverbruik is veel lager geworden."],"en":"Energy consumption has become much lower.","note":"A predicate adjective stays before the final verb. Only a comparison with dan could follow geworden."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Zonder nieuwe maatregelen kunnen we de opwarming stoppen niet.","answers":["Zonder nieuwe maatregelen kunnen we de opwarming niet stoppen."],"en":"Without new measures we cannot stop the warming.","note":"niet never stands after the final verbs. Here it goes directly before the infinitive stoppen."}]},
  {"id":"b2-12","level":"B2","icon":"🧩","title":"Mind and behaviour","grammar":"Adjectives and nouns with a fixed preposition","summary":"Many adjectives and nouns have a fixed preposition: trots op, bang voor, afhankelijk van, behoefte aan, invloed op. For a thing use er + preposition (Ik ben eraan gewend), in a question waar + preposition.","explain":"Like the verbs in B1 · 16, many adjectives and nouns come with their own preposition. It is part of the word, and it often differs from English and Ukrainian: `trots op` (proud **of**), `bang voor` (afraid **of**), `afhankelijk van` (dependent **on**). Learn the word and its preposition as one unit.\n\n### Adjectives\n\n| preposition | adjectives |\n| op | trots op, boos op, jaloers op, verliefd op, gek op |\n| voor | bang voor, verantwoordelijk voor, gevoelig voor, geschikt voor |\n| aan | gewend aan, verslaafd aan |\n| in | goed in, slecht in, geïnteresseerd in, teleurgesteld in |\n| met | blij met, tevreden met, bezig met, bekend met, het eens met |\n| van, over | afhankelijk van; verbaasd over, tevreden over |\n\nSome take two prepositions with a difference in meaning:\n\n- `boos op` a person, `boos over` a thing: `Ze is boos op haar baas over de planning`.\n- `teleurgesteld in` usually a person, `teleurgesteld over` a result or event.\n- `tevreden met` and `tevreden over` are both correct and often interchangeable; `met` leans towards what you have or get, `over` towards how something went.\n- `bekend met` (familiar with), `bekend om` (famous for).\n\n> Ze is erg gevoelig voor kritiek. — She is very sensitive to criticism.\n> Ik ben teleurgesteld in hem. — I am disappointed in him.\n\n### Nouns\n\n| preposition | nouns |\n| aan | behoefte aan, gebrek aan, hekel aan |\n| op | invloed op, kans op, recht op, antwoord op, reactie op, kritiek op |\n| voor | aandacht voor, belangstelling voor, respect voor, angst voor |\n| in, tussen | vertrouwen in, zin in; verschil tussen |\n\n`de reden` takes `voor` or `van`: `de reden voor het besluit`, `de reden van zijn vertrek`.\n\n> Veel mensen hebben behoefte aan rust. — Many people need peace and quiet.\n> Ouders hebben veel invloed op het gedrag van hun kinderen. — Parents have a lot of influence on their children's behaviour.\n\n### A person or a thing\n\nFor a person: preposition + pronoun, `Ik ben trots op haar`. For a thing: `er` + preposition, and in a question `waar` + preposition (B1 · 05, B2 · 10). Unstressed `er` comes early and the preposition stays with the adjective.\n\n> Ik ben er niet aan gewend. — I am not used to it.\n> Waar ben je bang voor? — What are you afraid of?\n\n### Pointing forward to a clause\n\n`er` + preposition can point forward to a `dat`-clause. With adjectives of feeling (`trots`, `blij`, `bang`, `gewend`) it is often left out, so both are correct: `Ze is er trots op dat ze geslaagd is` and `Ze is trots dat ze geslaagd is`. With many other words it must stay: `Hij is ervoor verantwoordelijk dat …`. Before an `om te`-clause it is normally left out: `Hij is bang om fouten te maken`, `Ik heb geen zin om te koken`.\n\n> Ik ben eraan gewend dat hij altijd te laat komt. — I am used to him always being late.\n\n### Where the phrase stands\n\nNormally the phrase follows the adjective: `Ik ben trots op hem`. Before it is also possible, with a little more weight on the adjective: `Ik ben op hem trots`. With final verbs, the phrase may stand before them or after them; both are correct:\n\n> Ze is altijd afhankelijk geweest van haar ouders. — She has always been dependent on her parents.\n> Ze is altijd afhankelijk van haar ouders geweest. — She has always been dependent on her parents.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **English prepositions do not carry over**: *trots van → `trots op`; *afhankelijk op → `afhankelijk van`; *geïnteresseerd voor → `geïnteresseerd in`; *gebrek van → `gebrek aan`.\n- **er for a thing, a pronoun for a person**: `Ik ben eraan gewend` (to it), but `Ik heb respect voor hem`, not *Ik heb er respect voor, when you mean a person.\n- **No er next to the noun itself**: `Ik ben gewend aan het werk`, not *Ik ben er gewend aan het werk.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nSome pairs match: `залежний від` is `afhankelijk van`. But where Ukrainian uses only a case, Dutch needs a preposition: `боятися чогось` is `bang zijn voor iets`, `пишатися кимось` is `trots zijn op iemand`, `цікавитися чимось` is `geïnteresseerd zijn in iets`.","words":[{"nl":"gedrag","art":"het","pl":"","en":"behaviour","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"eigenschap","art":"de","pl":"eigenschappen","en":"quality / trait","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zelfvertrouwen","art":"het","pl":"","en":"self-confidence","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"onzekerheid","art":"de","pl":"onzekerheden","en":"insecurity / uncertainty","pos":"n"},{"nl":"behoefte","art":"de","pl":"behoeften","en":"need","pos":"n","tag":"+ aan"},{"nl":"gebrek","art":"het","pl":"gebreken","en":"lack / shortage","pos":"n","tag":"+ aan"},{"nl":"invloed","art":"de","pl":"invloeden","en":"influence","pos":"n","tag":"+ op"},{"nl":"belangstelling","art":"de","pl":"","en":"interest","pos":"n","tag":"+ voor"},{"nl":"afhankelijk van","en":"dependent on","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"verslaafd aan","en":"addicted to","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"gevoelig voor","en":"sensitive to","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"verantwoordelijk voor","en":"responsible for","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"teleurgesteld in","en":"disappointed in (a person)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"zelfbeheersing","art":"de","pl":"","en":"self-control","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"impulsief","en":"impulsive","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"zich gedragen","past":"gedroeg zich","perf":"heeft zich gedragen","en":"to behave","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Mijn zoon is erg gevoelig voor kritiek van anderen.","en":"My son is very sensitive to criticism from others.","hint":["zoon","gevoelig voor","kritiek","ander"],"note":"gevoelig takes voor, where English says 'sensitive to'. The phrase follows the adjective."},{"nl":"Veel jongeren zijn verslaafd aan hun telefoon.","en":"Many young people are addicted to their phones.","hint":["jongere","verslaafd aan","telefoon"],"note":"verslaafd aan: here English 'to' and Dutch aan happen to match. With a thing as pronoun it becomes eraan: Ze zijn eraan verslaafd.","alt":["Veel jongeren zijn verslaafd aan hun telefoons."]},{"nl":"Ze is er trots op dat ze haar angst heeft overwonnen.","en":"She is proud that she has overcome her fear.","hint":["trots op","angst","overwinnen"],"note":"er … op points forward to the dat-clause. Many speakers leave er … op out: Ze is trots dat …; both are correct.","alt":["Ze is er trots op dat ze haar angst overwonnen heeft.","Ze is trots dat ze haar angst heeft overwonnen.","Ze is trots dat ze haar angst overwonnen heeft.","Ze is erop trots dat ze haar angst heeft overwonnen.","Ze is erop trots dat ze haar angst overwonnen heeft."]},{"nl":"Waar ben je eigenlijk zo bang voor?","en":"What are you actually so afraid of?","hint":["bang voor","eigenlijk"],"note":"Asking about a thing after bang voor: waar first, voor at the end. Waarvoor ben je eigenlijk zo bang? is also correct.","alt":["Waarvoor ben je eigenlijk zo bang?","Waar ben jij eigenlijk zo bang voor?","Waarvoor ben jij eigenlijk zo bang?"]},{"nl":"Kinderen hebben behoefte aan duidelijke regels en aandacht.","en":"Children need clear rules and attention.","hint":["kind","behoefte aan","duidelijk","regel","aandacht"],"note":"behoefte hebben aan is a more formal way of saying 'to need'; the preposition is always aan.","alt":["Kinderen hebben duidelijke regels en aandacht nodig."]},{"nl":"Hij is nog steeds financieel afhankelijk van zijn ouders.","en":"He is still financially dependent on his parents.","hint":["nog steeds","financieel","afhankelijk van","ouders"],"note":"afhankelijk takes van, not op as English 'on' suggests. Ukrainian залежний від works the same way.","alt":["Hij is nog steeds financieel van zijn ouders afhankelijk."]},{"nl":"Door een gebrek aan zelfvertrouwen durft ze niet te solliciteren.","en":"Because of a lack of self-confidence she does not dare to apply for jobs.","hint":["gebrek aan","zelfvertrouwen","durven","solliciteren"],"note":"gebrek takes aan, not van as English 'of' suggests. Door … is one element in first place, so durft comes next.","alt":["Ze durft door een gebrek aan zelfvertrouwen niet te solliciteren.","Door een gebrek aan zelfvertrouwen durft zij niet te solliciteren."]},{"nl":"Ik ben teleurgesteld in mijn broer, want hij heeft gelogen.","en":"I am disappointed in my brother, because he lied.","hint":["teleurgesteld in","broer","liegen"],"note":"teleurgesteld in for a person; for a result or event Dutch usually says teleurgesteld over.","alt":["Ik ben teleurgesteld in mijn broer, want hij loog."]},{"nl":"Vrienden hebben veel invloed op het gedrag van tieners.","en":"Friends have a lot of influence on the behaviour of teenagers.","hint":["vriend","invloed op","gedrag","tiener"],"note":"invloed takes op: invloed op iets. The whole phrase op het gedrag van tieners belongs to the noun invloed.","alt":["Vrienden hebben veel invloed op het gedrag van pubers."]},{"nl":"Ze is altijd verantwoordelijk geweest voor haar jongere zusjes.","en":"She has always been responsible for her younger sisters.","hint":["altijd","verantwoordelijk voor","jong","zus"],"note":"The phrase with voor may stand after the final verb geweest, or before it: verantwoordelijk voor haar jongere zusjes geweest.","alt":["Ze is altijd verantwoordelijk voor haar jongere zusjes geweest.","Ze is altijd verantwoordelijk geweest voor haar jongere zussen.","Ze is altijd verantwoordelijk voor haar jongere zussen geweest."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Hij is altijd jaloers ___ zijn oudere broer geweest.","options":["van","op","aan","voor"],"correct":1,"en":"He has always been jealous of his older brother.","note":"jaloers takes op. English 'of' tempts you to use van, but that is wrong here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze heeft veel belangstelling ___ psychologie.","options":["in","over","voor","aan"],"correct":2,"en":"She has a lot of interest in psychology.","note":"belangstelling takes voor. English 'interest in' leads to in, but that goes with geïnteresseerd, not with belangstelling."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze was boos ___ haar collega, omdat hij haar idee als zijn eigen idee had gepresenteerd.","options":["over","met","op"],"correct":2,"en":"She was angry with her colleague, because he had presented her idea as his own.","note":"boos op a person, boos over a thing. Met is the English 'with', which does not work in Dutch here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn dochter? Ja, ik ben heel trots ___.","options":["erop","op haar","op het"],"correct":1,"en":"My daughter? Yes, I am very proud of her.","note":"For a person, use the preposition + pronoun: op haar. Erop is for a thing, and op het is never used this way."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Door hun onzekerheid zijn sommige mensen erg gevoelig ___ kritiek.","answers":["voor"],"en":"Because of their insecurity some people are very sensitive to criticism.","note":"gevoelig takes voor, where English uses 'to'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ben je al gewend aan je nieuwe baan? Ja, ik ben ___ al aardig aan gewend.","answers":["er","daar"],"en":"Are you used to your new job yet? Yes, I am fairly used to it already.","note":"For a thing, er replaces aan + noun. Er stands early in the middle field and aan stays with gewend. Daar is the stressed form."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ heb je vanavond zin in?","answers":["Waar"],"en":"What do you feel like doing tonight?","note":"zin hebben in: a question about the thing becomes waar … in, with in at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wie is er verantwoordelijk ___ de planning van het project?","answers":["voor"],"en":"Who is responsible for the planning of the project?","note":"verantwoordelijk takes voor, just like English 'responsible for'. Not van."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ze is","words":["er","al jaren","aan gewend","dat","hij","laat","thuiskomt"],"answers":["Ze is er al jaren aan gewend dat hij laat thuiskomt."],"en":"She has been used to him coming home late for years.","note":"er points forward to the dat-clause. Er stands early, al jaren follows, aan stays with gewend, and the clause goes last."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hij","words":["is","altijd","erg afhankelijk","geweest","van zijn vrouw"],"answers":["Hij is altijd erg afhankelijk geweest van zijn vrouw.","Hij is altijd erg afhankelijk van zijn vrouw geweest.","Hij is altijd van zijn vrouw erg afhankelijk geweest.","Hij is van zijn vrouw altijd erg afhankelijk geweest."],"en":"He has always been very dependent on his wife.","note":"The phrase with van may follow geweest or stand in the middle field; after the adjective or after geweest is the most usual."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik heb","words":["altijd","een hekel","gehad","aan ruzie"],"answers":["Ik heb altijd een hekel gehad aan ruzie.","Ik heb altijd een hekel aan ruzie gehad.","Ik heb aan ruzie altijd een hekel gehad."],"en":"I have always hated arguments.","note":"een hekel hebben aan: the phrase with aan may follow the participle gehad or stand in the middle field."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace aan zijn telefoon with er + aan.","from":"Hij is verslaafd aan zijn telefoon.","answers":["Hij is eraan verslaafd.","Hij is er verslaafd aan."],"en":"He is addicted to it.","note":"er + aan replaces a thing. Eraan verslaafd is the usual written form; er verslaafd aan is also common."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Ask about the thing after in, with waar.","from":"Ze is geïnteresseerd in de eigenschappen van een goede leider.","answers":["Waar is ze in geïnteresseerd?","Waarin is ze geïnteresseerd?","Waar is zij in geïnteresseerd?","Waarin is zij geïnteresseerd?"],"en":"What is she interested in?","note":"geïnteresseerd in a thing: the question word is waar + in. Waar goes first; in may stay with it or wait before the adjective."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make one sentence. Start with Hij is er trots op dat.","from":"Hij heeft zijn verslaving overwonnen. Hij is daar trots op.","answers":["Hij is er trots op dat hij zijn verslaving heeft overwonnen.","Hij is er trots op dat hij zijn verslaving overwonnen heeft."],"en":"He is proud that he has overcome his addiction.","note":"er … op points forward to the dat-clause, which says what he is proud of. In the clause the verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ze is erg geïnteresseerd voor het gedrag van dieren.","answers":["Ze is erg geïnteresseerd in het gedrag van dieren."],"en":"She is very interested in the behaviour of animals.","note":"geïnteresseerd takes in. Voor goes with belangstelling, not with geïnteresseerd."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hij gedraagt zich soms impulsief door een gebrek van zelfbeheersing.","answers":["Hij gedraagt zich soms impulsief door een gebrek aan zelfbeheersing."],"en":"He sometimes behaves impulsively because of a lack of self-control.","note":"gebrek takes aan: een gebrek aan iets. English 'lack of' tempts you to use van."}]},
  {"id":"b2-13","level":"B2","icon":"💹","title":"Money and the economy","grammar":"Infinitive clauses: door te, zonder te, na te hebben","summary":"door / zonder / in plaats van / alvorens / om … te + infinitive, and na … te hebben or te zijn + participle: a short clause with the subject of the main clause. Another subject needs doordat, zonder dat, nadat.","explain":"News and formal Dutch often use a short clause without a subject of its own, built on `te` + infinitive: `door te sparen`, `zonder te betalen`, `na te hebben gekeken`. Dutch grammar calls it a *shortened sub-clause* (*de beknopte bijzin*). It can leave the subject out because the doer is **the subject of the main clause**.\n\n### The words that start it\n\n| word | meaning | example |\n| door … te | by doing | door te sparen |\n| zonder … te | without doing | zonder te betalen |\n| in plaats van … te | instead of doing | in plaats van te lenen |\n| alvorens … te | before doing (formal) | alvorens te tekenen |\n| om … te | in order to | om te investeren |\n| na … te hebben / te zijn | after doing | na te hebben gelezen |\n\nEverything that belongs to the verb comes between the first word and `te`; `te` + infinitive closes the clause. A separable verb takes `te` between its parts (unit B1 · 17): `zonder iets uit te geven`.\n\n> Door minder uit te geven, betaalde ze haar schulden af. — By spending less, she paid off her debts.\n> Hij tekende het contract zonder de kleine letters te lezen. — He signed the contract without reading the small print.\n> In plaats van te bezuinigen, nam de onderneming meer personeel aan. — Instead of cutting costs, the business took on more staff.\n\n### na: always te hebben or te zijn\n\nAfter `na` the action is finished, so Dutch uses the perfect: `te hebben` or `te zijn` + participle, with the auxiliary of the perfect (`heeft gelezen`, `is gegaan`). The participle may stand before or after `te hebben`; both are correct.\n\n> Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot hij te verkopen. — After looking at the figures, he decided to sell.\n> Na failliet gegaan te zijn, werd het bedrijf overgenomen. — After going bankrupt, the company was taken over.\n\n### om: required, optional or wrong\n\n- **Purpose** (in order to): `om` is required. `Ze leent geld om een huis te kopen`.\n- **After te + adjective**: also `om`: `Die aandelen zijn te riskant om te kopen`.\n- **After proberen, besluiten, beloven, vergeten, weigeren**: optional. `Hij besloot (om) te verkopen`: both are correct.\n- **After schijnen, lijken, blijken, hoeven** (next unit): never. `U hoeft niets te betalen`.\n\n### zijn, hebben, vallen, staan, geven + te\n\n- `zijn te`: can be done. `Dat is niet te betalen` (that is unaffordable); `Het is te hopen dat …` (it is to be hoped that …).\n- `hebben te`: have something to do. `Ik heb nog veel te doen`; `Je hebt niets te vrezen`.\n- `vallen te`, often with `er`: `Er valt weinig te verdienen`; `Dat valt nog te bezien` (that remains to be seen).\n- `staan te wachten`: lie ahead. `Ons staat een moeilijk jaar te wachten`.\n- `te denken geven`: `Die cijfers geven te denken` (those figures are a cause for concern).\n\n### The noun variant: door het + infinitive\n\n> Door het verhogen van de rente wil de centrale bank de inflatie remmen. — By raising interest rates the central bank wants to curb inflation.\n\n### Another subject: a full sub-clause\n\nWith `door`, `zonder`, `in plaats van`, `na` and `alvorens` the short clause only works when the doer is the subject of the main clause. Otherwise use a sub-clause with the verb at the end: `doordat`, `zonder dat`, `nadat`, `voordat`, `in plaats van dat`.\n\n> De koers daalde zonder dat iemand het had verwacht. — The share price fell without anyone having expected it.\n\n### Where the clause goes\n\nFirst or last. First, it fills the first place, so the conjugated verb follows and then the subject: `Door te sparen kun je later beleggen`. Last, it comes after the final verbs: `Hij heeft veel verloren door slecht te beleggen`. A comma after a clause at the start is common but not required. A short one can stand in the middle (`Hij is zonder te betalen vertrokken`), but first or last is the safe choice.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Na de cijfers te bekijken → `Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken`.\n- *Voordat te tekenen does not exist: `alvorens te tekenen` or `voordat je tekent`.\n- Another subject: *Door de koers te dalen → `Doordat de koers daalde`.\n- *Hij spaart een huis te kopen → `om een huis te kopen`.\n- *zonder te overnemen → `zonder over te nemen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian often uses an adverbial participle (дієприслівник): `Переглянувши цифри, він вирішив продати`. Dutch has no such form: it uses `na` + `te hebben` + participle, or `door` / `zonder` + `te` + infinitive.","words":[{"nl":"aandeel","art":"het","pl":"aandelen","en":"share (in a company)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beurs","art":"de","pl":"beurzen","en":"stock exchange","pos":"n"},{"nl":"belegger","art":"de","pl":"beleggers","en":"investor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beleggen","past":"belegde","perf":"heeft belegd","en":"to invest","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verlies","art":"het","pl":"verliezen","en":"loss","pos":"n"},{"nl":"concurrent","art":"de","pl":"concurrenten","en":"competitor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"koers","art":"de","pl":"koersen","en":"share price / exchange rate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rendement","art":"het","pl":"rendementen","en":"return (on an investment)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezuinigen","past":"bezuinigde","perf":"heeft bezuinigd","en":"to cut costs / to economise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overnemen","past":"nam over","perf":"heeft overgenomen","en":"to take over (a company)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"onderneming","art":"de","pl":"ondernemingen","en":"business / enterprise","pos":"n"},{"nl":"winstgevend","en":"profitable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"failliet","en":"bankrupt","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"prijsverhoging","art":"de","pl":"prijsverhogingen","en":"price increase","pos":"n"},{"nl":"consument","art":"de","pl":"consumenten","en":"consumer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"inflatie","art":"de","pl":"","en":"inflation","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Door elke maand een vast bedrag te beleggen, spreid je het risico.","en":"By investing a fixed amount every month, you spread the risk.","hint":["door","vast","bedrag","beleggen","spreiden","risico"],"note":"door … te + infinitive says how: the doer is je, the subject of the main clause. The clause stands first, so the verb spreid comes next, then je (no t: spreid je).","alt":["Je spreidt het risico door elke maand een vast bedrag te beleggen."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf heeft de concurrent overgenomen zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken.","en":"The company took over the competitor without examining the risks properly.","hint":["bedrijf","concurrent","overnemen","zonder","risico","onderzoeken"],"note":"English -ing after without becomes zonder … te + infinitive. The clause comes after the final verb overgenomen; the object de risico's stands between zonder and te.","alt":["Het bedrijf nam de concurrent over zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken.","Zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken, heeft het bedrijf de concurrent overgenomen.","Zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken, nam het bedrijf de concurrent over."]},{"nl":"Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot de belegger zijn aandelen te verkopen.","en":"After looking at the figures, the investor decided to sell his shares.","hint":["na","cijfer","bekijken","besluiten","belegger","aandeel"],"note":"After na the looking is finished, so te hebben + participle. After besluiten om is optional: besloot (om) zijn aandelen te verkopen.","alt":["Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot de belegger zijn aandelen te verkopen.","Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot de belegger om zijn aandelen te verkopen.","Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot de belegger om zijn aandelen te verkopen."]},{"nl":"Na failliet te zijn gegaan, werd de onderneming door een concurrent overgenomen.","en":"After going bankrupt, the business was taken over by a competitor.","hint":["na","failliet","gaan","onderneming","concurrent","overnemen"],"note":"Gaan takes zijn in the perfect (is failliet gegaan), so the na-clause has te zijn. Its doer is de onderneming, the subject of the main clause.","alt":["Na failliet gegaan te zijn, werd de onderneming door een concurrent overgenomen.","Na failliet te zijn gegaan, werd de onderneming overgenomen door een concurrent.","Na failliet gegaan te zijn, werd de onderneming overgenomen door een concurrent.","Na failliet te zijn gegaan, is de onderneming door een concurrent overgenomen.","Na failliet gegaan te zijn, is de onderneming door een concurrent overgenomen.","Na failliet te zijn gegaan, is de onderneming overgenomen door een concurrent.","Na failliet gegaan te zijn, is de onderneming overgenomen door een concurrent."]},{"nl":"In plaats van te bezuinigen, leende de onderneming nog meer geld.","en":"Instead of cutting costs, the business borrowed even more money.","hint":["in plaats van","bezuinigen","onderneming","lenen","geld"],"note":"in plaats van … te + infinitive. The clause fills the first place, so leende comes straight after it and the subject follows.","alt":["De onderneming leende nog meer geld in plaats van te bezuinigen.","In plaats van te bezuinigen, heeft de onderneming nog meer geld geleend.","De onderneming heeft nog meer geld geleend in plaats van te bezuinigen."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf leende geld om een nieuwe fabriek te bouwen.","en":"The company borrowed money to build a new factory.","hint":["bedrijf","lenen","geld","nieuw","fabriek","bouwen"],"note":"Purpose (in order to) always needs om … te. Without om the sentence is wrong.","alt":["Het bedrijf heeft geld geleend om een nieuwe fabriek te bouwen."]},{"nl":"Door de inflatie is een eigen huis voor veel consumenten niet meer te betalen.","en":"Because of inflation, a home of their own is no longer affordable for many consumers.","hint":["door","inflatie","eigen","huis","consument","betalen"],"note":"zijn + te + infinitive means that something can (or here cannot) be done: is niet te betalen. Door here is an ordinary preposition before a noun, without te.","alt":["Een eigen huis is door de inflatie voor veel consumenten niet meer te betalen.","Door de inflatie is een eigen huis niet meer te betalen voor veel consumenten."]},{"nl":"Kleine beleggers hebben voorlopig niets te vrezen.","en":"Small investors have nothing to fear for the time being.","hint":["klein","belegger","voorlopig","niets","vrezen"],"note":"hebben + te + infinitive: have something to do (here: nothing to fear). The infinitive with te goes to the end.","alt":["Voorlopig hebben kleine beleggers niets te vrezen."]},{"nl":"Over de gevolgen van de overname valt nog weinig te zeggen.","en":"Little can be said yet about the consequences of the takeover.","hint":["gevolg","overname","vallen","weinig","zeggen"],"note":"vallen + te + infinitive: something can (hardly) be done. When another part stands first, er is optional; when nothing else stands first, er fills that place: Er valt nog weinig te zeggen.","alt":["Over de gevolgen van de overname valt er nog weinig te zeggen.","Er valt nog weinig te zeggen over de gevolgen van de overname.","Er valt over de gevolgen van de overname nog weinig te zeggen."]},{"nl":"De directeur verkocht zijn aandelen zonder dat de aandeelhouders het wisten.","en":"The director sold his shares without the shareholders knowing.","hint":["directeur","verkopen","aandeel","zonder dat","aandeelhouder","weten"],"note":"The knowing is done by the shareholders, not by the director, so zonder … te is impossible: it has to be zonder dat with a full sub-clause and the verb at the end.","alt":["De directeur heeft zijn aandelen verkocht zonder dat de aandeelhouders het wisten.","De directeur verkocht zijn aandelen zonder dat de aandeelhouders ervan wisten.","De directeur heeft zijn aandelen verkocht zonder dat de aandeelhouders ervan wisten."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de prijzen te verhogen, maakte het bedrijf meer winst.","options":["Doordat","Na","Door"],"correct":2,"en":"By raising its prices, the company made more profit.","note":"Door … te + infinitive: by doing. Doordat needs a full sub-clause with its own subject and verb, and na needs te hebben + participle (na de prijzen te hebben verhoogd)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het bedrijf leent geld ___ een nieuwe winkel te openen.","options":["om","–","voor"],"correct":0,"en":"The company is borrowing money to open a new shop.","note":"Purpose needs om … te. Leaving om out is wrong, and voor … te is Flemish, not standard Netherlands Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Voor dit advies hoeft u niets ___ te betalen.","options":["om","voor","–"],"correct":2,"en":"You (formal) do not have to pay anything for this advice.","note":"After hoeven (and schijnen, lijken, blijken) te follows directly; om is never used there."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na jarenlang verlies te ___ geleden, is de onderneming nu eindelijk winstgevend.","options":["zijn","hebben","worden"],"correct":1,"en":"After making a loss for years, the business is finally profitable now.","note":"Verlies lijden takes hebben in the perfect (heeft verlies geleden), so the na-clause has te hebben. Te zijn is for verbs that take zijn, such as gaan or vertrekken."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze willen de concurrent kopen zonder alle schulden ___ (overnemen).","answers":["over te nemen"],"en":"They want to buy the competitor without taking over all its debts.","note":"Overnemen is separable, so te goes between the parts: over te nemen, written as three words."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op spaargeld ___ (vallen) nauwelijks rendement te behalen.","answers":["valt","valt er"],"en":"Hardly any return can be made on savings.","note":"vallen + te + infinitive means that something can or cannot be done. The verb agrees with rendement, which is singular: valt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Door de prijsverhogingen is benzine voor veel gezinnen bijna niet ___ (betalen).","answers":["te betalen"],"en":"Because of the price increases, petrol is almost unaffordable for many families.","note":"zijn + te + infinitive: is niet te betalen, it cannot be paid. Without te (is niet betalen) the sentence is wrong."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De lage koersen van dit jaar geven ___ (denken).","answers":["te denken"],"en":"This year's low share prices give food for thought.","note":"The fixed expression is te denken geven: something makes you worried or thoughtful. Te comes before the infinitive."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zonder de risico's te kennen,","words":["heeft","hij","al zijn geld","op de beurs","belegd"],"answers":["Zonder de risico's te kennen, heeft hij al zijn geld op de beurs belegd.","Zonder de risico's te kennen, heeft hij op de beurs al zijn geld belegd.","Zonder de risico's te kennen, heeft hij al zijn geld belegd op de beurs."],"en":"Without knowing the risks, he invested all his money on the stock exchange.","note":"The zonder-clause fills the first place, so the conjugated verb heeft follows, then the subject hij. The participle belegd goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het bedrijf leende geld","words":["om","de concurrent","over","te","nemen"],"answers":["Het bedrijf leende geld om de concurrent over te nemen."],"en":"The company borrowed money to take over the competitor.","note":"Om opens the clause, the object follows, and the separable verb closes it with te between its parts: over te nemen."},{"type":"order","lead":"De koers daalde","words":["zonder dat","de beleggers","het","hadden","verwacht"],"answers":["De koers daalde zonder dat de beleggers het hadden verwacht.","De koers daalde zonder dat de beleggers het verwacht hadden."],"en":"The share price fell without the investors having expected it.","note":"The investors are not the subject of the main clause, so it must be zonder dat with a full sub-clause. Both verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with door … te, starting with Door. Leave out zo.","from":"Het bedrijf bezuinigde op personeel. Zo voorkwam het een groot verlies.","answers":["Door op personeel te bezuinigen, voorkwam het bedrijf een groot verlies.","Door te bezuinigen op personeel, voorkwam het bedrijf een groot verlies."],"en":"By cutting staff costs, the company prevented a big loss.","note":"The company does both things, so door … te works. The clause stands first, so voorkwam comes next and then het bedrijf, which replaces het."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use na … te hebben instead of the nadat-clause.","from":"Nadat de belegger het jaarverslag had gelezen, kocht hij extra aandelen.","answers":["Na het jaarverslag te hebben gelezen, kocht de belegger extra aandelen.","Na het jaarverslag gelezen te hebben, kocht de belegger extra aandelen."],"en":"After reading the annual report, the investor bought extra shares.","note":"The na-clause has no subject, so de belegger moves into the main clause. Lezen takes hebben; te hebben may stand before or after gelezen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace the voordat-clause with alvorens … te.","from":"Voordat u belegt, moet u de risico's kennen.","answers":["Alvorens te beleggen, moet u de risico's kennen."],"en":"Before investing, you (formal) need to know the risks.","note":"Alvorens … te is the formal short form of a voordat-clause with the same subject. Voordat itself cannot take te."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Door de koers te dalen, verloren veel beleggers geld.","answers":["Doordat de koers daalde, verloren veel beleggers geld.","Omdat de koers daalde, verloren veel beleggers geld."],"en":"Because the share price fell, many investors lost money.","note":"The price falls, the investors lose: two different subjects. Door … te is only possible when the doer is the subject of the main clause, so it must be a full sub-clause: Doordat de koers daalde."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Na de cijfers te bekijken, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","answers":["Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot hij om zijn aandelen te houden.","Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot hij om zijn aandelen te houden.","Nadat hij de cijfers had bekeken, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","Nadat hij de cijfers bekeken had, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden."],"en":"After looking at the figures, he decided to keep his shares.","note":"After na the action is finished, so Dutch uses te hebben + participle: na de cijfers te hebben bekeken. Na + te + a plain infinitive is not standard Dutch."}]},
  {"id":"b2-14","level":"B2","icon":"⚖️","title":"Law and justice","grammar":"Verbs with te: schijnen, blijken, lijken, hoeven, dienen","summary":"lijken, schijnen, blijken, hoeven, dienen, weten, komen + te + infinitive at the end; never om. hoeven only with niet, geen, alleen, pas. Perfect: is gebleken, heeft niet hoeven (te) betalen.","explain":"Some Dutch verbs take a second verb with `te` and add a judgement to it: how sure the speaker is, whether something is required, or how it turned out. They work like the modal verbs (the second verb goes to the end), but they need `te`, and never `om`.\n\n### Seeming and turning out\n\n| verb | meaning | how you know |\n| lijken te | to seem | your own impression |\n| schijnen te | apparently | you have heard or read it |\n| blijken te | to turn out | there is evidence now |\n| heten te | to be said to | formal, rare in speech |\n\n> De verdachte lijkt de waarheid te spreken. — The suspect seems to be telling the truth.\n> De getuige schijnt in het buitenland te wonen. — Apparently the witness lives abroad.\n> Het alibi bleek vals te zijn. — The alibi turned out to be false.\n\nFor something that happened earlier, use `te hebben` or `te zijn` + participle; the participle may also come first: `Ze schijnt te hebben bekend` or `Ze schijnt bekend te hebben`.\n\n`blijken` and `schijnen` also take a dat-clause after `het`: `Het blijkt dat …`, `Het schijnt dat …`; with `lijken` say `Het lijkt erop dat …`. And they can stand without a second verb: `Dat blijkt uit het dossier`, `naar het schijnt` (so it is said), `Zo lijkt het`.\n\n### Need and duty\n\n`hoeven te` is the negative of `moeten`: `Je moet tekenen`, `Je hoeft niet te tekenen`. It is only used with a negation or a limiting word: `niet`, `geen`, `niets`, `nooit`, `alleen`, `maar`, `pas`, `nauwelijks`. It also occurs in questions: `Hoef ik te tekenen?`\n\n> U hoeft alleen uw naam te noemen. — You only have to give your name.\n\n`dienen te` and `behoren te` are a formal 'must, ought to', in rules and official letters. `hebben te` is a stern 'have to'.\n\n> U dient zich te legitimeren. — You are required to show identification.\n> Je hebt je aan de wet te houden. — You have to obey the law.\n\n### Managing and ending up\n\n- `weten te`: to manage to. `De verdachte wist te ontsnappen`.\n- `zien te`: to try and manage, mostly after `moeten`: `We moeten zien te bewijzen dat hij er was`.\n- `komen te`: to end up, formal: `Hij kwam te overlijden` (he died); `De regel komt te vervallen` (the rule lapses).\n- `plegen te`: to be in the habit of, formal. Not the same as `een misdrijf plegen`, to commit a crime.\n\n### The perfect\n\nLearn only these frequent forms:\n\n- `blijken` takes **zijn**, mostly with dat or without a second verb: `Het is gebleken dat hij loog`.\n- `hoeven` stays an infinitive, with or without `te`; both are correct: `Ze heeft niet hoeven te betalen` or `Ze heeft niet hoeven betalen`.\n- `weten` also stays an infinitive: `Hij heeft weten te ontsnappen`.\n\nWith `lijken`, `schijnen` and `dienen` use the present or the simple past: `Hij leek te liegen`.\n\n### Word order\n\nMain clause: the te-verb stands second, `te` + infinitive goes last. Sub-clause: both go to the end, the te-verb first.\n\n> In de krant staat dat de verdachte onschuldig blijkt te zijn. — The paper says that the suspect turns out to be innocent.\n\nYou will also read the reverse order, `… dat je niet te komen hoeft`; it is correct, but less usual.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No negation, no `hoeven`: *Je hoeft te tekenen → `Je moet tekenen`.\n- When `hoeven` is the conjugated verb, standard Dutch keeps the `te`: *Je hoeft niet tekenen → `Je hoeft niet te tekenen`.\n- `Je moet niet tekenen` means 'do not sign'. 'You do not have to sign' is `Je hoeft niet te tekenen`.\n- No `om`: *Hij blijkt om schuldig te zijn → `Hij blijkt schuldig te zijn`.\n- *Het heeft gebleken → `Het is gebleken`.\n- English 'seems' is usually `lijken`. `schijnen` means you only know it from others.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian puts this in a separate word: `здається`, `кажуть`, `виявилося`, `не треба`. Dutch makes it the conjugated verb, with the subject in front: `Він виявився невинним` is `Hij bleek onschuldig te zijn`.","words":[{"nl":"rechter","art":"de","pl":"rechters","en":"judge","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rechtbank","art":"de","pl":"rechtbanken","en":"court (of law)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"advocaat","art":"de","pl":"advocaten","en":"lawyer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanklager","art":"de","pl":"aanklagers","en":"prosecutor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verdachte","art":"de","pl":"verdachten","en":"suspect","pos":"n"},{"nl":"slachtoffer","art":"het","pl":"slachtoffers","en":"victim","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bewijs","art":"het","pl":"bewijzen","en":"evidence / proof","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vonnis","art":"het","pl":"vonnissen","en":"verdict / judgment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"misdrijf","art":"het","pl":"misdrijven","en":"crime / criminal offence","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gevangenisstraf","art":"de","pl":"gevangenisstraffen","en":"prison sentence","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verplichting","art":"de","pl":"verplichtingen","en":"obligation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"veroordelen","past":"veroordeelde","perf":"heeft veroordeeld","en":"to convict / to sentence","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vrijspreken","past":"sprak vrij","perf":"heeft vrijgesproken","en":"to acquit","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"bekennen","past":"bekende","perf":"heeft bekend","en":"to confess","pos":"v"},{"nl":"onschuldig","en":"innocent","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"strafbaar","en":"punishable / a criminal offence","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Uit het vonnis blijkt dat de rechter de getuige niet geloofde.","en":"It emerges from the verdict that the judge did not believe the witness.","hint":["uit","vonnis","blijken","rechter","getuige","geloven"],"note":"blijken with a dat-clause instead of te: uit … blijkt dat … means that something can be seen from a document or fact. In the dat-clause the verb geloofde goes to the end.","alt":["Het blijkt uit het vonnis dat de rechter de getuige niet geloofde."]},{"nl":"Na drie maanden in de cel bleek de verdachte onschuldig te zijn.","en":"After three months in a cell, the suspect turned out to be innocent.","hint":["maand","cel","blijken","verdachte","onschuldig","zijn"],"note":"blijken te: there is now evidence. Bleek is the conjugated verb in second place, te zijn closes the sentence.","alt":["De verdachte bleek na drie maanden in de cel onschuldig te zijn.","Na drie maanden in de cel bleek de verdachte onschuldig.","De verdachte bleek na drie maanden in de cel onschuldig."]},{"nl":"De verdachte schijnt alles te hebben bekend.","en":"The suspect has apparently confessed everything. (schijnen)","hint":["verdachte","schijnen","alles","bekennen"],"note":"schijnen te: the speaker has heard it, but has not seen it. The confessing came earlier, so te hebben + participle; bekend te hebben is also correct.","alt":["De verdachte schijnt alles bekend te hebben.","Het schijnt dat de verdachte alles heeft bekend.","Het schijnt dat de verdachte alles bekend heeft.","Naar het schijnt heeft de verdachte alles bekend."]},{"nl":"De aanklager lijkt het bewijs niet sterk genoeg te vinden.","en":"The prosecutor does not seem to find the evidence strong enough.","hint":["aanklager","lijken","bewijs","sterk","genoeg","vinden"],"note":"lijken te gives the speaker's impression. Niet goes with what follows (sterk genoeg), and te vinden stands at the end.","alt":["Het lijkt erop dat de aanklager het bewijs niet sterk genoeg vindt."]},{"nl":"Als getuige hoeft u alleen de waarheid te vertellen.","en":"As a witness you (formal) only have to tell the truth.","hint":["als","getuige","hoeven","alleen","waarheid","vertellen"],"note":"hoeven needs a negation or a limiting word; here it is alleen. With u the verb has a t: u hoeft.","alt":["U hoeft als getuige alleen de waarheid te vertellen.","Als getuige hoeft u alleen de waarheid te spreken.","U hoeft als getuige alleen de waarheid te spreken."]},{"nl":"Dankzij de nieuwe wet heeft ze de boete niet hoeven te betalen.","en":"Thanks to the new law, she did not have to pay the fine.","hint":["dankzij","nieuw","wet","hoeven","boete","betalen"],"note":"In the perfect hoeven stays an infinitive instead of becoming a participle, and the te before betalen may also be left out: niet hoeven betalen.","alt":["Dankzij de nieuwe wet heeft ze de boete niet hoeven betalen.","Dankzij de nieuwe wet hoefde ze de boete niet te betalen.","Ze heeft dankzij de nieuwe wet de boete niet hoeven te betalen.","Ze heeft dankzij de nieuwe wet de boete niet hoeven betalen.","Ze hoefde dankzij de nieuwe wet de boete niet te betalen.","Dankzij de nieuwe wet heeft zij de boete niet hoeven te betalen.","Dankzij de nieuwe wet heeft zij de boete niet hoeven betalen.","Dankzij de nieuwe wet hoefde zij de boete niet te betalen."]},{"nl":"Bezoekers dienen zich bij de ingang van de rechtbank te legitimeren.","en":"Visitors are required to show identification at the entrance of the court.","hint":["bezoeker","dienen","ingang","rechtbank","zich legitimeren"],"note":"dienen te is the formal must of rules and notices. The reflexive zich comes straight after dienen, te legitimeren goes to the end.","alt":["Bij de ingang van de rechtbank dienen bezoekers zich te legitimeren.","Bezoekers moeten zich bij de ingang van de rechtbank legitimeren.","Bij de ingang van de rechtbank moeten bezoekers zich legitimeren."]},{"nl":"Iedereen heeft zich aan de wet te houden, ook de politie.","en":"Everyone has to obey the law, the police included.","hint":["iedereen","hebben","zich houden aan","wet","politie"],"note":"hebben te: a firm 'has to'. It is heeft (iedereen is singular), and te houden goes to the end of the clause.","alt":["Iedereen moet zich aan de wet houden, ook de politie.","Iedereen dient zich aan de wet te houden, ook de politie."]},{"nl":"De verdachte wist tijdens het transport naar de rechtbank te ontsnappen.","en":"The suspect managed to escape during transport to the court.","hint":["verdachte","weten","tijdens","transport","rechtbank","ontsnappen"],"note":"weten te means to manage to, not to know. Wist is the simple past; in the perfect it is heeft weten te ontsnappen.","alt":["Tijdens het transport naar de rechtbank wist de verdachte te ontsnappen.","De verdachte heeft tijdens het transport naar de rechtbank weten te ontsnappen.","Tijdens het transport naar de rechtbank heeft de verdachte weten te ontsnappen."]},{"nl":"De rechter vond het opvallend dat de verdachte geen alibi bleek te hebben.","en":"The judge found it striking that the suspect turned out to have no alibi.","hint":["rechter","vinden","opvallend","verdachte","alibi","blijken"],"note":"In a sub-clause bleek and te hebben both go to the end, bleek first.","alt":["De rechter vond het opmerkelijk dat de verdachte geen alibi bleek te hebben.","De rechter vond het opvallend dat de verdachte geen alibi te hebben bleek."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Je ___ niet te tekenen: het is geen verplichting.","options":["moet","hoeft","dient"],"correct":1,"en":"You do not have to sign: it is not an obligation.","note":"'Not have to' is niet hoeven te. Moet never takes te, and dient niet te means 'should not', which does not fit 'it is not an obligation'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Uit de camerabeelden ___ dat de verdachte op de plaats van het misdrijf was.","options":["schijnt","lijkt","blijkt"],"correct":2,"en":"The camera footage shows that the suspect was at the scene of the crime.","note":"The footage is evidence, and uit … blijkt dat is the fixed way to say that something follows from it. Schijnen is for hearsay and lijken for an impression; neither goes with uit."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het ___ gebleken dat de getuige had gelogen.","options":["heeft","is","werd"],"correct":1,"en":"It has emerged that the witness had lied.","note":"blijken takes zijn in the perfect: het is gebleken. Heeft gebleken is a common mistake."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De advocaat wist ___ de rechter te overtuigen.","options":["om","–","er"],"correct":1,"en":"The lawyer managed to convince the judge.","note":"weten te (to manage to) takes te directly, like blijken, schijnen and hoeven; om is never used with these verbs."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tijdens het proces ___ (blijken) de man al twee keer eerder veroordeeld te zijn.","answers":["bleek"],"en":"During the trial the man turned out to have been convicted twice before.","note":"A story in the past, so the simple past bleek. Veroordeeld te zijn is the passive of veroordelen with te: he had been convicted."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na het hoger beroep heeft hij de gevangenisstraf niet hoeven ___ (uitzitten).","answers":["uit te zitten","uitzitten"],"en":"After the appeal he did not have to serve the prison sentence.","note":"After the infinitive hoeven in the perfect, te is optional: uit te zitten or uitzitten. With te, the separable verb takes it between its parts."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De oude regeling komt per 1 januari ___ (vervallen).","answers":["te vervallen"],"en":"The old arrangement will lapse on 1 January.","note":"komen te + infinitive is a formal 'end up': komt te vervallen, the arrangement stops being valid. Te is required."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Als burger heb je je aan de wet ___ (houden).","answers":["te houden"],"en":"As a citizen you have to obey the law.","note":"hebben te + infinitive is a firm 'have to'. Without te the sentence would be wrong."},{"type":"order","lead":"Volgens de krant","words":["schijnt","de rechter","de verdachte","te hebben","vrijgesproken"],"answers":["Volgens de krant schijnt de rechter de verdachte te hebben vrijgesproken.","Volgens de krant schijnt de rechter de verdachte vrijgesproken te hebben."],"en":"According to the newspaper, the judge has apparently acquitted the suspect.","note":"Volgens de krant fills the first place, so schijnt comes next, then the subject. The acquittal came earlier, so te hebben + participle, in either order."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het is opvallend","words":["dat","het slachtoffer","zich","niets","blijkt","te herinneren"],"answers":["Het is opvallend dat het slachtoffer zich niets blijkt te herinneren.","Het is opvallend dat het slachtoffer zich niets te herinneren blijkt."],"en":"It is striking that the victim turns out to remember nothing.","note":"In the dat-clause the verbs go to the end; blijkt te herinneren is the usual order, te herinneren blijkt is correct but less common. Zich comes straight after the subject."},{"type":"order","lead":"De advocaat zegt","words":["dat","zijn cliënt","niet","hoeft","te getuigen"],"answers":["De advocaat zegt dat zijn cliënt niet hoeft te getuigen.","De advocaat zegt dat zijn cliënt niet te getuigen hoeft."],"en":"The lawyer says that his client does not have to give evidence.","note":"Hoeft needs its negation niet, which stands before the verbs at the end of the sub-clause. Hoeft te getuigen is the usual order; te getuigen hoeft is also correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Rewrite with blijken + te, starting with De verdachte.","from":"Het blijkt dat de verdachte onschuldig is.","answers":["De verdachte blijkt onschuldig te zijn."],"en":"The suspect turns out to be innocent.","note":"The subject of the dat-clause becomes the subject of blijken, and is becomes te zijn at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative with hoeven.","from":"U moet een advocaat nemen.","answers":["U hoeft geen advocaat te nemen."],"en":"You (formal) do not have to get a lawyer.","note":"The negative of moeten is niet hoeven. Een advocaat becomes geen advocaat, and hoeven needs te before the infinitive."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"De verdachte hoefde niet te getuigen.","answers":["De verdachte heeft niet hoeven te getuigen.","De verdachte heeft niet hoeven getuigen."],"en":"The suspect did not have to give evidence.","note":"The perfect is heeft + the infinitive hoeven, not a participle. After it te is optional, so both answers are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het heeft gebleken dat zijn gedrag niet strafbaar was.","answers":["Het is gebleken dat zijn gedrag niet strafbaar was."],"en":"It has emerged that his behaviour was not a criminal offence.","note":"blijken takes zijn in the perfect: het is gebleken."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Je hoeft dit formulier te ondertekenen.","answers":["Je moet dit formulier ondertekenen.","Je dient dit formulier te ondertekenen.","Je hebt dit formulier te ondertekenen."],"en":"You have to sign this form.","note":"hoeven is only used with a negation or a limiting word such as alleen. Without one, 'have to' is moeten (without te), or formally dienen te."}]},
  {"id":"b2-15","level":"B2","icon":"🔐","title":"Privacy online","grammar":"Negation in detail: scope, niet meer, nog geen, nauwelijks","summary":"niet negates what follows it. al → nog niet, nog → niet meer, al een → nog geen, ooit → nog nooit. One negation per clause: niemand … iets. nauwelijks, amper, zelden never take a second niet.","explain":"Unit A2 · 7 gave the basics: `geen` for a noun with `een` or without an article, `niet` for the rest. At B2 the questions are what exactly is negated, and how negation combines with `al`, `nog`, `meer` and words like `nauwelijks`.\n\n### What niet negates\n\n`niet` negates what follows it. Its normal place is late in the clause: before the final verbs, a separable prefix, an adjective after `zijn`, and most phrases with a preposition. Put it earlier, directly before one part, and only that part is negated.\n\n> Ik heb niet alle voorwaarden gelezen. — I have not read all the terms (only some).\n> Ik heb geen van de voorwaarden gelezen. — I have not read any of the terms.\n> Hij logt niet vaak in. — He does not log in often.\n> Hij logt vaak niet in. — He often does not log in.\n\nAvoid `alle … niet` (Ik heb alle voorwaarden niet gelezen): it is unclear whether it means none or not all. To correct one part, use `niet … maar …`; to add one, `niet alleen … maar ook …`.\n\n> Het probleem is niet de app, maar de gebruiker. — The problem is not the app but the user.\n\nAfter `zijn` and `worden` the A2 rule still holds: a noun with `een` takes `geen` (`niet een` only stresses 'not one' or sets up a contrast); an adjective, or a noun with `de` or `het`, takes `niet`: `Dat is geen goed idee`, `Dat is niet goed`, `Dat is niet het probleem`.\n\n### al, nog and meer\n\n| positive | negative |\n| al (already) | nog niet (not yet) |\n| al een | nog geen |\n| nog (still) | niet meer (no longer) |\n| nog een, nog … | geen … meer |\n| al eens, ooit | nog nooit |\n\n> Ik heb nog geen back-up. — I do not have a backup yet.\n> Ik bewaar geen wachtwoorden meer op papier. — I no longer keep any passwords on paper.\n\nNote that `meer` comes **after** the noun: `geen tijd meer`. Stronger forms: `nog steeds niet` (still not), `nooit meer` (never again), `helemaal niet` (not at all), `absoluut geen`, and `geen enkel` + het-word, `geen enkele` + de-word (not a single): `geen enkel bedrijf`, `geen enkele app`.\n\n### Half-negative words\n\n`nauwelijks` and `amper` (hardly), `bijna niet` (almost not), `zelden` (rarely), `weinig` (little) and `slechts` (only) are negative enough: never add `niet`. After `nauwelijks` and `amper` use `iets`, `iemand`, `ooit`, not niets or niemand; `bijna` takes the negative word itself: `bijna niets`, `bijna niemand`. `hoeven` also goes with them (unit B2 · 14).\n\n> Ik heb nauwelijks iets gemerkt. — I hardly noticed anything.\n> Je hoeft amper iets in te vullen. — You hardly have to fill anything in.\n\n### One negation per clause\n\nAfter one negative word the rest of the clause uses the positive forms `iets`, `iemand`, `ooit`, `ergens`.\n\n> Niemand heeft iets gemerkt. — Nobody noticed anything.\n> Ik heb nooit iemand mijn pincode gegeven. — I have never given anyone my PIN.\n\nTwo negative words together cancel each other out and make a careful positive: `niet onaardig` (quite nice), `niet zonder risico` (somewhat risky), `niet onmogelijk` (possible, though hard).\n\n### Words that need a negation\n\nSome words only work in a negative sentence, a question, or after `als` or `zonder (dat)`: `ook maar iets` (anything at all), `ook maar één`, `hoeven`, and `meer` in the sense 'any more'.\n\n> Niemand heeft ook maar één klacht ingediend. — Nobody made even a single complaint.\n\n### Answering a negative question\n\nTo a question with `niet` or `geen`, `nee` agrees with the negation. To contradict it, say `jawel` or `toch wel`, or add `wel` to the answer; a plain `ja` is unclear.\n\n> Heb je geen virusscanner? Jawel, die heb ik wel. — Don't you have a virus scanner? Yes, I do.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Niemand heeft niets gezien → `Niemand heeft iets gezien`.\n- *Ik heb nauwelijks niet geslapen → `Ik heb nauwelijks geslapen`.\n- *Ik heb geen meer tijd → `Ik heb geen tijd meer`.\n- *Het systeem is geen veilig → `Het systeem is niet veilig`: an adjective takes `niet`.\n- `niet vaak` and `vaak niet` are not the same.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian stacks negations: `Ніхто нічого не бачив`. Dutch allows one: `Niemand heeft iets gezien`. Every extra negative word in Dutch counts, and two of them cancel out.","words":[{"nl":"persoonsgegevens","art":"de","pl":"","en":"personal data","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"toestemming","art":"de","pl":"","en":"consent / permission","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"beveiliging","art":"de","pl":"beveiligingen","en":"security / protection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"datalek","art":"het","pl":"datalekken","en":"data breach","pos":"n"},{"nl":"inbreuk","art":"de","pl":"inbreuken","en":"breach / infringement","pos":"n"},{"nl":"misbruik","art":"het","pl":"","en":"misuse / abuse","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"dienstverlener","art":"de","pl":"dienstverleners","en":"service provider","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toezichthouder","art":"de","pl":"toezichthouders","en":"regulator / supervisory body","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tweestapsverificatie","art":"de","pl":"","en":"two-step verification","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"inzage","art":"de","pl":"","en":"access (to see a file) / inspection","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"versleutelen","past":"versleutelde","perf":"heeft versleuteld","en":"to encrypt","pos":"v"},{"nl":"gebruiker","art":"de","pl":"gebruikers","en":"user","pos":"n"},{"nl":"traceren","past":"traceerde","perf":"heeft getraceerd","en":"to trace / to track down","pos":"v"},{"nl":"openbaar","en":"public","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"vertrouwelijk","en":"confidential","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"kwetsbaar","en":"vulnerable","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik heb niet alle voorwaarden gelezen, alleen de eerste pagina.","en":"I have not read all the terms, only the first page.","hint":["niet","alle","voorwaarde","lezen","alleen","pagina"],"note":"Niet stands directly before alle voorwaarden, so it negates 'all': I read some of them. For 'none of them' say geen van de voorwaarden.","alt":["Ik heb niet alle voorwaarden gelezen, maar alleen de eerste pagina."]},{"nl":"De meeste gebruikers veranderen hun wachtwoord niet vaak.","en":"Most users do not change their password often.","hint":["meeste","gebruiker","veranderen","wachtwoord","vaak"],"note":"Niet vaak: it happens rarely. Vaak niet would mean something else: often they do not change it (when they should).","alt":["De meeste gebruikers wijzigen hun wachtwoord niet vaak."]},{"nl":"Niemand heeft ons ook maar iets over het datalek verteld.","en":"Nobody told us anything at all about the data breach.","hint":["niemand","ook maar iets","datalek","vertellen"],"note":"Niemand is the one negation of the clause, so the rest is positive: iets, not niets. Ook maar strengthens it and only works with a negation.","alt":["Niemand heeft ons ook maar iets verteld over het datalek.","Niemand vertelde ons ook maar iets over het datalek."]},{"nl":"Voor deze app hoef je nauwelijks persoonsgegevens in te vullen.","en":"For this app you hardly have to enter any personal data.","hint":["app","hoeven","nauwelijks","persoonsgegevens","invullen"],"note":"Nauwelijks is enough negation for hoeven; adding niet would be wrong. English 'any' has no Dutch word here.","alt":["Je hoeft voor deze app nauwelijks persoonsgegevens in te vullen."]},{"nl":"Ik heb die dienstverlener nooit toestemming gegeven om mijn gegevens te delen.","en":"I never gave that service provider permission to share my data.","hint":["dienstverlener","nooit","toestemming","geven","gegevens","delen"],"note":"Nooit is the one negation of the clause, so toestemming stays without geen: nooit geen toestemming would be a second negation.","alt":["Ik gaf die dienstverlener nooit toestemming om mijn gegevens te delen."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf gebruikt nog steeds geen tweestapsverificatie.","en":"The company still does not use two-step verification.","hint":["bedrijf","gebruiken","nog steeds","tweestapsverificatie"],"note":"A noun without an article takes geen; nog steeds before it means 'still not', after a long time.","alt":["Het bedrijf gebruikt nog altijd geen tweestapsverificatie."]},{"nl":"Na het datalek gebruik ik die webwinkel niet meer.","en":"After the data breach I no longer use that online shop.","hint":["datalek","gebruiken","webwinkel","niet meer"],"note":"The negative of nog (still) is niet meer, with niet first. It goes at the end because there is no final verb.","alt":["Ik gebruik die webwinkel na het datalek niet meer."]},{"nl":"Dat is geen veilige manier om wachtwoorden te bewaren.","en":"That is not a safe way to store passwords.","hint":["veilig","manier","wachtwoord","bewaren"],"note":"After zijn a noun with een takes geen: een veilige manier becomes geen veilige manier. Niet would only fit an adjective alone: dat is niet veilig.","alt":["Dat is geen veilige manier om wachtwoorden op te slaan."]},{"nl":"Een openbaar wifinetwerk gebruiken is niet zonder risico.","en":"Using a public wifi network is not without risk.","hint":["openbaar","wifinetwerk","gebruiken","zonder","risico"],"note":"Niet + zonder: two negatives cancel out and give a careful positive: there is a real risk. Openbaar has no -e after een before a het-word.","alt":["Het gebruik van een openbaar wifinetwerk is niet zonder risico."]},{"nl":"Heb je nog nooit een verdachte e-mail gekregen?","en":"Have you never had a suspicious e-mail?","hint":["nog nooit","verdacht","e-mail","krijgen"],"note":"Nog nooit means 'never so far', the negative of ooit or al eens. Nooit is the negation, so een stays een.","alt":["Heb jij nog nooit een verdachte e-mail gekregen?","Heb je nog nooit een verdachte e-mail ontvangen?","Heb jij nog nooit een verdachte e-mail ontvangen?","Heb je nooit een verdachte e-mail gekregen?","Heb jij nooit een verdachte e-mail gekregen?","Heb je nooit een verdachte e-mail ontvangen?","Heb jij nooit een verdachte e-mail ontvangen?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Vertrouwelijke bestanden mailen zonder ze te versleutelen is ___ goed idee.","options":["niet","geen","niets"],"correct":1,"en":"E-mailing confidential files without encrypting them is not a good idea.","note":"After zijn, a noun with een is negated with geen: een goed idee becomes geen goed idee."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na de update is het systeem ___ kwetsbaar.","options":["geen","geen meer","niet meer"],"correct":2,"en":"After the update the system is no longer vulnerable.","note":"Kwetsbaar is an adjective, so niet; 'no longer' is niet meer. Geen is only for nouns."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb nauwelijks ___ van de update gemerkt.","options":["niets","iets","niks"],"correct":1,"en":"I hardly noticed anything of the update.","note":"Nauwelijks is already negative, so the rest of the clause uses the positive iets. Niets and niks would be a second negation."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Heb je geen extra beveiliging op je account? ___, ik gebruik tweestapsverificatie.","options":["Nee","Jawel","Geen"],"correct":1,"en":"Don't you have extra security on your account? Yes, I do: I use two-step verification.","note":"To contradict a negative question, Dutch says jawel. Nee would agree with the question: no, I don't have any."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Kan de politie de hacker al traceren? Nee, ze kunnen hem ___ traceren.","answers":["nog niet"],"en":"Can the police trace the hacker yet? No, they cannot trace him yet.","note":"The negative of al (already) is nog niet (not yet). Niet alone would lose the meaning 'yet'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heb je nog een back-up van die foto's? Nee, ik heb ___ back-up meer.","answers":["geen"],"en":"Do you still have a backup of those photos? No, I no longer have a backup.","note":"Nog een becomes geen … meer: geen before the noun, meer after it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Er is ___ enkele aanwijzing voor misbruik van je gegevens.","answers":["geen"],"en":"There is no indication at all of misuse of your data.","note":"Geen enkel(e) means 'not a single'. Aanwijzing is a de-word, so enkele with an e."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zonder dat ik ook maar ___ had aangeklikt, was mijn account gehackt.","answers":["iets"],"en":"Without my having clicked on anything at all, my account had been hacked.","note":"Ook maar iets is used after zonder (dat), negations and in questions. Zonder is the negative part, so iets, not niets."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik heb","words":["niet","alle cookies","geaccepteerd,","alleen de noodzakelijke"],"answers":["Ik heb niet alle cookies geaccepteerd, alleen de noodzakelijke."],"en":"I did not accept all the cookies, only the necessary ones.","note":"Niet goes directly before alle cookies because only 'all' is negated: some were accepted. For 'none' Dutch says geen enkele cookie."},{"type":"order","lead":"De app verzamelt","words":["niet alleen","je locatie,","maar ook","je contacten"],"answers":["De app verzamelt niet alleen je locatie, maar ook je contacten."],"en":"The app collects not only your location but also your contacts.","note":"Niet alleen stands directly before the first thing, maar ook before the second, in the order of the English."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gelukkig heeft","words":["niemand","iets","van het datalek","gemerkt"],"answers":["Gelukkig heeft niemand iets van het datalek gemerkt.","Gelukkig heeft niemand van het datalek iets gemerkt."],"en":"Fortunately nobody noticed anything of the data breach.","note":"Niemand is the only negation; the object is the positive iets. Iets may stand before or after van het datalek."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative: you have not had it so far.","from":"Ik heb al inzage gekregen in mijn dossier.","answers":["Ik heb nog geen inzage gekregen in mijn dossier.","Ik heb nog geen inzage in mijn dossier gekregen."],"en":"I have not yet been given access to my file.","note":"A noun without an article takes geen, and al becomes nog: al inzage becomes nog geen inzage."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use nauwelijks instead of bijna niets.","from":"Ik heb bijna niets over het datalek gelezen.","answers":["Ik heb nauwelijks iets over het datalek gelezen."],"en":"I have hardly read anything about the data breach.","note":"Nauwelijks is the negative part, so niets becomes the positive iets: nauwelijks iets."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Answer with Nee and a full sentence with nooit.","from":"Heb je ooit iemand je wachtwoord gegeven?","answers":["Nee, ik heb nooit iemand mijn wachtwoord gegeven.","Nee, ik heb nog nooit iemand mijn wachtwoord gegeven."],"en":"No, I have never given anyone my password.","note":"Ooit becomes nooit, but iemand stays iemand: one negation per clause. Je wachtwoord becomes mijn wachtwoord in the answer."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Niemand had niets gemerkt van de inbreuk op het systeem.","answers":["Niemand had iets gemerkt van de inbreuk op het systeem.","Niemand had iets van de inbreuk op het systeem gemerkt."],"en":"Nobody had noticed anything of the breach of the system.","note":"Dutch allows one negation per clause. After niemand the object is iets; niemand … niets would cancel out."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De toezichthouder heeft nauwelijks niet gereageerd op de klachten.","answers":["De toezichthouder heeft nauwelijks gereageerd op de klachten.","De toezichthouder heeft bijna niet gereageerd op de klachten.","De toezichthouder heeft nauwelijks op de klachten gereageerd.","De toezichthouder heeft amper gereageerd op de klachten."],"en":"The regulator has hardly responded to the complaints.","note":"Nauwelijks is already negative; it never takes a second niet."}]},
  {"id":"b2-16","level":"B2","icon":"🏥","title":"Health care","grammar":"Agreement: singular or plural verb?","summary":"een aantal / de helft van + plural noun: singular or plural verb. Groups (de regering, het personeel), iedereen, niemand, elk: singular. Two subjects with en: plural. Het zijn, dat zijn + plural. u bent.","explain":"Normally the verb simply follows the subject: one person or thing, singular; more than one, plural. This unit is about the cases where that is not obvious: words for an amount, groups, `iedereen`, two subjects, `u`, and `het zijn`.\n\n### Quantity words: een aantal, de helft, tien procent\n\nWith `een aantal`, `een groep`, `een reeks`, `een deel van`, `de helft van`, `tien procent van` and `een meerderheid van`, look at the noun that follows.\n\n| noun that follows | verb | example |\n| singular | singular | De helft van het geld gaat … |\n| plural | singular or plural | Een aantal artsen is / zijn … |\n\nWith a plural noun both are correct standard Dutch: the singular follows the grammar (one number), the plural follows the meaning (several doctors). After `een aantal` the plural is the more common choice.\n\n> Een aantal patiënten wachten al maanden. — A number of patients have been waiting for months.\n> Tien procent van de bevolking heeft een chronische ziekte. — Ten per cent of the population has a chronic illness.\n\n`een paar` (a few) is always plural: `Een paar huisartsen zijn gestopt`. So are `de meeste`, `sommige`, `alle`, `veel` and `beide` + plural noun: `De meeste verzekeraars verhogen de premie`.\n\n### Groups are singular\n\n`de politie`, `de regering`, `het kabinet`, `het team`, `het personeel`, `het publiek`, `de familie`: one group, so a singular verb, even where English often uses a plural.\n\n> Het personeel heeft te weinig tijd. — The staff have too little time.\n\nIn speech the next clause often goes on with `ze` or `hun` (`De regering zegt dat ze …`); careful writing uses `zij`, `haar` or `het`, `zijn` (unit B2 · 17).\n\n### iedereen, niemand, elk, ieder, men\n\nAlways singular: `Iedereen heeft recht op zorg`, `Niemand weet het`, `Elke patiënt krijgt een brief`, `Men zegt dat …`.\n\n### Two subjects\n\n- With `en`: plural. `De huisarts en de specialist overleggen`.\n- With `of`: two singular nouns usually take a singular verb: `De huisarts of de assistent belt u terug`; the plural also occurs. If one of them is plural, the verb is plural: `De huisarts of zijn assistenten bellen u terug`. `noch … noch` works the same way.\n- With `zowel … als`: strict grammar gives a singular verb for two singular nouns, but in practice the plural is more common. Both are correct.\n\n> Zowel de patiënt als de arts willen duidelijkheid. — Both the patient and the doctor want clarity.\n\n### u and je\n\n`u bent`, never *u is. With `hebben`, `kunnen`, `willen` and `zullen` both forms are correct: `u hebt` / `u heeft`, `u kunt` / `u kan`, `u wilt` / `u wil`, `u zult` / `u zal`; `u kan`, `u wil` and `u zal` are a little less formal. `je` directly after the verb loses the t as usual: `Heb je`, `Ben je`, `Kun je`.\n\n### het, dat, dit + zijn + a plural\n\nWhen `het`, `dat` or `dit` points at a plural noun after `zijn`, the verb is plural: `Het zijn mijn collega's`, `Dat zijn de regels`, `Dit zijn de kosten`. The same with `er zijn` + plural.\n\n> Dit zijn de behandelingen die worden vergoed. — These are the treatments that are reimbursed.\n\n### een van de … die\n\n> Ze is een van de artsen die 's nachts werken. — She is one of the doctors who work at night.\n\nThe relative clause is plural, because `die` refers to `de artsen`. This plural is the usual choice; the singular (`die 's nachts werkt`) also occurs and is not wrong. The main verb stays singular: `Een van de artsen werkt 's nachts`.\n\n### One amount, one title\n\nAn amount seen as one whole, and a title, take a singular verb: `Drie weken is lang`, `Vijftig euro is veel geld`, `'De Avonden' is een roman`.\n\n> Twee jaar is te lang om op een operatie te wachten. — Two years is too long to wait for an operation.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *De regering willen → `De regering wil`.\n- *Iedereen hebben → `Iedereen heeft`.\n- *Er is veel patiënten → `Er zijn veel patiënten`.\n- *U is → `U bent`.\n- English 'the staff are' is `het personeel is`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian also allows both after a quantity: `Кілька пацієнтів чекає` or `чекають`. Dutch `een aantal` behaves much the same. A group noun such as `de regering` takes a singular verb in both languages: `уряд вирішив`.","words":[{"nl":"zorgverzekeraar","art":"de","pl":"zorgverzekeraars","en":"health insurer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"basisverzekering","art":"de","pl":"basisverzekeringen","en":"basic health insurance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zorgstelsel","art":"het","pl":"zorgstelsels","en":"health-care system","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zorgverlener","art":"de","pl":"zorgverleners","en":"care provider / health-care worker","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zorgkosten","art":"de","pl":"","en":"health-care costs","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"wachtlijst","art":"de","pl":"wachtlijsten","en":"waiting list","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verwijzing","art":"de","pl":"verwijzingen","en":"referral","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ingreep","art":"de","pl":"ingrepen","en":"(medical) procedure / operation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"mantelzorger","art":"de","pl":"mantelzorgers","en":"informal carer (family member or friend)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verpleeghuis","art":"het","pl":"verpleeghuizen","en":"nursing home","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vergrijzing","art":"de","pl":"","en":"ageing of the population","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"personeelstekort","art":"het","pl":"personeelstekorten","en":"staff shortage","pos":"n"},{"nl":"doorverwijzen","past":"verwees door","perf":"heeft doorverwezen","en":"to refer (to a specialist)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"chronisch","en":"chronic","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"overbelast","en":"overloaded / overworked","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"toegankelijk","en":"accessible","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Een aantal patiënten staat al maanden op de wachtlijst.","en":"A number of patients have been on the waiting list for months.","hint":["aantal","patiënt","staan","maand","wachtlijst"],"note":"After een aantal + a plural noun the verb may be singular (staat, following aantal) or plural (staan, following patiënten). Both are correct.","alt":["Een aantal patiënten staan al maanden op de wachtlijst."]},{"nl":"De helft van het budget gaat naar de zorg voor ouderen.","en":"Half of the budget goes to care for the elderly.","hint":["helft","budget","gaan","zorg","oudere"],"note":"De helft van + a singular noun (het budget) takes a singular verb: gaat. Only with a plural noun would gaan also be possible.","alt":["De helft van het budget gaat naar de ouderenzorg."]},{"nl":"Het personeel van het verpleeghuis heeft te weinig tijd voor de bewoners.","en":"The staff of the nursing home have too little time for the residents.","hint":["personeel","verpleeghuis","hebben","weinig","tijd","bewoner"],"note":"Personeel is one group, so the verb is singular: heeft. English may say 'the staff have', Dutch never says personeel hebben.","alt":["Het personeel in het verpleeghuis heeft te weinig tijd voor de bewoners."]},{"nl":"Iedereen in Nederland is verplicht een basisverzekering af te sluiten.","en":"Everyone in the Netherlands is obliged to take out basic health insurance.","hint":["iedereen","Nederland","verplicht","basisverzekering","afsluiten"],"note":"Iedereen is always singular: iedereen is, not iedereen zijn, although it means many people.","alt":["Iedereen in Nederland is verplicht om een basisverzekering af te sluiten."]},{"nl":"Zowel de huisarts als de specialist vonden een operatie niet nodig.","en":"Both the GP and the specialist thought an operation was unnecessary.","hint":["zowel … als","huisarts","specialist","vinden","operatie","nodig"],"note":"With zowel … als and two singular nouns, the plural (vonden) is the more common choice; the singular vond is also correct.","alt":["Zowel de huisarts als de specialist vond een operatie niet nodig.","Zowel de huisarts als de specialist vonden dat een operatie niet nodig was.","Zowel de huisarts als de specialist vond dat een operatie niet nodig was.","Zowel de huisarts als de specialist vonden een operatie onnodig.","Zowel de huisarts als de specialist vond een operatie onnodig."]},{"nl":"Hebt u al een verwijzing van uw huisarts?","en":"Do you (formal) already have a referral from your GP?","hint":["hebben","al","verwijzing","huisarts"],"note":"With u both hebt and heeft are correct. With zijn there is no choice: u bent.","alt":["Heeft u al een verwijzing van uw huisarts?"]},{"nl":"Dit zijn de behandelingen die uw zorgverzekeraar vergoedt.","en":"These are the treatments that your (formal) health insurer pays for.","hint":["dit","zijn","behandeling","zorgverzekeraar","vergoeden"],"note":"Dit points at a plural noun, so the verb is zijn. In the relative clause the subject is uw zorgverzekeraar, so vergoedt is singular, with dt.","alt":["Dit zijn de behandelingen die je zorgverzekeraar vergoedt."]},{"nl":"Zij is een van de artsen die zich zorgen maken over het personeelstekort.","en":"She is one of the doctors who are worried about the staff shortage.","hint":["een van","arts","zich zorgen maken","personeelstekort"],"note":"Die refers to de artsen, so the relative clause is usually plural: maken (the singular maakt also occurs). The main clause has zij as subject: is.","alt":["Ze is een van de artsen die zich zorgen maken over het personeelstekort.","Zij is een van de artsen die zich over het personeelstekort zorgen maken.","Ze is een van de artsen die zich over het personeelstekort zorgen maken.","Zij is een van de artsen die zich zorgen maakt over het personeelstekort.","Ze is een van de artsen die zich zorgen maakt over het personeelstekort.","Zij is een van de artsen die zich over het personeelstekort zorgen maakt.","Ze is een van de artsen die zich over het personeelstekort zorgen maakt."]},{"nl":"Drie maanden is te lang om op een ingreep te wachten.","en":"Three months is too long to wait for an operation.","hint":["maand","lang","ingreep","wachten"],"note":"Drie maanden is one period of time here, so the verb is singular, as in English.","alt":["Drie maanden is te lang om op een operatie te wachten."]},{"nl":"De meeste mantelzorgers krijgen geen vergoeding voor hun werk.","en":"Most informal carers get no payment for their work.","hint":["meeste","mantelzorger","krijgen","vergoeding","werk"],"note":"De meeste + a plural noun always takes a plural verb: krijgen. Unlike een aantal, there is no choice here.","alt":["De meeste mantelzorgers ontvangen geen vergoeding voor hun werk."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het team van de huisartsenpost ___ elke nacht dienst.","options":["hebben","heeft","heb"],"correct":1,"en":"The team at the out-of-hours GP service is on duty every night.","note":"Het team is one group, so the verb is singular: heeft. A group noun does not take a plural verb in Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Iedereen ___ recht op goede zorg.","options":["hebben","hebt","heeft"],"correct":2,"en":"Everyone has the right to good care.","note":"Iedereen is singular, like hij: iedereen heeft."},{"type":"choose","nl":"U ___ verplicht om een wijziging aan uw zorgverzekeraar door te geven.","options":["bent","is","zijn"],"correct":0,"en":"You (formal) are obliged to report a change to your health insurer.","note":"With zijn, u always takes bent. U is is an old form that is no longer used; the choice between two forms only exists with hebben, kunnen, willen and zullen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Dat ___ de nieuwe regels voor de basisverzekering.","options":["is","bent","zijn"],"correct":2,"en":"Those are the new rules for basic health insurance.","note":"Dat points at a plural noun (de regels), so the verb is plural: dat zijn."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Een groot deel van de zorgverleners ___ (zijn) overbelast.","answers":["is","zijn"],"en":"A large proportion of health-care workers are overworked.","note":"Een deel van + a plural noun: the verb may follow deel (is) or zorgverleners (zijn). Both are correct."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tien procent van de bevolking ___ (vinden) de zorg slecht toegankelijk.","answers":["vindt"],"en":"Ten per cent of the population find care hard to access.","note":"The noun after procent van is singular (de bevolking), so the verb is singular: vindt, stem vind + t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De huisarts of de praktijkondersteuner ___ (kunnen) u doorverwijzen.","answers":["kan","kunnen"],"en":"The GP or the practice nurse can refer you.","note":"Two singular subjects joined by of usually take a singular verb, because only one of them does it: kan. The plural kunnen also occurs and is not wrong."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zowel de patiënt als de arts ___ (willen) een snelle behandeling.","answers":["willen","wil"],"en":"Both the patient and the doctor want a quick treatment.","note":"With zowel … als and two singular nouns, the plural willen is the more common, the singular wil follows strict grammar. Both are correct."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het","words":["zijn","vooral","ouderen","die","op een verpleeghuis","wachten"],"answers":["Het zijn vooral ouderen die op een verpleeghuis wachten.","Het zijn vooral ouderen die wachten op een verpleeghuis."],"en":"It is mainly elderly people who are waiting for a place in a nursing home.","note":"Het zijn + plural noun: the verb agrees with ouderen, not with het. In the relative clause wachten is plural because die refers to ouderen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zij is een van de specialisten","words":["die","chronische patiënten","in dit ziekenhuis","behandelen"],"answers":["Zij is een van de specialisten die chronische patiënten in dit ziekenhuis behandelen.","Zij is een van de specialisten die in dit ziekenhuis chronische patiënten behandelen."],"en":"She is one of the specialists who treat chronic patients in this hospital.","note":"Die refers to de specialisten, so the relative clause has a plural verb, at the end: behandelen. This plural is the usual choice."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Iedereen instead of Alle patiënten.","from":"Alle patiënten hebben recht op een second opinion.","answers":["Iedereen heeft recht op een second opinion."],"en":"Everyone has the right to a second opinion.","note":"Alle + plural noun takes a plural verb, but iedereen is singular: hebben becomes heeft."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal: use u.","from":"Heb je je eigen risico al betaald?","answers":["Hebt u uw eigen risico al betaald?","Heeft u uw eigen risico al betaald?"],"en":"Have you (formal) already paid your excess?","note":"With u both hebt and heeft are correct. Je as a possessive becomes uw."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace veel mensen with een groot deel van de bevolking.","from":"Door de vergrijzing maken veel mensen zich zorgen over de zorgkosten.","answers":["Door de vergrijzing maakt een groot deel van de bevolking zich zorgen over de zorgkosten."],"en":"Because of the ageing population, a large part of the population is worried about health-care costs.","note":"After een deel van the noun is singular here (de bevolking), so the verb must be singular: maakt. The verb stays in second place, before the subject."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De regering willen het zorgstelsel hervormen.","answers":["De regering wil het zorgstelsel hervormen."],"en":"The government wants to reform the health-care system.","note":"De regering is one group, so the verb is singular: wil. English sometimes says 'the government want', Dutch does not."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Er is te weinig verpleegkundigen in de regio.","answers":["Er zijn te weinig verpleegkundigen in de regio."],"en":"There are too few nurses in the region.","note":"After er the verb agrees with the noun that follows: verpleegkundigen is plural, so er zijn."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Kunt je mij doorverwijzen naar een specialist?","answers":["Kun je mij doorverwijzen naar een specialist?","Kan je mij doorverwijzen naar een specialist?","Kun je me doorverwijzen naar een specialist?","Kan je me doorverwijzen naar een specialist?"],"en":"Can you refer me to a specialist?","note":"Je directly after the verb takes the form without t: kun je (or kan je). Kunt goes with u, or with jij before the verb."}]},
  {"id":"b2-17","level":"B2","icon":"🎭","title":"Art and literature","grammar":"Referring back: hij or het, deze, die, zulke, de eerste","summary":"A de-word is hij / hem / zijn, a het-word het / zijn, a plural ze / hun. At the front: die, dat. hen after a preposition and as object, hun 'to them', never hun as subject. zo'n + singular, zulke + plural.","explain":"A good text does not repeat its nouns; it refers back to them. In Dutch the pronoun follows the **gender of the noun** (de or het), not the question whether it is a person or a thing. English says *it* for every thing; Dutch says `hij` for a film and `het` for a book. This unit puts the whole system together.\n\n### Things: hij, het, ze\n\n| noun | subject | object | possessive |\n| de film | hij | hem | zijn |\n| het boek | het | het | zijn |\n| de boeken | ze | ze | hun |\n\n> De roman is ontroerend, maar hij is erg lang. — The novel is moving, but it is very long.\n> Het museum toont een groot deel van zijn collectie. — The museum is showing a large part of its collection.\n\nThe possessive of a het-word is `zijn`, never *hun and not *haar: `het museum en zijn collectie`. `hun` is only for plurals. A person keeps natural gender: `de auteur` is hij or zij, and a het-word for a person usually gets the person's pronoun too: `Het meisje zegt dat ze moe is.`\n\n### Feminine reference: zij and haar\n\nIn formal writing, de-words for organisations (`de regering`, `de gemeente`) and abstract de-words in `-ing`, `-heid`, `-schap`, `-ie` and `-teit` are traditionally feminine: `de regering en haar plannen`. Recognise it when you read it. In the Netherlands `hij` / `zijn` is also accepted for these words and is what people say, so both are correct.\n\n### die and dat at the front\n\nWhen the pronoun takes the first place, usually to pick up what was just mentioned, Dutch uses `die` (de-words and plurals) and `dat` (het-words) instead of `hem` and `het`. An object `het` can never stand first.\n\n> Die film? Die heb ik vorige week al gezien. — That film? I already saw it last week.\n> Dat boek? Dat heb ik nog niet gelezen. — That book? I have not read it yet.\n\n### deze, de laatste, eerstgenoemde\n\nWhen `hij` could point at two people, written Dutch uses `deze`: the **last** person or thing mentioned. `de eerste … de laatste` and, more formally, `eerstgenoemde … laatstgenoemde` mean *the former … the latter*.\n\n> De auteur belde de uitgever. Deze vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek. — The author phoned the publisher. The latter did not find the main character likeable.\n\n### People in the plural: zij, ze, hen, hun\n\n- subject: `zij` or unstressed `ze`, **never** *hun hebben.\n- direct object: `hen` in writing, `ze` in speech: `Ik heb hen gezien.`\n- indirect object (to them): `hun` in writing, `ze` in speech: `Ik heb hun een kaartje gegeven.`\n- after a preposition: `hen`: `met hen`, `voor hen`, never *met hun. For things you use the er-word instead: `ermee`, `erover` (unit B1 · 05).\n\n> Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jaren samen met hen geëxposeerd. — I know those two artists well: I exhibited with them for years.\n\n### zo'n, zulke, zulk, dergelijk\n\n- `zo'n` + a singular noun you can count: `zo'n film`, `zo'n boek`.\n- `zulke` + a plural: `zulke films`; also before a de-word without plural: `zulke muziek`.\n- `zulk` + a het-word without plural: `zulk weer`, `zulk talent`.\n- `dergelijk` is the formal word and behaves like an adjective: `een dergelijke film`, `een dergelijk boek` (het-word after een: no -e), `dergelijke films`.\n- `zo iemand` (such a person) is two words, `zoiets` (something like that) one.\n\nBefore a number `zo'n` means *about*: `zo'n twintig doeken`. *zo'n mooie boeken is heard in speech, but in writing it is `zulke mooie boeken`.\n\n### dezelfde and hetzelfde\n\n`dezelfde` goes with de-words and plurals, `hetzelfde` with het-words, each written as one word. On its own, `hetzelfde` means *the same thing*.\n\n> Ze vertellen hetzelfde verhaal, maar niet op dezelfde manier. — They tell the same story, but not in the same way.\n\n### het pointing forward; het or er\n\n`het` can announce a clause that follows. It cannot be left out: `Ik vind het jammer dat …`, never *Ik vind jammer dat ….\n\n> Het is opvallend dat het museum zo weinig bezoekers heeft. — It is striking that the museum has so few visitors.\n\n`het` is also the subject of weather and time (`Het regent`) and of sentences that name or identify, even with a plural: `Het zijn goede acteurs`. *There is / there are* with an indefinite subject is `er`: `Er is een nieuwe tentoonstelling`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian also chooses він, вона, воно by grammatical gender, so the idea is familiar. But the genders do not match: `de roman` is hij like роман, but `het boek` is het while книга is вона. And `Шкода, що …` needs no 'it', while Dutch needs `het`.","words":[{"nl":"roman","art":"de","pl":"romans","en":"novel","pos":"n"},{"nl":"auteur","art":"de","pl":"auteurs","en":"author","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitgever","art":"de","pl":"uitgevers","en":"publisher","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoofdpersoon","art":"de","pl":"hoofdpersonen","en":"main character","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toneelstuk","art":"het","pl":"toneelstukken","en":"play (in the theatre)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voorstelling","art":"de","pl":"voorstellingen","en":"performance / show","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tentoonstelling","art":"de","pl":"tentoonstellingen","en":"exhibition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kunstenaar","art":"de","pl":"kunstenaars","en":"artist","pos":"n"},{"nl":"collectie","art":"de","pl":"collecties","en":"collection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"meesterwerk","art":"het","pl":"meesterwerken","en":"masterpiece","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kunstwerk","art":"het","pl":"kunstwerken","en":"work of art","pos":"n"},{"nl":"doek","art":"het","pl":"doeken","en":"canvas / painting","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verschijnen","past":"verscheen","perf":"is verschenen","en":"to appear / to be published","pos":"v"},{"nl":"exposeren","past":"exposeerde","perf":"heeft geëxposeerd","en":"to exhibit","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verfilmen","past":"verfilmde","perf":"heeft verfilmd","en":"to make into a film","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontroerend","en":"moving / touching","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De roman is ontroerend, maar hij is erg lang.","en":"The novel is moving, but it is very long.","hint":["roman","ontroerend","erg","lang"],"note":"Roman is a de-word, so English 'it' becomes hij. Het would be wrong here: het only refers back to het-words.","alt":["De roman is ontroerend, maar hij is heel lang."]},{"nl":"Het toneelstuk is goed geschreven, maar het duurt te lang.","en":"The play is well written, but it goes on too long.","hint":["toneelstuk","goed","schrijven","duren","lang"],"note":"Toneelstuk is a het-word, so it is referred to with het. A de-word would have needed hij."},{"nl":"Die film? Die heb ik vorige week al gezien.","en":"That film? I already saw it last week.","hint":["film","zien","vorige week","al"],"note":"The pronoun takes the first place to pick up the topic, so a de-word gets die instead of hem. The verb heb stays second and ik follows it.","alt":["Die film? Die heb ik al vorige week gezien.","Die film? Ik heb die vorige week al gezien.","Die film? Ik heb hem vorige week al gezien.","Die film? Ik heb hem al vorige week gezien."]},{"nl":"Het museum toont dit jaar een groot deel van zijn collectie.","en":"This year the museum is showing a large part of its collection.","hint":["museum","tonen","jaar","deel","collectie"],"note":"Museum is a het-word, and the possessive of a het-word is zijn. Hun is only for plurals, and haar does not go with a het-word.","alt":["Dit jaar toont het museum een groot deel van zijn collectie."]},{"nl":"De auteur belde de uitgever. Deze vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek.","en":"The author phoned the publisher. The latter did not find the main character likeable.","hint":["auteur","bellen","uitgever","hoofdpersoon","sympathiek"],"note":"Hij could mean the author or the publisher; deze points at the last one mentioned, the publisher. This is written style.","alt":["De auteur belde de uitgever. De laatste vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek.","De auteur belde de uitgever. Laatstgenoemde vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek."]},{"nl":"Ik wilde de acteurs na de voorstelling spreken, maar ik heb hen nergens gezien.","en":"I wanted to talk to the actors after the performance, but I did not see them anywhere.","hint":["willen","acteur","voorstelling","spreken","nergens","zien"],"note":"Them is the direct object of gezien, which is hen in written Dutch and ze in speech. Hun would be the indirect object (to them).","alt":["Ik wilde de acteurs na de voorstelling spreken, maar ik heb ze nergens gezien.","Ik wilde na de voorstelling de acteurs spreken, maar ik heb hen nergens gezien.","Ik wilde na de voorstelling de acteurs spreken, maar ik heb ze nergens gezien.","Ik wilde de acteurs na de voorstelling spreken, maar ik zag hen nergens.","Ik wilde de acteurs na de voorstelling spreken, maar ik zag ze nergens."]},{"nl":"Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jaren samen met hen geëxposeerd.","en":"I know those two artists well: I exhibited with them for years.","hint":["kennen","kunstenaar","jaar","exposeren"],"note":"After a preposition, people in the plural are hen: met hen. Never met hun. Geëxposeerd has a trema because ge and e belong to different syllables.","alt":["Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jarenlang samen met hen geëxposeerd.","Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jaren met hen geëxposeerd."]},{"nl":"Ik had nog nooit zulke grote doeken gezien.","en":"I had never seen such big canvases before.","hint":["nog nooit","groot","doek","zien"],"note":"Doeken is plural, so such is zulke. Zo'n only goes with a singular noun: zo'n groot doek.","alt":["Zulke grote doeken had ik nog nooit gezien."]},{"nl":"Het toneelstuk en de film vertellen hetzelfde verhaal, maar niet op dezelfde manier.","en":"The play and the film tell the same story, but not in the same way.","hint":["toneelstuk","film","vertellen","verhaal","manier"],"note":"Verhaal is a het-word: hetzelfde verhaal. Manier is a de-word: dezelfde manier. Both are written as one word."},{"nl":"Ik vind het jammer dat de roman nooit is verfilmd.","en":"I think it is a pity that the novel has never been made into a film.","hint":["vinden","jammer","roman","nooit","verfilmen"],"note":"Het announces the dat-clause that follows, and it cannot be left out: never 'Ik vind jammer dat'. At the end both orders are correct: is verfilmd and verfilmd is.","alt":["Ik vind het jammer dat de roman nooit verfilmd is."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De nieuwe roman is vorige week verschenen. ___ is echt heel spannend.","options":["Het","Hij","Ze"],"correct":1,"en":"The new novel came out last week. It is really very exciting.","note":"De roman is a de-word, so it is hij, although English says 'it'. Het is for het-words, ze for plurals."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het museum heeft ___ hele collectie online gezet.","options":["hun","haar","zijn","het"],"correct":2,"en":"The museum has put its whole collection online.","note":"Museum is a het-word, so its possessive is zijn. Hun is only for plurals, and 'het museum en hun collectie' is a common mistake even among native speakers."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ is deze maand een bijzondere tentoonstelling in het museum.","options":["Het","Die","Er"],"correct":2,"en":"There is a special exhibition in the museum this month.","note":"'There is' with an indefinite subject (een tentoonstelling) is er is. Het would say 'it is a special exhibition'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb nog nooit ___ mooie kunstwerken bij elkaar gezien.","options":["zo'n","zulke","zulk"],"correct":1,"en":"I have never seen such beautiful works of art together.","note":"Kunstwerken is plural, so zulke. Zo'n is for one countable thing (zo'n mooi kunstwerk), zulk for a het-word without plural (zulk weer)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De kunstenaars zaten in de zaal, en het hele publiek klapte voor ___.","options":["hen","zij","hun"],"correct":0,"en":"The artists were sitting in the hall, and the whole audience applauded them.","note":"After a preposition (voor), people in the plural are hen. Hun is only the indirect object without a preposition, and zij is a subject form."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wie staan er op de poster? ___ zijn de regisseur en de hoofdrolspeler.","options":["Er","Hun","Het"],"correct":2,"en":"Who is on the poster? They are the director and the lead actor.","note":"A sentence that identifies people starts with het, and the verb agrees with the plural: het zijn. Hun is never a subject, and er is for 'there is'. (Dat zijn … is also correct.)"},{"type":"gap","nl":"Die actrice speelt al tien jaar in ___ (the same) toneelstuk.","answers":["hetzelfde"],"en":"That actress has been acting in the same play for ten years.","note":"Toneelstuk is a het-word, so the same is hetzelfde, one word. With a de-word it would be dezelfde: dezelfde voorstelling."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Een ___ (dergelijk) meesterwerk zie je maar zelden.","answers":["dergelijk"],"en":"You only rarely see a masterpiece like that.","note":"Dergelijk behaves like an adjective. Meesterwerk is a het-word, and after een an adjective before a het-word has no -e: een dergelijk meesterwerk, but een dergelijke film."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na afloop heb ik de acteurs gesproken en ___ (them) een compliment gegeven.","answers":["hun","ze"],"en":"Afterwards I spoke to the actors and gave them a compliment.","note":"The actors receive the compliment: indirect object, so hun in written Dutch, ze in speech. Hen is for the direct object and after a preposition."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De gemeente verlaagt volgend jaar ___ (its) subsidie aan het theater.","answers":["haar","zijn"],"en":"Next year the municipality is cutting its grant to the theatre.","note":"Gemeente is a de-word for an organisation. Formal writing traditionally treats it as feminine (haar); in the Netherlands zijn is also accepted. Hun is wrong: the municipality is one thing."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dat boek?","words":["Dat","heb","ik","nog niet","gelezen"],"answers":["Dat boek? Dat heb ik nog niet gelezen.","Dat boek? Ik heb dat nog niet gelezen."],"en":"That book? I have not read it yet.","note":"Dat picks up the het-word boek and usually takes the first place; then heb comes second and ik follows. An object het cannot stand first: never 'Het heb ik nog niet gelezen'."},{"type":"order","lead":"De regisseur sprak met de schrijver.","words":["Deze","vond","het einde","te somber"],"answers":["De regisseur sprak met de schrijver. Deze vond het einde te somber."],"en":"The director spoke to the writer. The latter thought the ending was too gloomy.","note":"Deze refers to the last person mentioned, de schrijver. Hij would leave it open who found the ending too gloomy."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace de roman with a pronoun.","from":"Ik heb de roman in één weekend gelezen.","answers":["Ik heb hem in één weekend gelezen.","Die heb ik in één weekend gelezen.","Ik heb die in één weekend gelezen."],"en":"I read it in one weekend.","note":"Roman is a de-word, so the object pronoun is hem, not het. At the front of the sentence Dutch prefers die."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Het is jammer.","from":"Dat de première is uitgesteld, is jammer.","answers":["Het is jammer dat de première is uitgesteld.","Het is jammer dat de première uitgesteld is."],"en":"It is a pity that the premiere has been postponed.","note":"Het stands in the subject place and points forward to the dat-clause, which now comes at the end. Both orders of is and uitgesteld are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het kunstwerk werd in 1990 gestolen, maar de politie heeft hem nooit teruggevonden.","answers":["Het kunstwerk werd in 1990 gestolen, maar de politie heeft het nooit teruggevonden."],"en":"The work of art was stolen in 1990, but the police never found it again.","note":"Kunstwerk is a het-word, so the object pronoun is het, not hem. Hem is for de-words: de film … hem."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn ouders gaan niet mee, want hun hebben de voorstelling al gezien.","answers":["Mijn ouders gaan niet mee, want ze hebben de voorstelling al gezien.","Mijn ouders gaan niet mee, want zij hebben de voorstelling al gezien."],"en":"My parents are not coming along, because they have already seen the show.","note":"Hun is never a subject. The subject is zij or, unstressed, ze. 'Hun hebben' is heard, but it is a well-known mistake."}]},
  {"id":"b2-18","level":"B2","icon":"🧒","title":"Bringing up children","grammar":"Als, of, alsof, zoals, dan","summary":"als: if, when, as (a role), even … als. dan: after a comparative and anders. of: or, whether, 'or so'. alsof: as if. zoals + clause (zoals ik zei), net als + noun (net als ik), als + role (als kind).","explain":"Five short words cause more mistakes at B2 than many long ones: `als`, `dan`, `of`, `alsof` and `zoals`. English *if*, *as*, *like* and *than* do not map onto them one to one, so it helps to sort them by meaning.\n\n### als\n\n- **if** (a condition): `Als het regent, blijven we binnen.`\n- **when**, for something that happens again and again or in the future: `Als ik thuiskom, ga ik koken.` For one moment in the past Dutch says `toen` (unit B1 · 01).\n- **as**, in a role or at an age: `als leerkracht` (as a teacher), `als kind` (as a child). No article.\n- in comparisons of **equality**: `even groot als`, `net zo streng als`, `zo snel als mogelijk` (or simply `zo snel mogelijk`; both are correct).\n\nAs a conjunction `als` starts a sub-clause, so its verb goes to the end; when the clause comes first, the main clause starts with its verb.\n\n> Als kinderen genoeg slapen, kunnen ze zich beter concentreren. — If children get enough sleep, they can concentrate better.\n> Als leerkracht zie ik elke dag hoeveel prestatiedruk de leerlingen ervaren. — As a teacher I see every day how much pressure to perform the pupils feel.\n\n### dan\n\n`dan` follows a **comparative** (`groter dan`, `meer dan`, `liever dan`) and the words `anders` and `ander` (`anders dan vroeger`, `een andere school dan`). It also means *then*: `Eerst eten, dan huiswerk.`\n\n> De kinderopvang is veel duurder dan ik had verwacht. — Childcare is much more expensive than I had expected.\n\nIn speech you will hear *groter als. In writing that counts as a mistake: after a comparative the norm is `dan`. The rule of thumb: **equal** → `als`, **different** → `dan`.\n\n| | Dutch |\n| as big as | even groot als |\n| bigger than | groter dan |\n| different from | anders dan |\n\n### of\n\n- **or**: `thee of koffie`.\n- **whether**, the *if* of an indirect question: `Ik weet niet of hij komt.` Never *Ik weet niet als hij komt.\n- **or so**, with an amount: `een stuk of tien` (ten or so), `een dag of drie` (three days or so).\n- After a negative main clause, in fixed patterns you only need to recognise: `Het duurde niet lang of ze sliep.` (It was not long before she was asleep.)\n\n> Ik weet niet of er in de pauze genoeg toezicht is. — I do not know whether there is enough supervision during the break.\n\n### alsof\n\n`alsof` means *as if*. It starts a sub-clause, so the verb goes to the end. When the comparison is not true, the past tense is common, as in English *as if he were*. In everyday Dutch `of` is often used in the same way: `Hij doet of hij slaapt.`\n\n> Hij doet alsof hij zijn huiswerk al gemaakt heeft. — He acts as if he has already done his homework.\n> Ze behandelt haar zoon alsof hij nog een peuter was. — She treats her son as if he were still a toddler.\n\n### zoals, als or net als: English 'like'\n\n| English | Dutch |\n| like I said | zoals ik al zei |\n| like me, like his father | net als ik, net als zijn vader |\n| as a child | als kind |\n\n- `zoals` + a clause (*the way that*): `zoals ik al zei`, `zoals altijd`, `zoals afgesproken`.\n- `net als` + a noun or pronoun, when two people or things are the same: `Hij is net als zijn vader leraar.`\n- `als` + a noun without article, for a role: `als ouder`, `als kind`.\n- For *such as* in a list both are correct: `vakken zoals wiskunde` and `vakken als wiskunde`.\n\n> Mijn zoon is net als zijn vader erg zelfstandig. — Like his father, my son is very independent.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *if* = condition → `als`; *if* = whether → `of`.\n- *than* is `dan`; *as … as* is `als`.\n- *Zoals kind → `Als kind`. *Als ik al zei → `Zoals ik al zei`.\n\nTwo more: `hoe ouder, hoe wijzer` or `hoe ouder, des te wijzer` (the older, the wiser; unit B1 · 19), and the formal `evenals` and `alsmede` (as well as) in official texts.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `як` covers `als`, `zoals` and `net als`; `якщо` is `als` (if), `чи` is `of`, `ніж` is `dan`, and `ніби` or `наче` is `alsof`.","words":[{"nl":"opvoeding","art":"de","pl":"","en":"upbringing","pos":"n"},{"nl":"opvoeden","past":"voedde op","perf":"heeft opgevoed","en":"to bring up (a child)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"kinderopvang","art":"de","pl":"","en":"childcare","pos":"n"},{"nl":"peuter","art":"de","pl":"peuters","en":"toddler","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leerkracht","art":"de","pl":"leerkrachten","en":"teacher (in a school)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leerling","art":"de","pl":"leerlingen","en":"pupil","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leerplicht","art":"de","pl":"","en":"compulsory education","pos":"n"},{"nl":"onderwijs","art":"het","pl":"","en":"education (the system)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"achterstand","art":"de","pl":"achterstanden","en":"disadvantage / falling behind","pos":"n"},{"nl":"prestatiedruk","art":"de","pl":"","en":"pressure to perform","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toezicht","art":"het","pl":"","en":"supervision","pos":"n"},{"nl":"grens","art":"de","pl":"grenzen","en":"limit / border","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verwennen","past":"verwende","perf":"heeft verwend","en":"to spoil (a child)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"belonen","past":"beloonde","perf":"heeft beloond","en":"to reward","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zelfstandig","en":"independent","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"consequent","en":"consistent","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Als kinderen genoeg slapen, kunnen ze zich beter concentreren.","en":"If children get enough sleep, they can concentrate better.","hint":["kind","genoeg","slapen","zich concentreren"],"note":"Als is 'if' here: a condition. The als-clause has its verb at the end and counts as the first element, so the main clause continues with the verb kunnen.","alt":["Kinderen kunnen zich beter concentreren als ze genoeg slapen.","Kinderen kunnen zich beter concentreren als ze voldoende slapen.","Als kinderen voldoende slapen, kunnen ze zich beter concentreren."]},{"nl":"Als leerkracht zie ik elke dag hoeveel prestatiedruk de leerlingen ervaren.","en":"As a teacher I see every day how much pressure to perform the pupils feel.","hint":["leerkracht","zien","prestatiedruk","leerling","ervaren"],"note":"Als + a noun without article gives a role: as a teacher. Not zoals, and no een after als.","alt":["Ik zie als leerkracht elke dag hoeveel prestatiedruk de leerlingen ervaren.","Als leerkracht zie ik elke dag hoeveel prestatiedruk de leerlingen voelen."]},{"nl":"Mijn zoon is net als zijn vader erg zelfstandig.","en":"Like his father, my son is very independent.","hint":["zoon","net als","vader","zelfstandig"],"note":"English 'like' before a noun, when two people are the same, is net als. Zoals would need a clause after it.","alt":["Net als zijn vader is mijn zoon erg zelfstandig.","Mijn zoon is erg zelfstandig, net als zijn vader.","Mijn zoon is net als zijn vader heel zelfstandig.","Net als zijn vader is mijn zoon heel zelfstandig."]},{"nl":"Zoals ik al zei, moet je in de opvoeding consequent zijn.","en":"As I said, you have to be consistent in bringing up children.","hint":["zoals","zeggen","opvoeding","consequent"],"note":"Zoals + a clause: 'as' or 'like' in the sense of 'the way that'. Als ik al zei would be wrong.","alt":["Zoals ik al zei, je moet in de opvoeding consequent zijn.","Je moet, zoals ik al zei, in de opvoeding consequent zijn.","Zoals ik al zei, moet je consequent zijn in de opvoeding.","Zoals ik al zei, je moet consequent zijn in de opvoeding.","Zoals ik zei, moet je in de opvoeding consequent zijn.","Zoals ik zei, je moet in de opvoeding consequent zijn."]},{"nl":"In Nederland gaan kinderen eerder naar school dan in Oekraïne.","en":"In the Netherlands children start school earlier than in Ukraine.","hint":["Nederland","kind","vroeg","school","Oekraïne"],"note":"Eerder is a comparative, so 'than' is dan. Als after a comparative is heard in speech but counts as wrong in writing.","alt":["Kinderen gaan in Nederland eerder naar school dan in Oekraïne."]},{"nl":"De kinderopvang is hier veel duurder dan ik had verwacht.","en":"Childcare here is much more expensive than I had expected.","hint":["kinderopvang","hier","duur","verwachten"],"note":"Duurder is a comparative: dan. A clause can follow dan; it has sub-clause order, and both orders of the two verbs are correct.","alt":["De kinderopvang is hier veel duurder dan ik verwacht had.","Hier is de kinderopvang veel duurder dan ik had verwacht.","Hier is de kinderopvang veel duurder dan ik verwacht had."]},{"nl":"Ik weet niet of er in de pauze genoeg toezicht is.","en":"I do not know whether there is enough supervision during the break.","hint":["weten","pauze","genoeg","toezicht"],"note":"Whether, the 'if' of an indirect question, is of. Als would mean 'if' as a condition. The of-clause has its verb at the end.","alt":["Ik weet niet of er genoeg toezicht is in de pauze.","Ik weet niet of er tijdens de pauze genoeg toezicht is.","Ik weet niet of er genoeg toezicht is tijdens de pauze."]},{"nl":"Hij doet alsof hij zijn huiswerk al gemaakt heeft.","en":"He acts as if he has already done his homework.","hint":["doen","alsof","huiswerk","maken"],"note":"Alsof starts a sub-clause, so the verbs go to the end. In everyday Dutch of is also used here: Hij doet of hij …","alt":["Hij doet alsof hij zijn huiswerk al heeft gemaakt.","Hij doet of hij zijn huiswerk al gemaakt heeft.","Hij doet of hij zijn huiswerk al heeft gemaakt."]},{"nl":"Ze behandelt haar zoon alsof hij nog een peuter was.","en":"She treats her son as if he were still a toddler.","hint":["behandelen","zoon","alsof","nog","peuter"],"note":"The comparison is not true, so the past tense was is usual after alsof, like English 'were'. The present tense is also heard: alsof hij nog een peuter is.","alt":["Zij behandelt haar zoon alsof hij nog een peuter was.","Ze behandelt haar zoon alsof hij nog een peuter is."]},{"nl":"Er zitten een stuk of dertig leerlingen in de klas.","en":"There are thirty or so pupils in the class.","hint":["zitten","stuk","dertig","leerling","klas"],"note":"Een stuk of + a number means 'about' or 'or so'. The verb is plural, because the subject is the thirty pupils.","alt":["In de klas zitten een stuk of dertig leerlingen.","Er zitten in de klas een stuk of dertig leerlingen.","Er zijn een stuk of dertig leerlingen in de klas."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het onderwijs is tegenwoordig minder streng ___ vroeger.","options":["als","dan","of"],"correct":1,"en":"Education is less strict nowadays than it used to be.","note":"Minder streng is a comparative, so 'than' is dan. Als after a comparative is heard in speech, but in writing it is wrong."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn moeder werkte twintig jaar ___ leerkracht op een basisschool.","options":["zoals","net als","als"],"correct":2,"en":"My mother worked as a teacher at a primary school for twenty years.","note":"A role is als + noun without article: als leerkracht. Zoals needs a clause, and net als would mean 'just like a teacher', which she is not compared to."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze voedt haar kinderen op ___ haar ouders dat vroeger deden.","options":["zoals","als","alsof"],"correct":0,"en":"She brings up her children the way her parents used to do.","note":"A clause follows (haar ouders dat vroeger deden), and the meaning is 'the way that': zoals. Alsof would say it only seems so."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik vraag me af ___ we onze dochter te veel verwennen.","options":["als","dan","of"],"correct":2,"en":"I wonder whether we are spoiling our daughter too much.","note":"After zich afvragen comes an indirect question: of (whether). Als is 'if' only as a condition."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De achterstand in taal is bij deze leerlingen even groot ___ vorig jaar.","options":["dan","als","zoals"],"correct":1,"en":"The language gap among these pupils is as big as last year.","note":"Even groot is a comparison of equality: even … als. Dan only follows a comparative such as groter."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze doet ___ (as if) ze niets gemerkt heeft.","answers":["alsof","of"],"en":"She acts as if she has not noticed anything.","note":"As if is alsof, and in everyday Dutch of is used the same way after doen. The verbs go to the end of the clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Weet jij ___ (whether) er in Oekraïne ook een leerplicht is?","answers":["of"],"en":"Do you know whether there is compulsory education in Ukraine too?","note":"Whether is of, also when English says 'if': Do you know if …? Als cannot start an indirect question."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Belonen werkt vaak beter ___ (than) straffen.","answers":["dan"],"en":"Rewarding often works better than punishing.","note":"Beter is a comparative (of goed), so it is followed by dan."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij hebben onze kinderen heel anders opgevoed ___ (than) onze ouders ons.","answers":["dan"],"en":"We brought up our children very differently from the way our parents brought us up.","note":"Anders is followed by dan, just like a comparative: anders dan. Not anders als."},{"type":"order","lead":"Als je consequent bent,","words":["leren","kinderen","sneller","waar","de grenzen","liggen"],"answers":["Als je consequent bent, leren kinderen sneller waar de grenzen liggen."],"en":"If you are consistent, children learn more quickly where the limits are.","note":"The als-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with its verb leren and then the subject kinderen. In the waar-clause the verb liggen goes last."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hij","words":["doet","alsof","hij","het","niet","gehoord","heeft"],"answers":["Hij doet alsof hij het niet gehoord heeft.","Hij doet alsof hij het niet heeft gehoord."],"en":"He acts as if he has not heard it.","note":"Alsof makes a sub-clause: subject first, verbs at the end. Both orders of the two verbs at the end are correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with alsof, keeping the present tense.","from":"Ze praat tegen haar peuter. Hij is al volwassen.","answers":["Ze praat tegen haar peuter alsof hij al volwassen is."],"en":"She talks to her toddler as if he is already grown up.","note":"Alsof sends the verb is to the end of the clause. (With the unreal past, alsof hij al volwassen was, the sentence is also correct.)"},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it an indirect question after Ik weet niet.","from":"Gaat de kinderopvang in de zomer dicht?","answers":["Ik weet niet of de kinderopvang in de zomer dichtgaat.","Ik weet niet of de kinderopvang dichtgaat in de zomer."],"en":"I do not know whether the childcare centre closes in the summer.","note":"A yes/no question becomes an of-clause. The verb goes to the end, and the separable verb dichtgaan is written as one word there."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik weet niet als mijn dochter morgen naar school kan.","answers":["Ik weet niet of mijn dochter morgen naar school kan."],"en":"I do not know whether my daughter can go to school tomorrow.","note":"This 'if' means 'whether', so it is of. Als is only 'if' as a condition: Als ze beter is, gaat ze naar school."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Onze zoon is veel drukker als zijn zus.","answers":["Onze zoon is veel drukker dan zijn zus."],"en":"Our son is much livelier than his sister.","note":"After a comparative (drukker) the norm is dan. Als is for equality: even druk als."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Zoals kind wilde ik altijd leerkracht worden.","answers":["Als kind wilde ik altijd leerkracht worden."],"en":"As a child I always wanted to become a teacher.","note":"As a child is als kind: als + noun without article for a role or an age. Zoals is followed by a clause, as in zoals ik al zei."}]},
  {"id":"b2-19","level":"B2","icon":"✍️","title":"Language and writing","grammar":"Spelling: one word or two, tussen-n, trema, apostrophe, hyphen","summary":"A compound is one word: taalcursus. Linking -en- if the first part's only plural is -en (woordenboek), else -e- (groentesoep). Hyphen for clashing vowels, abbreviations, numbers. auto's, Anna's; geïnteresseerd.","explain":"Much of Dutch spelling is not about single letters but about where a word begins and ends, and how its parts are joined. These rules are grammar as much as spelling, and they are where educated writers are judged.\n\n### A compound is one word\n\nDutch writes a compound (*de samenstelling*) as **one word**, however long it is: `taalcursus`, `klantenservice`, `langeafstandsloper`. Writing the parts apart, as English does, is a well-known mistake called *de Engelse ziekte*: *taal cursus, *klanten service.\n\n> In het Nederlands schrijf je een samenstelling als één woord. — In Dutch you write a compound as one word.\n\n### The linking sound: -en-, -e- or -s-\n\nWhen you hear a weak *e* between the parts, look at the plural of the first part:\n\n| first part | plural | compound |\n| woord | woorden only | woordenboek |\n| boek | boeken only | boekenkast |\n| groente | groenten and groentes | groentesoep |\n| rijst | none | rijstepap |\n\n- Plural **only in -en** → write `-en-`, even when the meaning is singular: `pannenkoek`.\n- Plural also in -s, or no plural → write `-e-`: `gedachtestreepje` (gedachten / gedachtes).\n- Something unique → `-e-`: `zonneschijn`, `Koninginnedag`.\n\n> Dit woordenboek geeft bij elk woord de klemtoon en het meervoud. — This dictionary gives the stress and the plural for every word.\n\nA linking `-s-` is written when you pronounce it: `stadscentrum`, `gesprekspartner`, and also before another s: `stationsstraat`.\n\n### The hyphen\n\n- When two vowels meet that could be read as one sound: `zee-eend`, `auto-ongeluk`, `media-aandacht`.\n- After an abbreviation, a single letter or a figure: `tv-programma`, `pdf-bestand`, `e-mailadres`, `65-plusser`.\n- Between two equal parts: `zwart-wit`, `een Nederlands-Oekraïens woordenboek`; and in names such as `Zuid-Holland`.\n\n> Het tv-programma over taal was bedoeld voor 65-plussers. — The TV programme about language was meant for the over-65s.\n\n### The trema\n\nInside a single word (not a compound), the trema (¨) shows that a **new syllable** begins with that vowel, so that the two letters are not read as one sound: `geïnteresseerd`, `coördinator`, `België`, `Oekraïne`, `reünie`, `ideeën`, `tweeën`. Compare `zeeën` (one word, trema) with `zee-eend` (compound, hyphen).\n\n> Een trema laat zien dat een nieuwe lettergreep begint. — A trema shows that a new syllable begins.\n\n### The apostrophe\n\n- Plural with -s after a, i, o, u, y: `auto's`, `alinea's`, `menu's`, `baby's`. After -e there is none: `tantes`; nor after two vowel letters or é: `cadeaus`, `cafés`.\n- Genitive with -s after the same vowels: `Anna's tekst`, `Otto's boek`; otherwise just -s: `Peters tekst`, `Annes tekst`; after an s-sound only an apostrophe: `Hans' tekst`.\n- Abbreviations: `tv's`, `cd's`, `sms'je`.\n- Letters left out: `'s morgens`, `'s avonds`, `zo'n`.\n\n> 's Avonds lees ik Anna's samenvatting nog een keer. — In the evening I read Anna's summary once more.\n\n### Capitals and accents\n\nNames, languages and nationalities have a capital: `Nederlands`, `Oekraïens`, `een Oekraïense`. Days and months do not: `maandag 3 maart`. An accent marks stress when the meaning would otherwise be unclear: `één` (one) against `een` (a), `vóór` (before, in front of) against `voor`, `dé oplossing` (the solution).\n\n### Words that sound alike\n\n- `jou` is an object (`Ik zie jou`), `jouw` a possessive (`jouw tekst`).\n- `na` means *after*, `naar` *to*: `na de les`, `naar de docent`.\n- `me` and `mij` are objects; the possessive is `mijn`. *me tekst is speech only.\n> Na de les stuur ik jouw tekst naar de docent. — After the class I will send your text to the teacher.\n\n- `hun` and `hen` (unit B2 · 17), `als` and `dan` (unit B2 · 18).\n- `hij wil`, never *hij wilt. With jij both `jij wilt` and `jij wil` are correct, but after the verb it is `wil jij`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian often says it with an adjective and a noun, `мовний курс`, where Dutch makes one compound: `taalcursus`. When Ukrainian has two words, check whether Dutch has one.","words":[{"nl":"spelling","art":"de","pl":"spellingen","en":"spelling","pos":"n"},{"nl":"samenstelling","art":"de","pl":"samenstellingen","en":"compound (word)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"woordenboek","art":"het","pl":"woordenboeken","en":"dictionary","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leesteken","art":"het","pl":"leestekens","en":"punctuation mark","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afkorting","art":"de","pl":"afkortingen","en":"abbreviation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"klemtoon","art":"de","pl":"klemtonen","en":"stress (on a syllable)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lettergreep","art":"de","pl":"lettergrepen","en":"syllable","pos":"n"},{"nl":"meervoud","art":"het","pl":"meervouden","en":"plural","pos":"n"},{"nl":"taalgebruik","art":"het","pl":"","en":"use of language","pos":"n"},{"nl":"alinea","art":"de","pl":"alinea's","en":"paragraph","pos":"n"},{"nl":"samenvatting","art":"de","pl":"samenvattingen","en":"summary","pos":"n"},{"nl":"taalfout","art":"de","pl":"taalfouten","en":"language mistake","pos":"n"},{"nl":"formuleren","past":"formuleerde","perf":"heeft geformuleerd","en":"to phrase / to formulate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"corrigeren","past":"corrigeerde","perf":"heeft gecorrigeerd","en":"to correct","pos":"v"},{"nl":"leesbaar","en":"readable","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik volg sinds januari een taalcursus bij de bibliotheek.","en":"I have been taking a language course at the library since January.","hint":["volgen","januari","taalcursus","bibliotheek"],"note":"Taalcursus is a compound, so it is one word: not taal cursus. Months have no capital in Dutch: januari.","alt":["Sinds januari volg ik een taalcursus bij de bibliotheek.","Ik volg sinds januari een taalcursus in de bibliotheek.","Sinds januari volg ik een taalcursus in de bibliotheek."]},{"nl":"Dit woordenboek geeft bij elk woord de klemtoon en het meervoud.","en":"This dictionary gives the stress and the plural for every word.","hint":["woordenboek","geven","woord","klemtoon","meervoud"],"note":"Woord has only the plural woorden, so the linking sound is written -en-: woordenboek. Klemtoon is a compound too: one word.","alt":["Dit woordenboek geeft de klemtoon en het meervoud bij elk woord.","Dit woordenboek geeft bij ieder woord de klemtoon en het meervoud."]},{"nl":"Een trema laat zien dat een nieuwe lettergreep begint.","en":"A trema shows that a new syllable begins.","hint":["trema","laten zien","nieuw","lettergreep","beginnen"],"note":"That is why it is geïnteresseerd and coördinator: the i and the o start a new syllable. Lettergreep is a compound of letter and greep, written as one word.","alt":["Een trema geeft aan dat een nieuwe lettergreep begint."]},{"nl":"De docent heeft de taalfouten in mijn alinea's met rood gemarkeerd.","en":"The teacher has marked the language mistakes in my paragraphs in red.","hint":["docent","taalfout","alinea","rood","markeren"],"note":"Alinea ends in a, so the plural -s gets an apostrophe: alinea's, as in auto's. Without it, the a would be read short.","alt":["De docent heeft de taalfouten in mijn alinea's in het rood gemarkeerd.","De docent heeft de taalfouten in mijn alinea's rood gemarkeerd."]},{"nl":"Mijn collega is erg geïnteresseerd in het Oekraïens.","en":"My colleague is very interested in Ukrainian.","hint":["collega","geïnteresseerd","Oekraïens"],"note":"Two tremas: ge-ïn and Oe-kra-ïens, where a new syllable starts. A language has a capital: Oekraïens.","alt":["Mijn collega is heel geïnteresseerd in het Oekraïens.","Mijn collega is erg geïnteresseerd in Oekraïens."]},{"nl":"Het tv-programma over taal was bedoeld voor 65-plussers.","en":"The TV programme about language was meant for the over-65s.","hint":["tv-programma","taal","bedoelen","65-plusser"],"note":"After an abbreviation (tv) and after a figure (65) a compound gets a hyphen. The abbreviation tv is written in small letters.","alt":["Het tv-programma over taal was voor 65-plussers bedoeld."]},{"nl":"'s Avonds lees ik Anna's samenvatting nog een keer.","en":"In the evening I read Anna's summary once more.","hint":["'s avonds","lezen","samenvatting","keer"],"note":"'s avonds keeps an old 's (des). Anna's gets an apostrophe because the name ends in a; Peters would have none.","alt":["Ik lees 's avonds Anna's samenvatting nog een keer.","Ik lees Anna's samenvatting 's avonds nog een keer.","'s Avonds lees ik Anna's samenvatting nog eens."]},{"nl":"In het Nederlands schrijf je een samenstelling als één woord.","en":"In Dutch you write a compound as one word.","hint":["Nederlands","schrijven","samenstelling","één","woord"],"note":"één has accents because it means 'one', not 'a'. Nederlands is a language, so it has a capital.","alt":["Een samenstelling schrijf je in het Nederlands als één woord.","Je schrijft een samenstelling in het Nederlands als één woord.","In het Nederlands schrijf je een samenstelling aan elkaar."]},{"nl":"Ik heb het e-mailadres van de coördinator nergens kunnen vinden.","en":"I could not find the coordinator's e-mail address anywhere.","hint":["e-mailadres","coördinator","nergens","vinden"],"note":"E-mail keeps its hyphen, and e-mail + adres is written as one word: e-mailadres. Coördinator has a trema because co and or are separate syllables.","alt":["Ik kon het e-mailadres van de coördinator nergens vinden."]},{"nl":"Wil jij mijn tekst corrigeren voordat ik hem verstuur?","en":"Will you correct my text before I send it?","hint":["willen","tekst","corrigeren","voordat","versturen"],"note":"With jij after the verb it is wil jij, without t. (Jij wilt and jij wil are both correct, but hij always wil.)","alt":["Wil je mijn tekst corrigeren voordat ik hem verstuur?","Wil jij mijn tekst corrigeren voor ik hem verstuur?","Wil je mijn tekst corrigeren voor ik hem verstuur?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb gisteren de ___ gebeld.","options":["klanten service","klanten-service","klantenservice"],"correct":2,"en":"I called customer service yesterday.","note":"A compound is one word, without a space and without a hyphen. Klant has only the plural klanten, so -en-: klantenservice."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn zus is erg ___ in taalgebruik.","options":["geïnteresseerd","geinteresseerd","geïntresseerd"],"correct":0,"en":"My sister is very interested in the use of language.","note":"The i starts a new syllable (ge-ïn-te-res-seerd), so it gets a trema. The e after int is pronounced and written: -interesseerd."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We hebben de toets over spelling op ___.","options":["Maandag 3 Maart","maandag 3 maart","Maandag 3 maart"],"correct":1,"en":"We have the spelling test on Monday 3 March.","note":"Days and months are written with a small letter in Dutch, unlike in English. Languages and nationalities do get a capital."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de correctie is je tekst veel beter leesbaar.","options":["Naar","Na","Van"],"correct":1,"en":"After the correction your text is much more readable.","note":"After (in time) is na. Naar means 'to' or 'towards': naar de docent."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Hij ___ zijn samenvatting nog laten corrigeren.","options":["wilt","willen","wil"],"correct":2,"en":"He wants to have his summary corrected.","note":"Hij wil, never hij wilt. Only with jij and u is a t possible: jij wilt (or jij wil), u wilt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zoek dat woord even op in een ___ (woord + boek).","answers":["woordenboek"],"en":"Just look that word up in a dictionary.","note":"Woord has only the plural woorden, so the linking sound is written -en-, and the compound is one word."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Een ___ (gedachte + streepje) is een leesteken.","answers":["gedachtestreepje"],"en":"A dash is a punctuation mark.","note":"Gedachte has two plurals, gedachten and gedachtes. A first part with a plural in -s too is linked with -e- only, without n."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Bij een afkorting schrijf je het meervoud met een apostrof: twee ___ (tv).","answers":["tv's"],"en":"With an abbreviation you write the plural with an apostrophe: two TVs.","note":"An abbreviation gets 's in the plural, so that the letters stay recognisable: tv's, cd's."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De nieuwe woordenlijst kreeg veel ___ (media + aandacht).","answers":["media-aandacht"],"en":"The new word list got a lot of media attention.","note":"The a of media and the aa of aandacht would be read as one sound, so a hyphen keeps them apart: media-aandacht."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik heb ___ (Anna) tekst gisteren al gecorrigeerd.","answers":["Anna's"],"en":"I already corrected Anna's text yesterday.","note":"A name ending in a, i, o, u or y gets 's in the genitive: Anna's. Most other names just take s: Peters tekst."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik schrijf het liefst ___ (in the morning: 's …), als het nog stil is.","answers":["'s morgens","'s ochtends"],"en":"I prefer to write in the morning, when it is still quiet.","note":"'s morgens and 's ochtends are old genitives (des morgens): an apostrophe, a small s, and a space before the word."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace lijst van woorden by one compound noun.","from":"Ik zoek een lijst van woorden.","answers":["Ik zoek een woordenlijst."],"en":"I am looking for a word list.","note":"The compound is one word, and woord has only the plural woorden, so it is woordenlijst, with -en-."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make both nouns plural.","from":"De foto staat naast de alinea.","answers":["De foto's staan naast de alinea's."],"en":"The photos are next to the paragraphs.","note":"Foto and alinea end in o and a, so the plural -s comes after an apostrophe. The verb becomes plural too: staan."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Stuur me even de pdf bestanden van je samenvatting.","answers":["Stuur me even de pdf-bestanden van je samenvatting."],"en":"Just send me the PDF files of your summary.","note":"A compound with an abbreviation is joined with a hyphen: pdf-bestanden. Without the hyphen it would be two loose words."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb jou samenvatting al gelezen.","answers":["Ik heb jouw samenvatting al gelezen."],"en":"I have already read your summary.","note":"The possessive (your) is jouw. Jou is the object form: Ik heb jou gezien."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Wilt jij deze zin wat korter formuleren?","answers":["Wil jij deze zin wat korter formuleren?","Wil je deze zin wat korter formuleren?"],"en":"Will you phrase this sentence a bit more briefly?","note":"When jij comes after the verb, the t goes: wil jij. Jij wilt is correct, but wilt jij is not."}]},
  {"id":"b2-20","level":"B2","icon":"⭐","title":"Reviews and opinions","grammar":"Adverbs of degree and focus: nogal, best, pas, al, maar, slechts","summary":"Degree words have no ending: een erg dure auto. best and nogal are milder than they look. With numbers and times: al and wel (a lot, early), pas (late, little so far), maar and slechts (few). alleen: nothing else.","explain":"A review lives on small words: *rather* good, *far too* expensive, *only* two stars, *not until* nine o'clock. Dutch has a word for every step, and with numbers and times these words also show whether the writer thinks it is much or little, early or late.\n\n### A scale of degree\n\n| strength | words |\n| a little | een beetje, enigszins |\n| fairly | vrij, tamelijk, redelijk, best (wel) |\n| rather | nogal, behoorlijk, flink |\n| very | erg, heel, zeer |\n| extremely | bijzonder, uiterst, ontzettend |\n| too | te, veel te |\n\n- `best` means *quite, pretty*, not *the best*: `best goed` is pretty good, polite rather than enthusiastic. `best wel` is the informal version.\n- `nogal` is *rather*, and often carries a hint of criticism: `nogal duur`.\n- `zeer` is formal, `ontzettend` informal, `enigszins` formal.\n\n> De film was best goed, maar ik had er meer van verwacht. — The film was pretty good, but I had expected more of it.\n> Het restaurant is nogal duur, maar ik kan het toch aanraden. — The restaurant is rather expensive, but I can still recommend it.\n\n### Adverb or adjective?\n\nA degree word says something about the adjective, not about the noun, so it is an **adverb** and gets **no ending**, also when the adjective next to it does: `een erg dure telefoon`, `een vrij lange film`, `een ontzettend lekker gerecht`. Compare `een echt goede film` (a really good film) with `een echte, goede film` (a real film, and a good one).\n\n`heel` is the exception you will hear: in speech `een hele mooie film` is very common, but in writing it is `een heel mooie film`.\n\n> Het is een uiterst spannende film met een teleurstellend einde. — It is an extremely exciting film with a disappointing ending.\n\n### Much or little, early or late\n\n| word | meaning | example |\n| al | already, as early as | al om vijf uur |\n| wel | as many as | wel een uur |\n| pas | not until, only (so far) | pas om negen uur |\n| maar, slechts | only (few) | maar twee sterren |\n| zeker, minstens | at least | minstens vier sterren |\n| hooguit | at most | hooguit tien euro |\n| ruim, bijna | well over, almost | ruim een uur |\n\n> We moesten wel een uur op ons eten wachten. — We had to wait as long as an hour for our food.\n> De telefoon is pas twee maanden oud en hij doet het nu al niet meer. — The phone is only two months old and it already does not work any more.\n\n### Four words for 'only'\n\n- `alleen`: only, **nothing or nobody else**: `Alleen de bediening viel tegen.`\n- `maar`: only, **a small amount**: `maar tien euro`, `maar twee sterren`.\n- `slechts`: the same as `maar`, in formal writing.\n- `pas`: only with **time and age**, when it is late or still early days: `pas om negen uur` (not until nine), `pas twintig` (only twenty), `pas twee hoofdstukken` (only two so far, more will follow).\n\nSo *only at ten* is `pas om tien uur`, never *maar om tien uur.\n\n### Focus words: where they stand\n\n`zelfs`, `ook`, `alleen`, `vooral`, `juist` point at the word directly behind them: `Zelfs de prijs viel mee` (even the price). In the middle of the sentence they point at what follows: `De prijs viel zelfs mee` (it was even better than expected). `eigenlijk` (actually) and `inderdaad` (indeed) are about the whole sentence and stand first or right after the verb and subject.\n\n> Eigenlijk vond ik de film best goed. — Actually I thought the film was quite good.\n\n### graag, liever, het liefst\n\n`graag` (like to), `liever` (prefer to), `het liefst` (like most of all). A comparison after `liever` takes `dan`: `Ik eet liever thuis dan in een restaurant.`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *een erge dure auto → `een erg dure auto`.\n- *maar om tien uur → `pas om tien uur`.\n- `best` is not *the best*: that is `de beste`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `лише` and `тільки` cover all four Dutch words for *only*. Ask yourself which one you mean: nothing else (`alleen`), few (`maar`), or late (`pas`).","words":[{"nl":"recensent","art":"de","pl":"recensenten","en":"critic / reviewer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beoordeling","art":"de","pl":"beoordelingen","en":"rating / assessment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanrader","art":"de","pl":"aanraders","en":"something worth recommending","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bediening","art":"de","pl":"","en":"service (in a restaurant)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verwachting","art":"de","pl":"verwachtingen","en":"expectation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"prijs-kwaliteitverhouding","art":"de","pl":"","en":"value for money","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tegenvallen","past":"viel tegen","perf":"is tegengevallen","en":"to be disappointing","pos":"v"},{"nl":"meevallen","past":"viel mee","perf":"is meegevallen","en":"to be better than expected","pos":"v"},{"nl":"lovend","en":"full of praise","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"overtreffen","past":"overtrof","perf":"heeft overtroffen","en":"to exceed / to surpass","pos":"v"},{"nl":"teleurstellend","en":"disappointing","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"middelmatig","en":"mediocre","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"uitstekend","en":"excellent","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"overdreven","en":"exaggerated / over the top","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"uiterst","en":"extremely","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De film was best goed, maar ik had er meer van verwacht.","en":"The film was pretty good, but I had expected more of it.","hint":["film","best","goed","meer","verwachten"],"note":"Best goed is 'pretty good', a polite and mild judgement, not 'the best'. That is why the maar-clause fits.","alt":["De film was best wel goed, maar ik had er meer van verwacht."]},{"nl":"Het restaurant is nogal duur, maar ik kan het toch aanraden.","en":"The restaurant is rather expensive, but I can still recommend it.","hint":["restaurant","nogal","duur","toch","aanraden"],"note":"Nogal is 'rather', with a hint of criticism, which toch then softens. The separable verb aanraden stays in one piece at the end after kan.","alt":["Het restaurant is vrij duur, maar ik kan het toch aanraden.","Het restaurant is nogal duur, maar ik kan het wel aanraden."]},{"nl":"Het is een uiterst spannende film met een teleurstellend einde.","en":"It is an extremely exciting film with a disappointing ending.","hint":["uiterst","spannend","film","teleurstellend","einde"],"note":"Uiterst is an adverb before the adjective, so it has no ending, while spannende does. Einde is a het-word, so after een it is teleurstellend without -e."},{"nl":"Volgens de recensent is het een heel originele film.","en":"According to the critic it is a very original film.","hint":["volgens","recensent","heel","origineel","film"],"note":"In writing heel has no ending, because it is an adverb: een heel originele film. In speech een hele originele film is very common.","alt":["Volgens de recensent is het een zeer originele film.","Volgens de recensent is het een erg originele film.","Volgens de recensent is het een hele originele film."]},{"nl":"We moesten wel een uur op ons eten wachten.","en":"We had to wait as long as an hour for our food.","hint":["moeten","wel","uur","eten","wachten"],"note":"Wel before an amount means 'as much as': the writer thinks an hour is a lot. Maar een uur would say it was short.","alt":["We moesten wel een uur wachten op ons eten.","Wij moesten wel een uur op ons eten wachten.","We moesten maar liefst een uur op ons eten wachten."]},{"nl":"De telefoon is pas twee maanden oud en hij doet het nu al niet meer.","en":"The phone is only two months old and it already does not work any more.","hint":["telefoon","pas","maand","oud","al"],"note":"Pas with an age or a time: only, and the writer thinks that is young. Al: already, earlier than expected.","alt":["De telefoon is pas twee maanden oud en nu doet hij het al niet meer.","De telefoon is nog maar twee maanden oud en hij doet het nu al niet meer."]},{"nl":"De film kreeg slechts twee sterren, maar ik vond hem een echte aanrader.","en":"The film got only two stars, but I thought it was a real must-see.","hint":["film","krijgen","slechts","ster","aanrader"],"note":"Slechts is the formal word for maar: only, a small number. Echte has an ending here, because it is an adjective with the noun aanrader.","alt":["De film kreeg maar twee sterren, maar ik vond hem een echte aanrader.","De film kreeg slechts twee sterren, maar ik vond het een echte aanrader."]},{"nl":"Alleen de bediening viel tegen; het eten was uitstekend.","en":"Only the service was disappointing; the food was excellent.","hint":["alleen","bediening","tegenvallen","eten","uitstekend"],"note":"Alleen means 'nothing else': everything else was good. Maar and slechts are for small amounts, so they do not fit here."},{"nl":"Het eten overtrof al mijn verwachtingen, en zelfs de prijs viel mee.","en":"The food exceeded all my expectations, and even the price was better than expected.","hint":["eten","overtreffen","verwachting","zelfs","prijs","meevallen"],"note":"Zelfs stands directly before de prijs, the word it points at. Al here is 'all' (al mijn), not the focus word 'already'."},{"nl":"Ik bestel liever iets eenvoudigs dan zo'n overdreven gerecht.","en":"I would rather order something simple than such an over-the-top dish.","hint":["bestellen","liever","eenvoudig","overdreven","gerecht"],"note":"Liever is the comparative of graag, so the comparison continues with dan. Overdreven has no ending: gerecht is a het-word after zo'n (= zo een).","alt":["Ik bestel liever iets simpels dan zo'n overdreven gerecht."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het was een ___ lange film, maar ik heb me geen moment verveeld.","options":["erge","erg","ergere"],"correct":1,"en":"It was a very long film, but I was not bored for a moment. (in writing)","note":"Erg says how long, so it is an adverb and stays without ending. Only lange, the adjective with film, gets the -e. Erge lange is heard in speech, like hele lange."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De recensent schrijft dat het een ___ sterke voorstelling is.","options":["hele","helemaal","heel"],"correct":2,"en":"The critic writes that it is a very strong performance. (in writing)","note":"In writing heel is an adverb without ending: een heel sterke voorstelling. Hele is what you hear in speech. Helemaal means 'completely'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het restaurant gaat ___ om zes uur open; dat vind ik laat.","options":["pas","al","maar"],"correct":0,"en":"The restaurant does not open until six; I think that is late.","note":"Pas with a time means 'not until': the writer thinks it is late. Al would mean 'already', and maar cannot be used with a clock time."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ het dessert was lekker; de rest was middelmatig.","options":["Pas","Alleen","Zelfs"],"correct":1,"en":"Only the dessert was good; the rest was mediocre.","note":"Only in the sense 'nothing else' is alleen. Pas is for time and age, and zelfs (even) would not fit with a mediocre rest."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik eet ___ thuis dan in zo'n overdreven duur restaurant.","options":["graag","het liefst","liever"],"correct":2,"en":"I would rather eat at home than in such an outrageously expensive restaurant.","note":"Dan shows a comparison, so the comparative of graag is needed: liever … dan. Graag and het liefst do not take dan."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het boek kost ___ tien euro; dat is echt niet veel.","options":["al","pas","maar"],"correct":2,"en":"The book costs only ten euros; that really is not much.","note":"Maar before an amount: only, and the writer thinks it is little. Al would mean 'already' and pas is for time."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We hebben ___ (as many as) tien recensies gelezen voordat we het hotel boekten.","answers":["wel","maar liefst"],"en":"We read as many as ten reviews before we booked the hotel.","note":"Wel or, more formally, maar liefst before an amount means 'as many as': the speaker thinks it is a lot."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit product kreeg in elke beoordeling ___ (at least) vier sterren.","answers":["minstens","minimaal","ten minste","zeker","op zijn minst","op z'n minst"],"en":"This product got at least four stars in every rating.","note":"At least is minstens, minimaal, ten minste or op zijn minst; zeker also works with an amount. The opposite is hooguit (at most)."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het was een ___ (behoorlijk) teleurstellende avond.","answers":["behoorlijk"],"en":"It was a pretty disappointing evening.","note":"Behoorlijk says how disappointing, so it is an adverb without ending. Only teleurstellende, the adjective with avond, gets an -e."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De zaak is ___ (only) een maand open, maar er staan al veel lovende recensies online.","answers":["pas","nog maar"],"en":"The place has only been open for a month, but there are already many glowing reviews online.","note":"Only with time, meaning 'not long yet', is pas, or nog maar. Maar alone would suggest it opens for one month only."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vanavond","words":["begint","de film","pas","om tien uur"],"answers":["Vanavond begint de film pas om tien uur."],"en":"Tonight the film does not start until ten o'clock.","note":"Vanavond comes first, so the verb begint follows and then the subject. Pas stands directly before the time it is about."},{"type":"order","lead":"Eigenlijk","words":["vond","ik","de film","best","goed"],"answers":["Eigenlijk vond ik de film best goed."],"en":"Actually I thought the film was quite good.","note":"Eigenlijk, about the whole sentence, takes the first place; the verb vond follows and then ik. Best stands directly before goed."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het eten was uitstekend, en","words":["zelfs","de prijs","viel","mee"],"answers":["Het eten was uitstekend, en zelfs de prijs viel mee.","Het eten was uitstekend, en de prijs viel zelfs mee."],"en":"The food was excellent, and even the price was better than expected.","note":"Zelfs stands before the word it points at: zelfs de prijs. After the verb, de prijs viel zelfs mee, it means much the same."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Pas na een uur.","from":"Het eten kwam pas na een uur.","answers":["Pas na een uur kwam het eten."],"en":"The food did not come until an hour later.","note":"Pas belongs to na een uur and moves with it to the first place. The verb kwam stays second, and het eten follows it."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het is een vrije dure telefoon, maar de prijs-kwaliteitverhouding is goed.","answers":["Het is een vrij dure telefoon, maar de prijs-kwaliteitverhouding is goed."],"en":"It is a fairly expensive phone, but it is good value for money.","note":"Vrij says how expensive, so it is an adverb and has no ending. Only dure, the adjective with telefoon, gets an -e."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De winkel gaat maar om tien uur open, dus we moeten nog even wachten.","answers":["De winkel gaat pas om tien uur open, dus we moeten nog even wachten."],"en":"The shop does not open until ten, so we have to wait a little longer.","note":"With a clock time, 'only' meaning 'not until' is pas. Maar is for small amounts: maar tien euro."}]},
  {"id":"b2-21","level":"B2","icon":"🏰","title":"History and tradition","grammar":"Fixed formal phrases: old case forms and the subjunctive","summary":"Old case forms live on in fixed phrases: 's avonds, des te beter, ten slotte, ter ere van, wiens / wier. The old subjunctive lives on in wishes: Leve de koning!, Het ga je goed, Moge … Learn each one whole; never build new ones.","explain":"Old Dutch had cases, like Ukrainian, and a form for wishes, the subjunctive (*de aanvoegende wijs*). Modern Dutch lost both, but they survive in fixed phrases, some everyday, some ceremonial. Learn each one as one piece; do not build new ones.\n\n### The old genitive: 's, des, der\n\n`'s` is short for `des`, the old 'of the' (the genitive, *de tweede naamval*) for masculine and neuter words; `der` for feminine and plural ones.\n\n| phrase | meaning | where |\n| 's morgens, 's avonds, 's nachts | in the morning, evening, night | everyday |\n| 's zomers, 's winters | in summer, in winter | everyday |\n| een dezer dagen | one of these days | everyday |\n| hoe …, des te … | the …, the … | everyday |\n| 's werelds oudste | the world's oldest | news |\n| het Koninkrijk der Nederlanden | the Kingdom of the Netherlands | official |\n| de heer des huizes | the master of the house | ceremonial |\n\n> 's Avonds is het paleis verlicht. — In the evening the palace is lit up.\n> Hoe ouder het gebouw is, des te strenger zijn de regels. — The older the building, the stricter the rules.\n\nAt the start of a sentence the next word gets the capital: `'s Avonds` (never *s'avonds). Place names add a hyphen: `'s-Hertogenbosch` (the duke's forest).\n\nFormal 'whose' is `wiens` after a masculine or neuter singular word, `wier` after a feminine word or a plural. In speech: `van wie` (unit B2 · 8).\n\n> Het zijn families wier namen iedereen kent. — They are families whose names everyone knows.\n\n### The old dative: ten and ter\n\nAfter `te` the old dative article (*de derde naamval*) merged with it: `ten` = te + den (masculine and neuter), `ter` = te + der (feminine). You cannot tell masculine from feminine de-words today, so learn which a phrase takes: `ten tijde van` but `ter wereld`.\n\n- **Everyday**: `ten eerste` (firstly), `ten slotte` (finally), `ten minste` (at least), `ter plaatse` (on the spot), `ter wereld` (in the world), `van harte` (sincerely), `met name` (especially).\n- **Formal**: `ten gevolge van` (as a result of), `ten behoeve van` (for the benefit of), `ten opzichte van` (compared with), `ter gelegenheid van` (on the occasion of), `ter ere van` (in honour of), `ter attentie van` (for the attention of), `te allen tijde` (at all times).\n- **Ceremonial**: `in dier voege` (thus), `in den beginne` (in the beginning).\n\n> Ter ere van de bevrijding is er een concert. — There is a concert in honour of the liberation.\n\nTwo pairs differ in meaning. `ten minste` = not less than; `tenminste` = at least, that is (correcting yourself). `ten slotte` = finally; `tenslotte` = after all. The official word list keeps them apart, though many people mix them up.\n\n### The subjunctive: wishes in set formulas\n\nThe old subjunctive is the stem + `-e` (but `zij`, `ga`). Only fixed formulas survive:\n\n| formula | meaning |\n| Leve de koning! / Lang leve … | Long live the king! |\n| Het ga je goed! | All the best! |\n| God zij dank. | Thank God. |\n| Moge het nieuwe jaar … | May the new year … |\n| koste wat het kost | at any price |\n| als het ware / zij het | as it were / albeit |\n| hetzij … hetzij / het zij zo | either … or / so be it |\n| Wat er ook gebeure / Men neme … | Whatever may happen / Take … |\n\n`Als het ware`, `koste wat het kost` (also `koste wat kost`), `God zij dank` and `Het ga je goed` are everyday Dutch, and `Leve` is used freely: `Leve de vakantie!` `Moge`, `zij het` and `hetzij` are formal; `Men neme` (old recipes) and `Wat er ook gebeure` sound old or jokey. `dankzij` (once `dank zij`, 'thanks be') only goes with a good cause.\n\n> Moge dit monument ons altijd aan de oorlog herinneren. — May this monument always remind us of the war.\n> Het was een succes, zij het een klein succes. — It was a success, albeit a small one.\n\n### Word order\n\nEach phrase is one element; in first place the verb follows it: `Ten slotte legde de koning een krans`. So does the verb after `des te` + comparative. A `wat … ook` clause stands outside the sentence, and the main clause keeps its normal order: `Wat er ook gebeurt, wij blijven.`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Dankzij de storm is het beschadigd: a bad cause takes `door`, `vanwege` or `ten gevolge van`.\n- No new forms: *Hij kome morgen, *ten auto. Say `Hij komt morgen`, `met de auto`.\n- *de prinses, wiens moeder: for a woman it is `wier`.\n- No t in a wish: `Het ga je goed!` *Het gaat je goed is a statement: 'you are doing well'.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`Хай живе король!` is `Leve de koning!`; outside such formulas Dutch says `Ik hoop dat …`.","words":[{"nl":"vorstenhuis","art":"het","pl":"vorstenhuizen","en":"royal house / dynasty","pos":"n"},{"nl":"troon","art":"de","pl":"tronen","en":"throne","pos":"n"},{"nl":"inhuldiging","art":"de","pl":"inhuldigingen","en":"inauguration (of a monarch)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"staatshoofd","art":"het","pl":"staatshoofden","en":"head of state","pos":"n"},{"nl":"opvolger","art":"de","pl":"opvolgers","en":"successor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"regeren","past":"regeerde","perf":"heeft geregeerd","en":"to reign / to govern","pos":"v"},{"nl":"herdenking","art":"de","pl":"herdenkingen","en":"commemoration","pos":"n"},{"nl":"herdenken","past":"herdacht","perf":"heeft herdacht","en":"to commemorate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"gedenkteken","art":"het","pl":"gedenktekens","en":"memorial","pos":"n"},{"nl":"monarchie","art":"de","pl":"monarchieën","en":"monarchy","pos":"n"},{"nl":"plechtigheid","art":"de","pl":"plechtigheden","en":"ceremony","pos":"n"},{"nl":"plechtig","en":"solemn","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"bevrijding","art":"de","pl":"bevrijdingen","en":"liberation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"erfgoed","art":"het","pl":"","en":"heritage","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"overlevering","art":"de","pl":"overleveringen","en":"tradition (passed down) / legend","pos":"n"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ter gelegenheid van de inhuldiging was de hele stad oranje versierd.","en":"On the occasion of the inauguration the whole city was decorated in orange.","hint":["gelegenheid","inhuldiging","stad","oranje","versieren"],"note":"ter = te + der, the old dative of a feminine word (de gelegenheid). The whole phrase is one element in first place, so the verb was follows it.","alt":["De hele stad was ter gelegenheid van de inhuldiging oranje versierd.","Ter gelegenheid van de inhuldiging werd de hele stad oranje versierd."]},{"nl":"Op 4 mei herdenken we 's avonds de slachtoffers van de oorlog.","en":"On 4 May we commemorate the victims of the war in the evening.","hint":["4 mei","herdenken","avond","slachtoffer","oorlog"],"note":"'s avonds is the old genitive des avonds. The apostrophe stands before the s, and inside the sentence the word stays lower case.","alt":["We herdenken op 4 mei 's avonds de slachtoffers van de oorlog.","Op 4 mei 's avonds herdenken we de slachtoffers van de oorlog."]},{"nl":"Het gedenkteken werd ter ere van de gevallen soldaten onthuld.","en":"The memorial was unveiled in honour of the fallen soldiers.","hint":["gedenkteken","ere","gevallen","soldaat","onthullen"],"note":"ter ere van: ter + the old dative ere (from de eer). Never *ten ere or *te eer.","alt":["Het gedenkteken werd onthuld ter ere van de gevallen soldaten.","Ter ere van de gevallen soldaten werd het gedenkteken onthuld.","Het gedenkteken is ter ere van de gevallen soldaten onthuld.","Het gedenkteken is onthuld ter ere van de gevallen soldaten.","Ter ere van de gevallen soldaten is het gedenkteken onthuld."]},{"nl":"De koning, wiens opvolger nog een kind was, regeerde veertig jaar.","en":"The king, whose successor was still a child, reigned for forty years.","hint":["koning","opvolger","kind","regeren","jaar"],"note":"wiens is formal 'whose' for a masculine word: de koning. In speech you would also hear van wie de opvolger.","alt":["De koning, wiens opvolger nog een kind was, heeft veertig jaar geregeerd.","De koning, van wie de opvolger nog een kind was, regeerde veertig jaar."]},{"nl":"Dit is een van de oudste vorstenhuizen ter wereld.","en":"This is one of the oldest royal houses in the world.","hint":["oud","vorstenhuis","wereld"],"note":"ter wereld is the fixed phrase for 'in the world' after a superlative. The genitive 's werelds oudste vorstenhuizen says the same in the style of the news.","alt":["Dit is een van 's werelds oudste vorstenhuizen.","Dit is een van de oudste vorstenhuizen van de wereld.","Dit is één van de oudste vorstenhuizen ter wereld."]},{"nl":"Moge het nieuwe jaar u veel geluk en gezondheid brengen.","en":"May the new year bring you (formal) much happiness and health.","hint":["mogen","nieuw","jaar","geluk","gezondheid","brengen"],"note":"Moge is the old subjunctive of mogen: a wish, typical of greeting cards and speeches. It stands first, and brengen goes to the end."},{"nl":"Ten gevolge van de oorlog werd de plechtigheid een jaar uitgesteld.","en":"As a result of the war (ten …) the ceremony was postponed for a year.","hint":["gevolg","oorlog","plechtigheid","jaar","uitstellen"],"note":"ten = te + den, the old dative of a neuter word (het gevolg). Ten gevolge van is the formal way to name a cause, often a bad one.","alt":["De plechtigheid werd ten gevolge van de oorlog een jaar uitgesteld.","Ten gevolge van de oorlog is de plechtigheid een jaar uitgesteld.","De plechtigheid is ten gevolge van de oorlog een jaar uitgesteld."]},{"nl":"Dankzij de bevrijding werd het feest na vijf jaar weer gevierd.","en":"Thanks to the liberation the festival was celebrated again after five years.","hint":["dankzij","bevrijding","feest","jaar","vieren"],"note":"dankzij comes from dank zij, 'thanks be': it is only used for a good cause, like the liberation.","alt":["Dankzij de bevrijding werd het feest na vijf jaar opnieuw gevierd.","Het feest werd dankzij de bevrijding na vijf jaar weer gevierd.","Dankzij de bevrijding is het feest na vijf jaar weer gevierd."]},{"nl":"Het staatshoofd was zelf ter plaatse om de nabestaanden te steunen.","en":"The head of state was on the spot in person to support the bereaved.","hint":["staatshoofd","zelf","plaats","nabestaande","steunen"],"note":"ter plaatse: ter because de plaats was feminine, plaatse with the old dative -e. It means 'there, where it happened', a phrase of the news.","alt":["Het staatshoofd was zelf ter plekke om de nabestaanden te steunen."]},{"nl":"Ons erfgoed moet te allen tijde beschermd worden.","en":"Our heritage must be protected at all times.","hint":["erfgoed","moeten","allen","tijd","beschermen"],"note":"te allen tijde keeps two old dative endings, allen and tijde. It belongs to formal writing; in speech you say altijd.","alt":["Ons erfgoed moet te allen tijde worden beschermd."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Hoe plechtiger de ceremonie is, ___ te stiller wordt het publiek.","options":["des","de","dan","hoe"],"correct":0,"en":"The more solemn the ceremony, the quieter the audience becomes.","note":"des te is a fixed pair: des is the old genitive. You can say hoe … hoe stiller (without te), but before te only des fits. The verb follows straight after the comparative."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na de herdenking werd geld ingezameld ___ behoeve van de nabestaanden.","options":["ter","ten","te","voor"],"correct":1,"en":"After the commemoration money was collected for the benefit of the bereaved.","note":"The fixed phrase is ten behoeve van. You cannot work out ten or ter from modern de and het; learn the phrase whole."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de storm is het oude gedenkteken zwaar beschadigd.","options":["Dankzij","Ter ere van","Door"],"correct":2,"en":"Because of the storm the old memorial has been badly damaged.","note":"Damage is a bad result, so door. Dankzij (from 'thanks be') only goes with a good cause."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De troon is ___ driehonderd jaar oud.","options":["tenminste","ten minste","ter minste"],"correct":1,"en":"The throne is at least three hundred years old.","note":"'Not less than' is ten minste, two words. Tenminste, one word, corrects what you just said: 'at least, that is what I think'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We moeten het oude centrum beschermen; het is ___ ons erfgoed.","options":["ten slotte","ter slotte","tenslotte"],"correct":2,"en":"We must protect the old centre; it is our heritage, after all.","note":"'After all' is tenslotte, one word. Ten slotte, two words, means 'finally, as the last step'. Ter slotte does not exist."},{"type":"choose","nl":"U kunt de kaartjes voor het paleis ___ online, hetzij aan de kassa kopen.","options":["het zij","zowel","of","hetzij"],"correct":3,"en":"You can buy the tickets for the palace either online or at the ticket office.","note":"hetzij … hetzij is a fixed formal pair: either … or. The second hetzij needs a first one; zowel would need als, and of … of would need of twice."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Volgens de overlevering verscheen de geest van de graaf 's ___ (nacht) in het kasteel.","answers":["nachts"],"en":"According to tradition the count's ghost appeared in the castle at night.","note":"'s nachts is the old genitive des nachts, so the noun gets an s: nacht becomes nachts."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (gaan) je goed in je nieuwe land!","answers":["ga"],"en":"All the best in your new country!","note":"A wish in the old subjunctive: the stem + e, which for gaan is ga. Het gaat je goed would be a statement: 'you are doing well'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Lang ___ (leven) de koningin!","answers":["leve"],"en":"Long live the queen!","note":"leve is the subjunctive: the stem leef + e, with f becoming v. No t: it is a wish, not a statement."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De koningen, ___ portretten hier hangen, hebben allemaal lang geregeerd.","answers":["wier"],"en":"The kings, whose portraits hang here, all reigned for a long time.","note":"'whose' after a plural is wier. wiens is only for a masculine or neuter singular word."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ten slotte","words":["legde","het staatshoofd","een krans","bij het gedenkteken"],"answers":["Ten slotte legde het staatshoofd een krans bij het gedenkteken.","Ten slotte legde het staatshoofd bij het gedenkteken een krans."],"en":"Finally the head of state laid a wreath at the memorial.","note":"Ten slotte (finally) is one element in first place, so the verb legde comes next and then the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal: replace Door with ten gevolge van.","from":"Door de brand was het paleis maandenlang gesloten.","answers":["Ten gevolge van de brand was het paleis maandenlang gesloten."],"en":"As a result of the fire the palace was closed for months.","note":"Ten gevolge van takes the place of door as one element, so the inversion stays: was het paleis."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it in everyday Dutch: replace the old subjunctive by the present tense.","from":"Wat er ook gebeure, de monarchie blijft.","answers":["Wat er ook gebeurt, de monarchie blijft."],"en":"Whatever happens, the monarchy stays.","note":"gebeure is the old subjunctive; today it is gebeurt. The wat … ook clause stands outside the sentence, so the main clause keeps its normal order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn it into a ceremonial wish that starts with Moge.","from":"Wij hopen dat de nieuwe koning lang en wijs zal regeren.","answers":["Moge de nieuwe koning lang en wijs regeren!"],"en":"May the new king reign long and wisely!","note":"Moge is the subjunctive of mogen and takes the first place; the infinitive regeren goes to the end. The subject de nieuwe koning follows moge."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dankzij de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","answers":["Door de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Ten gevolge van de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Als gevolg van de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Vanwege de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Wegens de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld."],"en":"Because of the war the inauguration was postponed for a year.","note":"A war is not something to be thankful for, so dankzij is wrong. Use door, vanwege, wegens or the formal ten gevolge van."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De koningin, wiens zoon nu regeert, woont nog altijd in het paleis.","answers":["De koningin, wier zoon nu regeert, woont nog altijd in het paleis.","De koningin, van wie de zoon nu regeert, woont nog altijd in het paleis."],"en":"The queen, whose son now reigns, still lives in the palace.","note":"'whose' refers to de koningin, a woman, so it is wier. wiens is for a man or a masculine or neuter word."}]},
  {"id":"b2-22","level":"B2","icon":"🤵","title":"Formal and informal","grammar":"Register: u and je, written and spoken alternatives","summary":"Choose one level and stay in it. u takes the verb with t (u bent, u hebt / heeft, u kunt) and uw. Formal letters: Geachte …, Met vriendelijke groet. Soften with Zou u … kunnen? Formal words: al → reeds, als → indien.","explain":"Dutch has two words for 'you': `je / jij` and `u`. Choosing between them, and between everyday and formal words, is choosing a *register*. The rule at B2: pick one level for a whole text or conversation and stay in it.\n\n### u or je?\n\n`u` is for strangers in formal settings, officials, customers, and often for older people. `je` is for friends, family, children and, in most Dutch workplaces, colleagues and even the boss. When in doubt, start with `u`; the other person says `Zeg maar je` if they prefer. The older or more senior person usually proposes the switch: `Zullen we elkaar tutoyeren?` (`tutoyeren` = to say je).\n\n> Mag ik u iets vragen? — May I ask you something?\n> Zeg maar je, dat is makkelijker. — Just call me je, that is easier.\n\n### Verb forms with u\n\n`u` takes the verb form with t, also when it comes after the verb, where `je` loses the t: `Werkt u hier?` but `Werk je hier?`. A few verbs have two accepted forms:\n\n| | as with jij | also correct |\n| zijn | u bent | – |\n| hebben | u hebt | u heeft |\n| kunnen | u kunt | u kan |\n| zullen | u zult | u zal |\n\nNever *u is. In fact `u heeft` is heard more than `u hebt`; both are standard. `u kan` and `u zal` sound less formal. The possessive is `uw` (`uw adres`), the object form `u` (`Ik bel u morgen`). `u` is also the polite plural: one `u` for a whole group.\n\n### One level in one text\n\nOnce you choose `u`, everything follows: `u`, `uw`, the verb with t. Mixing is the commonest mistake in letters: *Kunt u laten weten of je komt? → `Kunt u laten weten of u komt?`\n\n### The formal letter or e-mail\n\n| part | formal | less formal |\n| salutation (aanhef) | Geachte heer Smit, / Geachte mevrouw Smit, | Beste Anna, |\n| reader unknown | Geachte heer, mevrouw, | – |\n| closing | Met vriendelijke groet, | Hartelijke groet, |\n| very formal closing | Hoogachtend, | – |\n\n`Geachte` goes with the surname, never with a first name. After the salutation comes a comma, and the letter begins on a new line with a capital letter. Fixed sentences:\n\n- `Naar aanleiding van …` — following, in response to …\n- `Hierbij stuur ik u …` — I hereby send you …\n- `Graag ontvang ik …` — I would like to receive …\n- `Ik zie uw reactie met belangstelling tegemoet.` — I look forward to your reply.\n- `Bij voorbaat dank.` — Thank you in advance.\n\n`Naar aanleiding van …`, `Hierbij` and `Graag` are the first element, so the verb follows: `Hierbij stuur ik u`, never *Hierbij ik stuur u.\n\n> Naar aanleiding van uw advertentie solliciteer ik naar deze functie. — In response to your advertisement I am applying for this position.\n\n### Softening a request\n\nEach step is more polite than the one before:\n\n> Kun je me helpen? — Can you help me?\n> Zou u mij misschien kunnen helpen? — Could you perhaps help me?\n> Ik vroeg me af of u mij zou kunnen helpen. — I was wondering whether you could help me.\n> Het zou fijn zijn als u mij kon helpen. — It would be nice if you could help me.\n\n`Ik vroeg me af` uses the past to sound modest; after `of` and `als` the verb goes to the end.\n\n### Everyday and formal words\n\n| everyday | formal |\n| maar | echter |\n| ook / al / nu | tevens / reeds / thans |\n| als (if) | indien |\n| over | betreffende, omtrent |\n| krijgen / vragen | ontvangen / verzoeken |\n| zeggen / kopen / helpen | mededelen / aanschaffen / assisteren |\n| omdat / daarom, dus | aangezien / derhalve |\n\n- `echter` is not a conjunction like `maar`: it stands inside the clause, usually after the verb: `Dat is echter niet mogelijk.`\n- `verzoeken` takes `te`, with or without `om`: `Wij verzoeken u (om) dit formulier te ondertekenen.`\n- `mededelen` is separable: `Wij delen u mede dat …` (`meedelen`, `delen … mee` is also correct).\n\nDo not overdo it: an ordinary e-mail to a company with `al`, `ook` and `als` is fine. The formal words belong in official letters.\n\n### Keep out of formal writing\n\n- `hun` as a subject: *Hun hebben gebeld is not standard Dutch at all. Write `Zij hebben gebeld`.\n- spoken forms: `ie` (`heeft ie` → `heeft hij`), `d'r` (for `haar` or `er`), `'t`, `'k`, `m'n`, `z'n`.\n- `heel erg`, `super`: write `zeer`.\n- the particles `hoor`, `even`, `toch`: `Kunt u even bellen?` → `Kunt u mij bellen?`\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`u` is like `ви`, but it takes a singular verb: `u bent`, `u werkt`, where Ukrainian says `ви працюєте`.","words":[{"nl":"onderhandeling","art":"de","pl":"onderhandelingen","en":"negotiation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"loonsverhoging","art":"de","pl":"loonsverhogingen","en":"pay rise","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verzoeken","past":"verzocht","perf":"heeft verzocht","en":"to request (formal)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"inwilligen","past":"willigde in","perf":"heeft ingewilligd","en":"to grant (a request)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"bezwaarschrift","art":"het","pl":"bezwaarschriften","en":"letter of objection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afhandelen","past":"handelde af","perf":"heeft afgehandeld","en":"to deal with / to settle","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"excuus","art":"het","pl":"excuses","en":"apology / excuse","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanhef","art":"de","pl":"aanheffen","en":"salutation (of a letter)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tegemoetkoming","art":"de","pl":"tegemoetkomingen","en":"compensation / allowance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ontvangstbevestiging","art":"de","pl":"ontvangstbevestigingen","en":"acknowledgement of receipt","pos":"n"},{"nl":"betreffende","en":"concerning / regarding","pos":"prep","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"aanspreken","past":"sprak aan","perf":"heeft aangesproken","en":"to address (a person)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"tutoyeren","past":"tutoyeerde","perf":"heeft getutoyeerd","en":"to say je (not u) to someone","pos":"v"},{"nl":"tegemoetzien","past":"zag tegemoet","perf":"heeft tegemoetgezien","en":"to look forward to (formal)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"zakelijk","en":"businesslike / professional","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Zullen we elkaar voortaan tutoyeren?","en":"Shall we say je to each other from now on?","hint":["zullen","elkaar","voortaan","tutoyeren"],"note":"This is how you propose the switch from u to je. After this question it would be odd to go back to u."},{"nl":"Hebt u de bijlage bij mijn e-mail ontvangen?","en":"Did you (formal) receive the attachment to my e-mail?","hint":["hebben","bijlage","e-mail","ontvangen"],"note":"With u both hebt and heeft are standard, also after the verb. u keeps its t where je loses it: Heb je …? but Hebt u …?","alt":["Heeft u de bijlage bij mijn e-mail ontvangen?","Hebt u de bijlage bij mijn e-mail gekregen?","Heeft u de bijlage bij mijn e-mail gekregen?"]},{"nl":"Naar aanleiding van ons gesprek stuur ik u hierbij mijn voorstel.","en":"Following our conversation I hereby send you (formal) my proposal.","hint":["naar aanleiding van","gesprek","sturen","hierbij","voorstel"],"note":"Naar aanleiding van ons gesprek is one long first element, so the verb stuur comes straight after it, then ik.","alt":["Hierbij stuur ik u naar aanleiding van ons gesprek mijn voorstel."]},{"nl":"Zou u mij misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht wordt afgehandeld?","en":"Could you (formal) perhaps tell me when my complaint will be dealt with?","hint":["zullen","misschien","kunnen","vertellen","klacht","afhandelen"],"note":"Zou … kunnen plus misschien makes the question soft. Zou has no t, even with u. In the sub-clause after wanneer the verbs go to the end.","alt":["Zou u mij misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht afgehandeld wordt?","Zou u me misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht wordt afgehandeld?","Zou u me misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht afgehandeld wordt?","Zou u mij misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht zal worden afgehandeld?","Zou u mij misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht afgehandeld zal worden?","Zou u mij misschien kunnen vertellen wanneer mijn klacht zal afgehandeld worden?"]},{"nl":"Ik vroeg me af of er ruimte is voor een loonsverhoging.","en":"I was wondering whether there is room for a pay rise.","hint":["zich afvragen","ruimte","loonsverhoging"],"note":"The past vroeg makes the question modest. Of means 'whether' and sends the verb is to the end of its clause.","alt":["Ik vroeg mij af of er ruimte is voor een loonsverhoging.","Ik vroeg me af of er ruimte voor een loonsverhoging is.","Ik vroeg mij af of er ruimte voor een loonsverhoging is."]},{"nl":"Indien u het niet eens bent met dit besluit, kunt u een bezwaarschrift indienen.","en":"If you (formal) do not agree with this decision, you can submit a letter of objection.","hint":["indien","het eens zijn","besluit","kunnen","bezwaarschrift","indienen"],"note":"Indien is the formal als. The sub-clause comes first, so the main clause starts with its verb: kunt u.","alt":["Indien u het met dit besluit niet eens bent, kunt u een bezwaarschrift indienen.","Indien u het niet eens bent met dit besluit, kan u een bezwaarschrift indienen.","Indien u het met dit besluit niet eens bent, kan u een bezwaarschrift indienen."]},{"nl":"Wij verzoeken u beleefd het formulier voor 1 juni terug te sturen.","en":"We kindly ask you to return the form before 1 June.","hint":["verzoeken","beleefd","formulier","juni","terugsturen"],"note":"verzoeken is the formal vragen and takes te + infinitive at the end; te goes between the parts of terugsturen.","alt":["Wij verzoeken u beleefd om het formulier voor 1 juni terug te sturen.","Wij verzoeken u vriendelijk het formulier voor 1 juni terug te sturen.","Wij verzoeken u vriendelijk om het formulier voor 1 juni terug te sturen.","Wij verzoeken u beleefd het formulier vóór 1 juni terug te sturen."]},{"nl":"Ik zie uw reactie met belangstelling tegemoet.","en":"I look forward to your reply. (formal closing line)","hint":["tegemoetzien","reactie","belangstelling"],"note":"The standard last line of a formal letter. tegemoetzien is separable, so tegemoet goes to the end.","alt":["Ik zie uw reactie met interesse tegemoet.","Ik zie uw antwoord met belangstelling tegemoet."]},{"nl":"Wij bieden u onze excuses aan voor het ongemak.","en":"We offer you our apologies for the inconvenience.","hint":["aanbieden","excuus","ongemak"],"note":"A fixed formal phrase: excuses aanbieden. The phrase with voor may stand after the separated aan or before it.","alt":["Wij bieden u onze excuses voor het ongemak aan."]},{"nl":"In een zakelijke e-mail spreek je een onbekende altijd met u aan.","en":"In a business e-mail you always address a stranger as u.","hint":["zakelijk","e-mail","aanspreken","onbekende","altijd"],"note":"Here je means 'people in general', which is fine in a text about rules. aanspreken is separable: aan goes to the end.","alt":["Een onbekende spreek je in een zakelijke e-mail altijd met u aan.","Je spreekt een onbekende in een zakelijke e-mail altijd met u aan.","In een zakelijke e-mail spreek je een onbekende altijd aan met u."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mevrouw, u ___ te laat voor uw afspraak.","options":["is","bent","zijn"],"correct":1,"en":"Madam, you are late for your appointment.","note":"u takes the same form as jij: u bent. *u is is the old third-person form; it is no longer used."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ u morgen om tien uur tijd voor een gesprek?","options":["Is","Hebben","Heeft","Bent"],"correct":2,"en":"Do you (formal) have time for a meeting at ten tomorrow?","note":"Tijd hebben, so a form of hebben, and u is singular: heeft. Hebt u … is just as correct; only the plural hebben is wrong."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Zou u mij misschien ___ helpen?","options":["kunnen","kunt","kan"],"correct":0,"en":"Could you (formal) perhaps help me?","note":"zou is the conjugated verb; kunnen goes to the end as an infinitive. Two conjugated verbs in one clause is impossible."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik vroeg me af ___ de onderhandelingen al zijn afgerond.","options":["dat","als","of"],"correct":2,"en":"I was wondering whether the negotiations have been concluded yet.","note":"'Whether' is of. Als introduces a condition ('if, in case'), not a question, so standard Dutch does not use it after zich afvragen; dat does not fit a question either."},{"type":"choose","nl":"In een officiële brief aan de gemeente schrijf je in de aanhef: ___ heer, mevrouw.","options":["Lieve","Geachte","Hoi"],"correct":1,"en":"In an official letter to the municipality you write in the salutation: Dear Sir or Madam.","note":"Geachte is the formal salutation. Lieve is for family and friends, Hoi for a quick message to someone you know."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mevrouw, ___ (hebben) u de ontvangstbevestiging al ontvangen?","answers":["hebt","heeft"],"en":"Madam, have you received the acknowledgement of receipt yet?","note":"With u both hebt and heeft are standard Dutch. The t stays, also when u follows the verb."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij hebben uw brief ___ (formal: al) ontvangen.","answers":["reeds"],"en":"We have already received your letter.","note":"reeds is the formal word for al (already). It stands in the same place in the sentence."},{"type":"gap","nl":"U kunt ons bellen ___ (formal: als) u nog vragen heeft.","answers":["indien"],"en":"You can call us if you have any further questions.","note":"indien is the formal als in the sense 'if'. Like als, it sends the verb heeft to the end of its clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij delen u ___ (formal: mee) dat uw verzoek om een tegemoetkoming is ingewilligd.","answers":["mede"],"en":"We inform you that your request for compensation has been granted.","note":"mededelen is the formal form of meedelen; in the main clause the particle mede splits off and stands before the dat-clause."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hierbij","words":["maak","ik","bezwaar","tegen het besluit van 12 mei"],"answers":["Hierbij maak ik bezwaar tegen het besluit van 12 mei.","Hierbij maak ik tegen het besluit van 12 mei bezwaar."],"en":"I hereby object to the decision of 12 May.","note":"Hierbij is the first element, so the verb maak follows and then ik. Bezwaar maken tegen is the fixed phrase of official objections."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zou","words":["u","mij","misschien","kunnen","terugbellen"],"answers":["Zou u mij misschien kunnen terugbellen?"],"en":"Could you perhaps call me back?","note":"A soft request starts with Zou. The short pronoun mij comes right after u, before misschien; the infinitives close the sentence."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal: say u to the reader.","from":"Kun je me je adres sturen?","answers":["Kunt u mij uw adres sturen?","Kunt u me uw adres sturen?","Kan u mij uw adres sturen?","Kan u me uw adres sturen?"],"en":"Could you send me your address?","note":"With u the verb keeps its t (kunt u; kan u is also correct), and the possessive je becomes uw. Change every je, not only the first."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it informal: say je to a colleague.","from":"Hebt u morgen tijd voor een gesprek?","answers":["Heb je morgen tijd voor een gesprek?","Heb jij morgen tijd voor een gesprek?"],"en":"Do you have time for a chat tomorrow?","note":"u keeps the t after the verb, je and jij lose it: heb je, never *hebt je."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal: use betreffende for over, ontvangen for gekregen, and echter for maar.","from":"Wij hebben uw brief over de factuur gekregen, maar wij kunnen uw verzoek niet inwilligen.","answers":["Wij hebben uw brief betreffende de factuur ontvangen; wij kunnen uw verzoek echter niet inwilligen.","Wij hebben uw brief betreffende de factuur ontvangen; wij kunnen echter uw verzoek niet inwilligen."],"en":"We have received your letter concerning the invoice; however, we cannot grant your request.","note":"echter is not a conjunction: it cannot take the place of maar between two clauses. It goes inside the second clause, after the verb kunnen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hun hebben mijn klacht nog steeds niet afgehandeld.","answers":["Zij hebben mijn klacht nog steeds niet afgehandeld."],"en":"They still have not dealt with my complaint.","note":"hun is an object form; as a subject it is heard in speech but it is not standard Dutch. The subject is zij or ze."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Kunt u mij laten weten of je de bijlage hebt ontvangen?","answers":["Kunt u mij laten weten of u de bijlage hebt ontvangen?","Kunt u mij laten weten of u de bijlage heeft ontvangen?","Kunt u mij laten weten of u de bijlage ontvangen hebt?","Kunt u mij laten weten of u de bijlage ontvangen heeft?"],"en":"Could you let me know whether you have received the attachment?","note":"The sentence starts with u, so it must stay u: switching to je halfway is the classic register mistake."}]},
  {"id":"b2-23","level":"B2","icon":"🌐","title":"Society and change","grammar":"Long sentences: combining clauses without losing the thread","summary":"Every clause keeps its own order: verb second in a main clause, verbs at the end in a sub-clause, also after en or maar inside it. A clause in first place is followed by the verb. Check the subject, the auxiliary, er and wat.","explain":"A B2 text often packs three or four clauses into one sentence. Nothing new happens in the grammar: each clause keeps its own rules. The skill is to see which clause every verb belongs to, and to keep track of the subject.\n\n### Each clause has its own verb place\n\nIn a main clause the conjugated verb stands second. In a sub-clause (after `dat`, `omdat`, `of`, `hoewel`, `die`, `wat` …) all the verbs go to the end.\n\n| clause | verb |\n| Ik denk | denk: second place |\n| dat hij niet komt | komt: end |\n| omdat hij moet werken | moet werken: end |\n\n> Ik denk dat hij niet komt omdat hij moet werken. — I think he is not coming because he has to work.\n\nAt the end of a sub-clause two verbs may stand in either order: `dat ze is gevlucht` or `dat ze gevlucht is`; `omdat hij moet werken` or, less often, `omdat hij werken moet`. Both are correct.\n\n### A clause inside a clause\n\nA relative clause can sit inside a dat-clause. The inner clause is finished first, then the verb of the outer clause follows. A comma after the inner clause helps the reader see where it ends.\n\n> Ik hoop dat de mensen die hier wonen, tevreden zijn. — I hope that the people who live here are satisfied.\n\n`wonen` belongs to the die-clause, `zijn` to the dat-clause, and its subject is `de mensen`.\n\n### A clause in first place\n\nA whole sub-clause or infinitive clause can be the first element. The main clause then starts with its verb, followed by the subject, however long the first element is.\n\n> Omdat de huren stijgen, verhuizen veel gezinnen. — Because rents are rising, many families are moving.\n> Om de taal te leren, volgt zij een cursus. — To learn the language, she is taking a course.\n\nThe exception is a clause with `wat … ook`, `hoe … ook` or `al` (unit B2 · 6): it stands outside the sentence, so no inversion (`Wat je ook zegt, ik blijf`).\n\n### en and maar inside a sub-clause\n\nAfter `en` or `maar` the sub-clause simply continues, so the verb stays at the end. The subject may be left out only when it is the same.\n\n> Ze blijft thuis omdat ze ziek is en niet kan werken. — She is staying at home because she is ill and cannot work.\n> Het gaat goed, omdat de kinderen naar school gaan en de ouders werk hebben. — Things are going well, because the children go to school and the parents have work.\n\n*omdat ze ziek is en kan niet werken is wrong: the second half still belongs to the omdat-clause.\n\n### Passive, relative clause and connector in one\n\n> De wet die vorig jaar werd aangenomen, wordt nu ingevoerd, maar veel gemeenten zijn nog niet klaar. — The law that was passed last year is now being introduced, but many municipalities are not ready yet.\n\nThree clauses: the die-clause (verbs at the end), the main clause (`wordt` second, subject `de wet`) and a second main clause after `maar`, which does not count as an element (`zijn` second).\n\n### Typical B2 errors\n\n- **Main-clause order in a sub-clause**: *dat de samenleving wordt diverser → `dat de samenleving diverser wordt`.\n- **Forgotten inversion** after a long first element: *Na drie jaar in de opvang het gezin kreeg een huis → `kreeg het gezin`.\n- **Wrong auxiliary**: a change of place or state takes `zijn`: `dat hij is geëmigreerd`, `dat het aantal is gestegen`.\n- **A lost er**: an indefinite subject keeps its `er` in a sub-clause too: `of er genoeg woningen zijn`.\n- **The verb agrees with the nearest noun**: *Het aantal vluchtelingen zijn gestegen. The subject is `het aantal`, the number itself (unlike `een aantal`, unit B2 · 16): `is gestegen`.\n- **dat for wat**: when it refers to a whole clause it is `wat` (formally also `hetgeen`): `Hij kwam niet, wat ik jammer vond.` After `alles` the normal choice is `wat`: `alles wat ik weet`. After `iets` and `niets` both `wat` and `dat` are used.\n\n### How to shorten\n\n- Split into two sentences, and link them with a word like `daarom` or `bovendien`.\n- Move the heavy part to the end: `Het is bekend dat …` rather than `Dat …, is bekend`.\n- Replace a dat-clause by `te` + infinitive when the subject is the same: `Zij hoopt dat zij werk vindt` → `Zij hoopt werk te vinden`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian keeps the same order in main and sub-clauses: `Я думаю, що він не прийде`. Dutch sends the verb to the end of every sub-clause, however many there are, so check each clause separately.","words":[{"nl":"samenleving","art":"de","pl":"samenlevingen","en":"society","pos":"n"},{"nl":"nieuwkomer","art":"de","pl":"nieuwkomers","en":"newcomer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vluchteling","art":"de","pl":"vluchtelingen","en":"refugee","pos":"n"},{"nl":"asielzoeker","art":"de","pl":"asielzoekers","en":"asylum seeker","pos":"n"},{"nl":"opvang","art":"de","pl":"","en":"reception / shelter (for refugees)","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"inburgering","art":"de","pl":"","en":"civic integration","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"afkomst","art":"de","pl":"","en":"origin / descent","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"diversiteit","art":"de","pl":"","en":"diversity","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"discriminatie","art":"de","pl":"","en":"discrimination","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"vooroordeel","art":"het","pl":"vooroordelen","en":"prejudice","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ongelijkheid","art":"de","pl":"ongelijkheden","en":"inequality","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitsluiting","art":"de","pl":"uitsluitingen","en":"exclusion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"maatschappelijk","en":"social / societal","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"verwelkomen","past":"verwelkomde","perf":"heeft verwelkomd","en":"to welcome","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich vestigen","past":"vestigde me","perf":"heeft zich gevestigd","en":"to settle (in a place)","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik denk dat veel nieuwkomers zich hier welkom voelen omdat de buren hen helpen.","en":"I think that many newcomers feel welcome here because the neighbours help them.","hint":["denken","nieuwkomer","zich voelen","welkom","buurman","helpen"],"note":"One main clause and two sub-clauses: denk stands second, voelen and helpen stand at the end of their own clause.","alt":["Ik denk dat veel nieuwkomers zich hier welkom voelen omdat de buren ze helpen."]},{"nl":"Uit onderzoek blijkt dat de vluchtelingen die hier al jaren wonen, goed Nederlands spreken.","en":"Research shows (uit … blijkt) that the refugees who have lived here for years speak good Dutch.","hint":["onderzoek","blijken","vluchteling","wonen","Nederlands","spreken"],"note":"The die-clause sits inside the dat-clause. It closes with wonen; then the dat-clause goes on, and its verb spreken comes last.","alt":["Uit onderzoek blijkt dat de vluchtelingen die al jaren hier wonen, goed Nederlands spreken."]},{"nl":"Omdat de opvang in veel gemeenten vol zit, moeten asielzoekers vaak lang wachten.","en":"Because reception (de opvang) is full in many municipalities, asylum seekers often have to wait a long time.","hint":["omdat","opvang","gemeente","vol","asielzoeker","wachten"],"note":"The whole omdat-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with its verb moeten, then the subject asielzoekers.","alt":["Omdat de opvang in veel gemeenten vol is, moeten asielzoekers vaak lang wachten.","Asielzoekers moeten vaak lang wachten omdat de opvang in veel gemeenten vol zit.","Asielzoekers moeten vaak lang wachten omdat de opvang in veel gemeenten vol is."]},{"nl":"Om de taal sneller te leren, volgt mijn buurvrouw naast haar werk een inburgeringscursus.","en":"To learn the language faster, my neighbour is taking an integration course alongside her job.","hint":["taal","leren","volgen","buurvrouw","werk","inburgeringscursus"],"note":"An infinitive clause with om … te counts as one element too. After it comes the verb volgt, then the subject mijn buurvrouw.","alt":["Mijn buurvrouw volgt naast haar werk een inburgeringscursus om de taal sneller te leren.","Om de taal sneller te leren, volgt mijn buurvrouw een inburgeringscursus naast haar werk."]},{"nl":"Hij vertelde ons dat hij als kind met zijn ouders was geëmigreerd en hier naar school was gegaan.","en":"He told us that he had emigrated with his parents as a child and had gone to school here.","hint":["vertellen","kind","ouders","emigreren","school","gaan"],"note":"After en the dat-clause continues: verbs at the end, and hij is left out because it is the same subject. Emigreren and gaan both take zijn.","alt":["Hij vertelde ons dat hij als kind met zijn ouders geëmigreerd was en hier naar school was gegaan.","Hij vertelde ons dat hij als kind met zijn ouders was geëmigreerd en hier naar school gegaan was.","Hij vertelde ons dat hij als kind met zijn ouders geëmigreerd was en hier naar school gegaan was."]},{"nl":"Het gaat goed met het gezin omdat de kinderen hier naar school gaan en de ouders werk hebben gevonden.","en":"The family is doing well because the children go to school here and the parents have found work.","hint":["gezin","kind","school","ouders","werk","vinden"],"note":"After en a new subject begins, de ouders, so it must be said. The omdat-clause continues, so hebben gevonden stays at the end.","alt":["Het gaat goed met het gezin omdat de kinderen hier naar school gaan en de ouders werk gevonden hebben.","Met het gezin gaat het goed omdat de kinderen hier naar school gaan en de ouders werk hebben gevonden.","Met het gezin gaat het goed omdat de kinderen hier naar school gaan en de ouders werk gevonden hebben."]},{"nl":"Het plan dat door de gemeente werd voorgesteld, is afgewezen omdat er te weinig draagvlak voor was.","en":"The plan that was proposed by the municipality has been rejected because there was too little support for it.","hint":["plan","gemeente","voorstellen","afwijzen","draagvlak"],"note":"A passive relative clause inside the subject, then the main verb is, then an omdat-clause. One er does two jobs: 'there was' and, with voor, 'for it'.","alt":["Het plan dat door de gemeente voorgesteld werd, is afgewezen omdat er te weinig draagvlak voor was.","Het plan dat werd voorgesteld door de gemeente, is afgewezen omdat er te weinig draagvlak voor was."]},{"nl":"Discriminatie bij het zoeken naar werk is iets wat veel jongeren met een migratieachtergrond meemaken.","en":"Discrimination when looking for work is something that many young people with a migration background experience.","hint":["discriminatie","zoeken","werk","jongere","migratieachtergrond","meemaken"],"note":"After iets both wat and dat are correct. The relative clause sends meemaken to the end, and the separable verb stays in one piece there.","alt":["Discriminatie bij het zoeken naar werk is iets dat veel jongeren met een migratieachtergrond meemaken."]},{"nl":"Het aantal inwoners van buitenlandse afkomst is in de grote steden sterk gestegen.","en":"The number of inhabitants of foreign origin has risen sharply in the big cities.","hint":["aantal","inwoner","buitenlands","afkomst","stad","stijgen"],"note":"The subject is het aantal, singular, so is, even though inwoners stands closer to the verb. Stijgen is a change, so it takes zijn.","alt":["In de grote steden is het aantal inwoners van buitenlandse afkomst sterk gestegen."]},{"nl":"Hoewel de diversiteit in de samenleving toeneemt, zijn er nog veel mensen die vooroordelen hebben over nieuwkomers.","en":"Although diversity in society is increasing, there are still many people who have prejudices about newcomers.","hint":["hoewel","diversiteit","samenleving","toenemen","vooroordeel","nieuwkomer"],"note":"The hoewel-clause is the first element, so zijn comes next and then er. In the die-clause hebben goes to the end; over nieuwkomers may follow it.","alt":["Hoewel de diversiteit in de samenleving toeneemt, zijn er nog veel mensen die vooroordelen over nieuwkomers hebben.","Er zijn nog veel mensen die vooroordelen hebben over nieuwkomers, hoewel de diversiteit in de samenleving toeneemt.","Er zijn nog veel mensen die vooroordelen over nieuwkomers hebben, hoewel de diversiteit in de samenleving toeneemt."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Omdat de huren in de grote steden al jaren stijgen, ___ veel gezinnen naar kleinere plaatsen.","options":["verhuizen","zij verhuizen","verhuist"],"correct":0,"en":"Because rents in the big cities have been rising for years, many families are moving to smaller places.","note":"The omdat-clause is the first element, so the verb follows at once and the subject veel gezinnen comes after it. The subject is plural: verhuizen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik begrijp dat veel jongeren het dorp verlaten omdat ze geen werk vinden en ___.","options":["zien geen toekomst","geen toekomst zien","geen toekomst ziet"],"correct":1,"en":"I understand that many young people leave the village because they cannot find work and see no future.","note":"After en the omdat-clause continues, so zien stays at the end. The subject ze is the same, so it may be left out; the verb is plural."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Hij zei niets over zijn afkomst, ___ ik heel begrijpelijk vond.","options":["dat","die","wat","welke"],"correct":2,"en":"He said nothing about his origin, which I found very understandable.","note":"The relative pronoun refers to the whole clause before it, not to one noun: then it is wat (or, very formally, hetgeen). Dat would be a typical mistake here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het aantal asielaanvragen uit verschillende landen ___ dit jaar gedaald.","options":["zijn","hebben","is","heeft"],"correct":2,"en":"The number of asylum applications from various countries has fallen this year.","note":"The subject is het aantal, singular, not asielaanvragen or landen. Dalen is a change, so its auxiliary is zijn: is gedaald."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zij vertelde dat haar broer al op jonge leeftijd naar Canada ___ (emigreren).","answers":["was geëmigreerd","geëmigreerd was"],"en":"She said that her brother had emigrated to Canada at a young age.","note":"Emigreren is moving from one country to another, so it takes zijn. At the end of the dat-clause both orders of the two verbs are correct."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik weet niet of ___ genoeg woningen zijn voor alle nieuwkomers.","answers":["er"],"en":"I do not know whether there are enough homes for all the newcomers.","note":"With an indefinite subject (genoeg woningen) Dutch needs er, in a sub-clause just as in a main clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De opvang van asielzoekers in kleine gemeenten ___ (kosten) veel geld.","answers":["kost"],"en":"The reception of asylum seekers in small municipalities costs a lot of money.","note":"The subject is de opvang, singular. Asielzoekers and gemeenten stand closer to the verb, but they belong to the phrase after van and in."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De vormen van uitsluiting ___ in dit rapport worden beschreven, zijn niet nieuw.","answers":["die","welke"],"en":"The forms of exclusion that are described in this report are not new.","note":"The relative pronoun refers to vormen, a plural, so it is die (in old-fashioned formal writing also welke). The relative clause ends with worden beschreven; then zijn follows as the verb of the main clause."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik denk","words":["dat","de mensen","die hier al lang wonen,","de nieuwkomers","graag","verwelkomen"],"answers":["Ik denk dat de mensen die hier al lang wonen, de nieuwkomers graag verwelkomen.","Ik denk dat de mensen die hier al lang wonen, graag de nieuwkomers verwelkomen."],"en":"I think that the people who have lived here for a long time are glad to welcome the newcomers.","note":"The die-clause stands right after de mensen and is finished first. Then the dat-clause continues, and verwelkomen closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hoewel","words":["zij","pas twee jaar in Nederland","woont,","spreekt","ze","al vloeiend Nederlands"],"answers":["Hoewel zij pas twee jaar in Nederland woont, spreekt ze al vloeiend Nederlands.","Hoewel ze pas twee jaar in Nederland woont, spreekt zij al vloeiend Nederlands."],"en":"Although she has only lived in the Netherlands for two years, she already speaks fluent Dutch.","note":"In the hoewel-clause woont goes to the end. That clause is the first element, so spreekt follows straight after the comma, and then the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the three into one sentence: make the first a relative clause with die after Mijn buurman, and join the last with en.","from":"Mijn buurman is drie jaar geleden gevlucht. Hij werkt nu als verpleegkundige. Hij wil graag blijven.","answers":["Mijn buurman, die drie jaar geleden is gevlucht, werkt nu als verpleegkundige en wil graag blijven.","Mijn buurman, die drie jaar geleden gevlucht is, werkt nu als verpleegkundige en wil graag blijven.","Mijn buurman, die drie jaar geleden is gevlucht, werkt nu als verpleegkundige en hij wil graag blijven.","Mijn buurman, die drie jaar geleden gevlucht is, werkt nu als verpleegkundige en hij wil graag blijven."],"en":"My neighbour, who fled three years ago, now works as a nurse and would like to stay.","note":"In the die-clause the verbs go to the end. After it the main clause continues with werkt. After en, hij may be left out because the subject is the same."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Shorten it: replace the dat-clause by te + infinitive.","from":"Zij hoopt dat zij zich binnenkort in deze stad vestigt.","answers":["Zij hoopt zich binnenkort in deze stad te vestigen."],"en":"She hopes to settle in this city soon.","note":"This works because hoopt and vestigt have the same subject, zij. The reflexive zich stays, and te + infinitive closes the sentence."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Move the heavy part to the end: start with Het is.","from":"Dat de maatschappelijke ongelijkheid de laatste jaren is toegenomen, is in verschillende onderzoeken aangetoond.","answers":["Het is in verschillende onderzoeken aangetoond dat de maatschappelijke ongelijkheid de laatste jaren is toegenomen.","Het is in verschillende onderzoeken aangetoond dat de maatschappelijke ongelijkheid de laatste jaren toegenomen is."],"en":"It has been shown in several studies that social inequality has increased in recent years.","note":"A long dat-clause at the start makes the reader wait for the main verb. Het takes its place, and the dat-clause moves to the end, where it is easier to follow."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik denk dat de samenleving wordt steeds diverser.","answers":["Ik denk dat de samenleving steeds diverser wordt."],"en":"I think that society is becoming more and more diverse.","note":"In a dat-clause the verb goes to the end: steeds diverser wordt. Main-clause order after dat is the most common B2 error."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Na een lange periode in een asielzoekerscentrum het gezin kreeg eindelijk een eigen huis.","answers":["Na een lange periode in een asielzoekerscentrum kreeg het gezin eindelijk een eigen huis."],"en":"After a long period in an asylum seekers' centre the family finally got a home of its own.","note":"The long time phrase is the first element, however many words it has. The verb kreeg must come second, before the subject het gezin."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik weet dat mijn collega uit Polen komt en werkt al tien jaar in Nederland.","answers":["Ik weet dat mijn collega uit Polen komt en al tien jaar in Nederland werkt.","Ik weet dat mijn collega uit Polen komt en dat hij al tien jaar in Nederland werkt."],"en":"I know that my colleague comes from Poland and has been working in the Netherlands for ten years.","note":"After en the dat-clause goes on, with the subject left out, so werkt must go to the end. Main-clause order after en is only possible with a new main clause."}]}
];
